export const DEVELOPER_DOCS: Record<string, string> = {
  "ai": "# Building with AI agents\n\nThis page is written for the agent as much as for you. If you are an AI agent, Claude Code, Codex, ChatGPT, Cursor or any other, and you have been asked to build or change something on a Storelib site, read this page before writing anything. The rest of the hub is the full reference; this is how to use it and the standard your output is held to.\n\n## Where the reference is\n\n| URL | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| [storelib.com/llms.txt](/llms.txt) | A map of this hub in the llms.txt format: every page, one line each. Read this first. |\n| [storelib.com/llms-full.txt](/llms-full.txt) | Every page of the hub in one plain-text file, with every reference table expanded. Load this when you need the whole framework in context. |\n| [storelib.com/developers/ai](/developers/ai) | This page. |\n\nEverything in them is generated from the framework itself: the control types, filters, capabilities and limits come from the compiler that enforces them, so they cannot describe a version of Storelib that does not exist.\n\n## What you produce\n\nA Storelib section is **one file**, `sections/<type>.storelib`, with four blocks: `<template>`, `<style>`, an optional `<script>`, and `<schema>`. There is no other format. Read [Sections](/developers/sections) for the file and the template language, and [Schema](/developers/schema) for every setting type.\n\nWhen a person asks you for a section, answer with the file and nothing else around it, unless they asked for an explanation. They will paste it into the code editor, or you will write it into `sections/` in their theme.\n\n## Six things agents get wrong\n\nThese are the mistakes that produce a file which looks right and is not. Check each before you answer.\n\n1. **A section's script runs against a copy.** It runs in a sandbox, and `root` is a copy of the section's own markup: toggle classes, set styles and text, add listeners, and the page mirrors each change. It cannot add or remove elements, measure layout, or set `src`, `href` or `on*`. Where HTML does it alone (`<details>`, radio inputs and `:checked`, scroll snapping, the `popover` attribute), use HTML. See [JavaScript](/developers/javascript).\n2. **The template language is not Vue, React, JSX or Liquid.** `v-if`, `@click`, `:class`, `{% assign %}` and JSX braces are refused. It is `{{ }}`, `{% if %}`, `{% for %}`, `{# #}` and filters, and nothing else.\n3. **Every name must be declared.** `{{ settings.subtitle }}` does not compile unless the schema declares `subtitle`. Check every path your template and CSS read against your schema.\n4. **Repeated content is blocks.** Never `feature_1`, `feature_2`, `feature_3`.\n5. **Colour comes from the scheme.** Declare a `color_scheme` setting and read `var(--scheme-*)`. A hex code for anything the scheme answers stops responding to the creator's theme.\n6. **Container queries, not media queries.** The builder's phone preview is not the width of the window. A width-only media query is rewritten for you, but say what you mean.\n\n## Know the website before you write for it\n\nA section written without knowing the site is a section from another website: the wrong colours, an invented product, a headline for a business nobody runs. Before writing, find out:\n\n- **What it sells.** The published products, with their real titles and prices. Never invent a product, a price or a review. A connected agent reads them with `storelib_site`; the builder's AI is given them.\n- **What it is called and what it says about itself.** The brand name, tagline, site title and description. Write copy in the same voice and the site's language.\n- **How it looks.** The colour schemes and fonts. Use `var(--scheme-*)` and the theme's type styles, never a new palette.\n- **What is already on the page.** The sections each page has, so a new one does not repeat the one above it.\n\nThen read [Selling](/developers/selling) if the section shows a product, a price or a Buy button.\n\n## An email box collects emails\n\nA `<form>` with an `<input type=\"email\">` in a section sends every address to the site creator's Subscribers. The page does it, with no script. Mark it `data-storelib-form=\"subscribe\"`, and never write a script to submit a form or ask the creator to paste a mailing list URL to make it work. See [Forms](/developers/javascript#forms).\n\n## The loop your output goes through\n\nThis is what the builder's own AI does with every section it writes, and it is the loop you should run yourself when you can:\n\n@[ai-loop]\n\nWhen you cannot run the compiler, the person will: the code editor compiles on save and shows each error as `file:line message`. Fix the line it names. Do not rewrite the file.\n\n## Change content before source\n\nMost requests are not new sections. \"Make the heading bigger\", \"use the dark scheme\", \"add a fourth question\" are changes to a section's **settings** or **blocks**, which the creator can make in the builder without new code. Write or change a section file only when no existing setting can do what was asked. When you do change one, change the least that does it, and never rename or remove a setting `id`: nothing moves a stored value to a new id, so whatever the creator typed under the old one stops showing. See [Changing a section safely](/developers/schema#changing-a-section-safely).\n\n## What you may not do\n\n- Write any file but `sections/<type>.storelib`, or change one of the library's built-in sections. Both are refused; see [Rules before you ship](/developers/rules#1-what-you-may-change).\n- Invent an asset. Images are chosen by the creator through an `image_picker`; never put a URL to a picture into a default.\n- Declare a capability the section does not need.\n- Use `window`, `document`, `fetch`, `localStorage` or `eval`. They are refused when the file is saved; query inside `root` instead.\n- Add a contact form's sender to the mailing list. Only a box they ticked may do that.\n\n## The brief the builder's AI is given\n\nWhen a creator asks the builder's AI to write a new section, this is the brief it receives, word for word. It is assembled from the same modules as this hub, so what you are reading is what it follows. If you follow it too, your sections and the builder's will be indistinguishable.\n\n@[ai-brief]\n\nSome reference is added only when a request is about it. These topics are included when the words in a request call for them:\n\n@[ai-topics-extra]\n\n## Setting up your agent\n\n**Claude Code.** Add this to the `CLAUDE.md` at the root of your theme folder:\n\n```md\nThis is a Storelib theme. Sections are .storelib files written in the\nStorelib Builder Framework. Before writing or changing any file under\nsections/, read https://storelib.com/llms-full.txt and follow\nhttps://storelib.com/developers/ai. A section's script runs in a\nsandbox against a copy of its own markup: prefer HTML and CSS.\n```\n\n**Codex.** Put the same paragraph in `AGENTS.md` at the root of the theme folder.\n\n**Cursor.** Type `@Docs`, choose Add new doc, and give it `https://storelib.com/llms-full.txt`. Mention it with `@Docs` whenever you ask for a section, or add the paragraph above to a rule in `.cursor/rules`.\n\n**ChatGPT, Claude.ai and others.** Give it the link, or download [llms-full.txt](/llms-full.txt) and attach it, and ask for \"a Storelib section, as one .storelib file\".\n\n## Two ways to connect\n\n| | Through GitHub | Direct connection |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What it is | The site's sections live in a repository you own. | A token that lets an agent on your computer read and save the site's sections. |\n| How a change arrives | The agent pushes to the branch. Storelib compiles every changed section and saves it. | The agent calls `storelib_write_section`. |\n| Best for | Claude Code or Codex working in a repository, a team, a history of every change. | One person and one agent, nothing to set up on GitHub. |\n| What you see | The change in the builder within seconds, and a Storelib check on the commit. | The change in the builder within seconds. |\n\nBoth reach one site and nothing else, both save drafts that go live only when you publish, and both are under **Website Settings › Connections** in the builder, which the plug button in the top bar (**Connect Claude Code, Codex or ChatGPT**) opens. Pick your tool there and it shows only the steps for it.\n\n**ChatGPT** works through GitHub. Codex in ChatGPT edits a GitHub repository, so link one below, ask Codex for the change, and merge it into the linked branch: it reaches the site like any other push.\n\n## Connect through GitHub\n\n1. In the builder, press the plug button in the top bar, or open **Website Settings › Connections**.\n2. Press **Connect GitHub**. GitHub asks which repositories to give Storelib; pick one, or make an empty one first.\n3. Back in the builder, pick the repository, the branch (its default branch unless you say otherwise) and, if the site should live in a folder of a bigger repository, the folder. Press **Link and send sections**.\n\nStorelib writes one commit into the repository: every section file the site has, under `sections/`, and an `AGENTS.md` and a `CLAUDE.md` that tell an agent what the files are and how a push reaches the site. From then on:\n\n- **Push a change** to `sections/<type>.storelib` on that branch and it is compiled and saved to the site as a draft. An open builder shows it within a few seconds; a new section is placed on the page being edited.\n- **Every push gets a status** named Storelib on its commit: green with how many sections were saved, or red with the first error and its line. A file that does not compile is not saved, and nothing else in the push is held back by it.\n- **Only `sections/*.storelib` is read.** Everything else in the repository is yours and ignored. Deleting a section file on GitHub does not remove the section from the site, because a page may be using it.\n- **Send to GitHub**, in Connections, writes the site's current sections into the repository again, for instance after editing in the builder.\n- At most 30 section files are taken from one push. Storelib's own commits are recognised and not synced back.\n\nWith Claude Code, the whole loop is a conversation: open the repository, ask for the section, and let it commit and push.\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/you/your-site && cd your-site\nclaude \"Add a pricing section with three tiers to sections/, then commit and push\"\n```\n\n## Connect an agent directly\n\nAn agent can work on a site directly: read the project and the section the creator has selected, change settings and pages, write sections, run the checks and preview the drafts. The creator connects it once, from the builder.\n\n1. Open the site in the website builder, then **Website Settings › Connections**, and pick **Claude Code** or **Codex**.\n2. Name the connection, for example \"Claude Code\", and press **Connect**.\n3. Copy the token it shows. It is shown once. Storelib keeps only its hash, so a lost token is disconnected and replaced, never recovered.\n\n**Claude Code.** Run this in a terminal, with the token in place of `slb_...`:\n\n```bash\nclaude mcp add --transport http --scope user storelib https://mcp.storelib.com --header \"Authorization: Bearer slb_...\"\n```\n\n**Codex.** Add this to `~/.codex/config.toml`, and set the environment variable `STORELIB_TOKEN` to the token (`export STORELIB_TOKEN=\"slb_...\"` in the terminal you start Codex from):\n\n```toml\n[mcp_servers.storelib]\nurl = \"https://mcp.storelib.com\"\nbearer_token_env_var = \"STORELIB_TOKEN\"\n```\n\n**Anything else** that speaks MCP over HTTP takes the same address and header. An agent or script that does not speak MCP can use the same token with plain HTTP, below.\n\n### What arrives with the connection\n\nWhen Claude Code or Codex connects, the server's instructions put the [agent rules](/developers/rules) in the model's context: the workflow (inspect, plan, implement, test, repair) and the edges of what it may change. Nobody has to paste them. The same rules are the `storelib-rules` prompt, the `storelib://rules` resource and the `storelib_rules` tool, and Storelib AI in the builder works by them too.\n\nFor the task itself, `storelib_skills` with the task in the agent's own words returns only the skills it needs: the steps, the tools, the part of the framework involved, what goes wrong, and the checks that say it is done. The `storelib-task` prompt gives the rules and those skills together.\n\n### The tools\n\n| Tool | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `storelib_rules` | The agent rules, then [Rules before you ship](/developers/rules) in full. |\n| `storelib_skills` | The skills for a task: pass `task` in plain words, or `id` for one. |\n| `storelib_read_project` | The site brief, every page, the creator's own section types, and what is open in the builder. |\n| `storelib_read_page` | One page's sections in order, with ids, settings and blocks. Defaults to the page open in the builder. |\n| `storelib_read_selected_section` | The section selected in the builder, or one by id: every control it has, its values and blocks, and whether its file can be changed. |\n| `storelib_search_components` | The sections this site can use, built in and the creator's own, ranked for what you are looking for. |\n| `storelib_create_section` | Saves a new section file and, with `page`, places it there. Refused with what to fix unless it compiles and passes the quality checks. |\n| `storelib_edit_section` | Saves a change to a section file the creator has, with `base_updated_at`. The previous version is kept. |\n| `storelib_patch_section` | The same, as find-and-replace `edits` instead of the whole file. Each `find` is in the file exactly once. Checked and saved exactly as `storelib_edit_section`, for a fraction of the tokens. |\n| `storelib_update_styles` | Settings on a section, a block or the theme: colour scheme, button colours and hover, spacing, alignment, type. Checked against what the section declares. |\n| `storelib_create_page` | A new page with the site's header and footer and the sections listed. |\n| `storelib_create_safe_patch` | Several page operations as one change. All are checked first; if one is refused, nothing is written. A restore point is taken before it is applied. |\n| `storelib_run_tests` | Compile and quality for a file; section types, settings, contrast and limits for a page. |\n| `storelib_run_preview` | A link to the page as the drafts make it, the builder link, and the page checks. |\n| `storelib_guide`, `storelib_site`, `storelib_list_files`, `storelib_read_file`, `storelib_compile`, `storelib_write_section` | The framework, the site brief, and the section files, as before. |\n\nThe loop that works: read the project and the selected section, get the skills for the task, change settings where a setting does it and write a file only where it does not, patch the lines that change rather than resending a file, run the tests, and preview. When changing a file that exists, pass the `updated_at` you read as `base_updated_at`, and a save made elsewhere in the meantime is refused rather than overwritten.\n\nWhen a fact that changes the result is missing and no tool has it (which product to feature, which page, brand colours the site does not have, a business fact), ask the person you are working with one short question with a few options, and wait. Never ask what `storelib_site` or the page can tell you. `storelib_site` lists the published products with every field of the [product data](/developers/selling#products-reach-a-section-as-products), including each one's `checkout_url` and options.\n\n### What a connection can and cannot do\n\n- It reaches **one site**. Not the creator's other sites, orders, buyers, subscribers or account. It sees the site's published products only as a visitor does: title, prices, pictures, options, address and buy link. No argument to any tool names a site; the token decides it.\n- It writes **section files** as `sections/<type>.storelib`, lower case, digits, `_` and `-`, and **never changes a built-in section**: a different version is written as a new section. Paths with `../`, a leading `/` or another folder are refused.\n- It changes **pages and settings only through the page tools**, which refuse a setting a section does not declare or a value its control cannot take. Before every page or theme change a restore point of the whole site is taken, listed in Website Settings › Backups.\n- **Every change is a draft**. Nothing reaches visitors until the creator publishes. It cannot publish, and it cannot delete a page or a section file.\n- **The builder follows along.** A change to a page appears in a builder that is open, in place, without a reload; a new section file without a page appears on the page the creator has open. Page changes need `RUN_AGENT_MCP.sql` to have been run on the database.\n- `storelib_run_tests` runs checks over the site's rows. It runs no command and reaches nothing on the server.\n- The creator can disconnect it at any time; the token stops working on its next request.\n\n### Over plain HTTP\n\nEvery request carries `Authorization: Bearer slb_...`.\n\n| Request | Does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET /api/agent/v1/site` | What the website is, as `storelib_site` answers it. |\n| `GET /api/agent/v1/files` | Lists the theme's files. |\n| `GET /api/agent/v1/files?path=sections/x.storelib` | Reads one. |\n| `POST /api/agent/v1/compile` with `{ \"path\", \"source\" }` | Compiles without saving. `422` with `errors` when it does not compile. |\n| `PUT /api/agent/v1/files` with `{ \"path\", \"source\", \"base_updated_at\"? }` | Saves a section as a draft. |\n\n```bash\ncurl -X PUT https://storelib.com/api/agent/v1/files \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer slb_...\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"path\":\"sections/price_table.storelib\",\"source\":\"<template>...\"}'\n```\n\n## Without a connection\n\n- **One section.** Open the code editor in the website builder, create `sections/<type>.storelib`, paste the file and save. It compiles, appears in Add Section, and can be placed on any page.\n- **A whole theme.** Export the theme from your library as a `.storelib-theme.zip` file, unzip it, let the agent work on the folder, update `manifest.json` with the new hashes, zip the folder's contents and import it as a new draft website. The import checks every file and refuses the package with the reasons if anything is wrong, a stale hash included, so a broken edit never reaches a live site. [Editing a package by hand](/developers/sharing-themes#editing-a-package-by-hand) has the script and the zip command.\n",
  "blocks": "# Blocks\n\nA block is a part of a section the creator can add, remove, reorder, duplicate and hide: one question in an FAQ, one slide, one logo, one tier on a pricing table, one picture in a grid. The section says which blocks it takes and the builder does the rest.\n\nThere are two kinds, the same two Shopify has:\n\n| Kind | Where it is written | Who can hold it | Shopify's name |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Section blocks** | inside the section's own schema | that section only | section blocks |\n| **Theme blocks** | a file of its own, `blocks/<type>.storelib` | any section, or block, that accepts it | theme blocks |\n\nA section uses one kind or the other, never both. Section blocks are right for content that only makes sense in one place (an FAQ's questions). Theme blocks are right for the parts a page is built from (a heading, a picture, a button, a group of them), written once and used everywhere.\n\n**Repeated content is always blocks.** Never `question_1`, `question_2`, `question_3` as settings. Numbered settings cannot be reordered, cannot grow past the number somebody guessed, and leave an empty control in the panel for every one that is not used.\n\n## Section blocks\n\nIn the schema, `blocks` lists the types this section declares for itself. Each has a `name` and `settings`:\n\n```json\n\"blocks\": [\n  {\n    \"type\": \"question\",\n    \"name\": \"Question\",\n    \"limit\": 20,\n    \"settings\": [\n      { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"question\", \"label\": \"Question\", \"default\": \"What is your return policy?\" },\n      { \"type\": \"richtext\", \"id\": \"answer\", \"label\": \"Answer\", \"default\": \"<p>Thirty days, no questions asked.</p>\" }\n    ]\n  },\n  { \"type\": \"divider\", \"name\": \"Divider\", \"limit\": 3, \"settings\": [] }\n],\n\"max_blocks\": 30\n```\n\n| Key | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `type` | Unique within the section and stable for ever, like a setting id. |\n| `name` | What the builder calls it. |\n| `settings` | The block's own settings, declared exactly like a section's. See [Schema](/developers/schema). |\n| `limit` | The most of this type one section may hold. |\n\nDraw them with a loop. `blocks` is the instance's blocks in order, each `{ id, type, settings }`, with the block type's defaults applied:\n\n```storelib\n{% for block in blocks %}\n  {% if block.type == \"question\" %}\n    <details class=\"faq__item\" name=\"{{ section.id }}-faq\" data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\">\n      <summary>{{ block.settings.question }}</summary>\n      <div class=\"faq__answer\">{{ block.settings.answer | raw }}</div>\n    </details>\n  {% elsif block.type == \"divider\" %}\n    <hr data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\">\n  {% endif %}\n{% else %}\n  {% if page.in_editor %}<p class=\"faq__empty\">Add a question</p>{% endif %}\n{% endfor %}\n```\n\n- **`data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\"` on each block's root** is what lets the creator click a block in the canvas to select it. Leave it out and the block can only be reached through the panel.\n- Read a block's settings as `block.settings.<id>`. The id must be declared on that block type, or the template does not compile.\n- The `{% else %}` branch of a `for` runs when there are no blocks. Keep an empty-state prompt behind `page.in_editor`, so it helps the creator and never reaches a visitor.\n- Section blocks hold no blocks of their own. Blocks inside blocks are theme blocks.\n\n## Theme blocks\n\nA theme block is a `.storelib` file in `blocks/`: a template, a style and a schema, like a section, and no script. The file name is the type, so `blocks/heading.storelib` is the block `heading`.\n\n@[library-block:heading]\n\nIts schema:\n\n| Key | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `name` | What Add block and the sidebar call it. Required. |\n| `settings` | Its settings, declared exactly like a section's. A `visible_if` here reads `block.settings.<id>`. |\n| `blocks` | The theme blocks it may hold: `[{ \"type\": \"@theme\" }]` for any, or types by name. Absent, it holds none. |\n| `max_blocks` | The most blocks it may hold. |\n| `presets` | What Add block offers for it, each a state it arrives in. See [Presets](#presets). |\n| `tag` | The element wrapped round it: `div` (the default), `section`, `article`, `aside`, `figure`, `li`, `ul`, `ol`, `span`, `blockquote` or `p`. `null` for none. |\n| `class` | Classes on that element, beside `sl-block`. |\n| `capabilities` | `[\"embed\"]` for a block that shows a YouTube or Vimeo player through the `embed` filter. Nothing else; a script belongs to the section. |\n\n**A private block** is one whose file name starts with `_`, such as `blocks/_slide.storelib`. `@theme` does not offer it; only a section or block that names it (`{ \"type\": \"_slide\" }`) can hold it. That is how a slideshow keeps its slide out of every other Add block menu.\n\nA block has no `<script>`. Its section owns the sandbox, and the section's script finds its blocks with `root.querySelectorAll('[data-block-type=\"heading\"]')`.\n\nThese ship with every theme, and are what `@theme` offers on a new site:\n\n@[theme-blocks]\n\n## Accepting theme blocks\n\nA section accepts theme blocks by listing them in `blocks` with a type and nothing else:\n\n```json\n\"blocks\": [{ \"type\": \"@theme\" }]\n```\n\n`@theme` is every public theme block in the theme, including the ones a creator or an agent adds later. To take only some, name them:\n\n```json\n\"blocks\": [{ \"type\": \"image\" }, { \"type\": \"_slide\" }]\n```\n\nA section that writes `{ \"type\": \"@app\" }` is refused: Storelib has no app blocks.\n\n## Rendering theme blocks: content_for\n\n```storelib\n{% content_for \"blocks\" %}\n```\n\ndraws every theme block this section holds, in the creator's order, each by its own file. Each is wrapped in its element:\n\n```html\n<div class=\"sl-block\" data-block-id=\"b-k3f9\" data-block-type=\"heading\">\n  <h2 class=\"sl-heading\">…</h2>\n</div>\n```\n\n`data-block-id` is what makes a click on the canvas select that block, at any depth. Style the wrapper from the parent: a row of blocks is `.row > .sl-block { flex: 1 1 0 }`.\n\nWith `\"tag\": null` nothing is wrapped round the block, and its template puts the attributes on its own outer element instead. The compiler refuses a `\"tag\": null` block that does not:\n\n```storelib\n<li class=\"slide\" {{ block.storelib_attributes }}>…</li>\n```\n\n`content_for \"blocks\"` in a section whose blocks are its own is a compile error, and so is one in a section or block that accepts no blocks.\n\n## Blocks inside blocks\n\nA theme block that accepts blocks draws them with the same tag, in its own template. The Group block that ships is all of it:\n\n@[library-block:group]\n\nInside a block's template:\n\n| Path | Is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `block.settings.<id>`, or `settings.<id>` | this block's settings, checked against its schema when the file compiles |\n| `block.id`, `block.type` | this block |\n| `block.blocks` | the blocks it holds, in order, each `{ id, type, settings }` |\n| `block.storelib_attributes` | `data-block-id` and `data-block-type`, for `\"tag\": null` |\n| `section.settings.<id>` | the settings of whatever section it sits in, not checked, since that can be any section |\n| `theme`, `scheme`, `page`, `shop`, `routes`, `customer`, `product` | the same as in the section |\n\nBlocks nest **eight levels deep** at most, and a section holds **50 blocks** counting every level. The sidebar shows the tree: a block that holds blocks lists them in its open row, each with its own Add block.\n\n## Static blocks\n\nA static block is one the template places itself, at a fixed spot, instead of in the list:\n\n```storelib\n<div class=\"head\">{% content_for \"block\", type: \"heading\", id: \"title\" %}</div>\n<div class=\"grid\">{% content_for \"blocks\" %}</div>\n```\n\n`type` and `id` are written in quotes, not worked out: the id is how the block is found again. The creator edits a static block like any other, and can hide it, but cannot move, duplicate or delete it, because the template decides where it is. Until it is edited it is drawn with its file's defaults; a preset can give it settings with `\"static\": true` and the same id. One template places each id once.\n\n@[library-section:image_grid]\n\n## Presets\n\nA **preset** is a state a section, or a theme block, arrives in. It is written in the file, so changing what Add section adds is changing a few lines of JSON, and every preset is a card in Add section (or an entry in Add block) the moment the file saves.\n\nWrite blocks the way Shopify does, keyed by id, with their order beside them. This is the form that nests, and the one static blocks need:\n\n```json\n\"presets\": [\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Image with text\",\n    \"category\": \"media\",\n    \"settings\": { \"max_width\": \"wide\" },\n    \"blocks\": {\n      \"row\": {\n        \"type\": \"group\",\n        \"settings\": { \"direction\": \"row\", \"gap\": 40 },\n        \"blocks\": {\n          \"image\": { \"type\": \"image\", \"settings\": { \"ratio\": \"portrait\" } },\n          \"words\": {\n            \"type\": \"group\",\n            \"blocks\": {\n              \"heading\": { \"type\": \"heading\", \"settings\": { \"text\": \"Made by hand\" } },\n              \"button\": { \"type\": \"button\" }\n            },\n            \"block_order\": [\"heading\", \"button\"]\n          }\n        },\n        \"block_order\": [\"image\", \"words\"]\n      }\n    },\n    \"block_order\": [\"row\"]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nThe older list form still works, and may nest too:\n\n```json\n\"presets\": [\n  { \"name\": \"FAQ\", \"blocks\": [{ \"type\": \"question\" }, { \"type\": \"question\" }, { \"type\": \"question\" }] }\n]\n```\n\n| Key | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `name` | The card's title in Add section, or the entry in Add block. |\n| `category` | Where Add section files this card. Absent, the section's own `category`. |\n| `settings` | The section's (or block's) settings as it arrives. Each must be one it declares. |\n| `blocks` | Its blocks: keyed by id with `block_order`, or a list. Each may have `settings`, `blocks` and `block_order` of its own. |\n| `block_order` | The order of the keyed blocks. Every block is in it, except static ones. |\n| `static` | `true` for a block the template places with `content_for \"block\"`. Its key is that id. |\n\nWhat is refused, with the line: a block in `blocks` that is not in `block_order` and not static (it would never be drawn), an id in `block_order` with no block, a static block in `block_order`, a static block without an id, a type the owner does not accept, a setting it does not declare, and a tree deeper than eight or larger than 50.\n\nWhen a preset is added, every block in it gets a new id, so adding the same preset twice gives two sets of blocks, not one shared. A static block keeps its key, because that is the id its template places it under.\n\n**A theme block's presets** are what Add block offers for it: one entry per preset, named by the preset. The Group block's three presets (\"Group\", \"Two columns\", \"Image with text\") are three entries, each arriving with its blocks in it. A block with one preset, or none, is one entry under its own name.\n\n**A section with no presets is not offered in Add Section** at all. An instance already on a page keeps working, and one can still arrive by import or from an agent's page tools.\n\n## Limits\n\nWhere each limit is held:\n\n| Where | `limit` per type | `max_blocks` | 50 per section, 8 deep |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| The builder's Add and Duplicate | yes | yes | yes |\n| Ask AI and a connected agent's page tools | no | yes | yes |\n| Saving a page, from anywhere | no | no | yes |\n| Importing a theme | no | no | yes |\n\n@[limits]\n\n## How blocks are stored\n\nIn the page, as an ordered list on the section instance. A block that holds blocks carries them the same way; a static block is marked, and a hidden one too:\n\n```json\n\"blocks\": [\n  { \"id\": \"title\", \"type\": \"heading\", \"static\": true, \"settings\": { \"text\": \"From the studio\" } },\n  { \"id\": \"b-8f2a\", \"type\": \"group\", \"settings\": { \"direction\": \"row\" }, \"blocks\": [\n    { \"id\": \"b-c31d\", \"type\": \"image\", \"settings\": {} },\n    { \"id\": \"b-77e0\", \"type\": \"text\", \"settings\": {}, \"hidden\": true }\n  ] }\n]\n```\n\n- A block id is made when the block is created and never changes. Reordering changes the order of the list only; duplicating a block, or a section, gives the copy new ids all the way down. Treat an id as opaque.\n- A block whose type nobody declares any more, or whose file was deleted, is **kept** with its settings and everything inside it. The editor shows where it was; a visitor sees nothing. Updating a section or deleting a block file never deletes content.\n- A hidden block is kept and not drawn. It comes back with the eye in the sidebar.\n- An exported theme writes blocks in the keyed form at every level, `\"blocks\": { \"b-8f2a\": { … } }` with `\"block_order\"`, the same form presets use, and import converts it back.\n\n## Styling blocks\n\nA theme block's `<style>` is scoped to `[data-block-type=\"<type>\"]`, so it reaches every block of that type and nothing else. When a rule reads a setting (`{{ block.settings.gap }}`), the stylesheet is drawn once per block instead, scoped to that block in that section.\n\nBlocks sit inside a section and take its colours: read the section's `--s-*` properties (`--s-heading`, `--s-para`, `--s-btn`, `--s-border`, `--s-radius`…) with a fallback, so the same block is right on a light scheme and a dark one. Give classes a prefix of the block's own (`sl-heading`, `card-block__title`): a section's rules reach into the blocks inside it.\n\n## Products are shaped like blocks\n\n`products` holds the creator's published products in the same `{ id, type, settings }` shape, so a card written for `blocks` works for `products` with nothing changed but the loop:\n\n```storelib\n{% for block in products %}\n  <a class=\"card\" href=\"{{ block.settings.url | url }}\">\n    {% if block.settings.image != \"\" %}<img src=\"{{ block.settings.image }}\" alt=\"{{ block.settings.title }}\">{% endif %}\n    <span class=\"card__title\">{{ block.settings.title }}</span>\n    <span class=\"card__price\">{{ block.settings.price }}</span>\n  </a>\n{% endfor %}\n```\n\n`price` arrives formatted, such as `$48.00`. Templates do not do money.\n\n## From Shopify\n\n| Shopify | Storelib |\n| --- | --- |\n| `blocks/heading.liquid` | `blocks/heading.storelib` |\n| `\"blocks\": [{ \"type\": \"@theme\" }]` | the same |\n| `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` | `{% content_for \"blocks\" %}` |\n| `{% content_for 'block', type: 'heading', id: 'title' %}` | `{% content_for \"block\", type: \"heading\", id: \"title\" %}` |\n| `{{ block.shopify_attributes }}` | `{{ block.storelib_attributes }}`, needed only with `\"tag\": null` |\n| `blocks/_slide.liquid`, private | `blocks/_slide.storelib`, private |\n| presets with `blocks` and `block_order` | the same, `static` included |\n| `{% javascript %}` in a block | the section's `<script>` |\n| `@app` blocks | none |\n",
  "builder": "# How the builder works\n\nA Storelib website is a **theme**: a set of source files that say how things look and behave, and a set of pages that say what is on them. The website builder is an editor over those two things, and the storefront is a renderer over them. This page is the map; the rest of the hub is the detail.\n\n## Source and content\n\nEverything in a theme is one of two kinds, and the split is the whole design.\n\n| Kind | What it is | Where it lives | Who changes it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Source** | Section files: markup, CSS, schema, optional script. One file per section type. | `sections/<type>.storelib` | A developer, the code editor, an AI agent |\n| **Content** | Pages: which sections they hold, in what order, with what settings and blocks. Never markup. | A row per page; `templates/<page>.json` in a package | The creator, in the builder |\n\nChange a section file and every page using that section changes how it looks; nobody's content moves. Change a page and one page's content changes; no section changes how it works. There is no file that does both, which is why a section can be rewritten without losing a single word a creator typed.\n\n## A theme as files\n\nAn exported theme is a folder of files in a zip, and the code editor shows a website's files in a similar layout:\n\n```text\nmy-shop/\n├── theme.json              name, version, the engine it needs\n├── manifest.json           the SHA-256 of every file, checked on import\n├── config/\n│   └── settings.json       theme-wide settings: colour schemes, fonts, radius\n├── templates/\n│   ├── home.json           the home page: its sections, in order\n│   ├── product.json        the template every product page is drawn with\n│   └── about.json          a page of its own, at /about\n├── sections/\n│   ├── hero_centered.storelib   one file per section type\n│   └── features_grid.storelib\n└── assets/\n    └── 3f2a…c9.webp        media, named by the SHA-256 of its bytes\n```\n\nIn the database the same website is rows: one for the settings, one per page, one per section file you saved. [Themes](/developers/themes) has the package format and [Data model](/developers/data-model) the rows.\n\n- **A section's type is its file name**, `sections/<type>.storelib`, in lowercase letters, digits, `-` and `_`. That name is what a page stores. Keep the schema's `type` the same: Ask AI and the agent tools `storelib_create_section` and `storelib_edit_section` refuse a file where the two differ, and everywhere else the file name wins.\n- **Paths are relative to the theme root.** A path may not begin with `/`, contain `..` or a backslash.\n- **Only section files draw anything.** The site reads `sections/*.storelib` from a theme's files. The other files the code editor shows are views of the theme's data, and editing one changes nothing on the site.\n\n## The hierarchy\n\n```text\nTheme\n ├─ Theme settings       colour schemes, fonts, text styles, spacing, radius\n └─ Pages\n     └─ Sections          an instance of a section type, with its own settings\n         └─ Blocks        repeatable children: a question, a slide, a logo\n```\n\nA **section instance**, as the builder stores it:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"faq_accordion-1727200000000\",\n  \"type\": \"faq_accordion\",\n  \"name\": \"Accordion FAQ\",\n  \"settings\": { \"heading\": \"Questions\" },\n  \"blocks\": [\n    { \"id\": \"block-m1x2-1-ab3cd\", \"type\": \"qa\", \"settings\": { \"question\": \"Do you ship abroad?\" } },\n    { \"id\": \"block-m1x2-2-q9t4w\", \"type\": \"qa\", \"settings\": { \"question\": \"Returns?\" } }\n  ],\n  \"hidden\": false\n}\n```\n\n- Ids are made once and never change. Reordering moves an instance; duplicating gives the copy new ids.\n- `settings` holds what the section's preset set and what the creator has set since. Everything else comes from the schema's defaults when the section is drawn, so a later change to a default reaches every instance that does not store that setting.\n- A setting the schema no longer declares is kept, not deleted. Updating a section never destroys content.\n\n## Draft and published\n\nEvery page, the theme settings and every section file you saved have a draft and a published copy. The builder writes drafts as you work; **Publish** copies every draft over its published copy in one step. Visitors only ever see published values, and the builder shows drafts, so nothing you try in the editor reaches a visitor until you choose. [Publishing and checks](/developers/publishing) has every step.\n\n## What happens when you save a section file\n\n1. **Parse.** The file is split into its four blocks, each with the line it starts on.\n2. **Validate the schema.** Every setting type, id, default and option is checked against the control registry.\n3. **Compile the template.** The markup becomes a tree. Every name the template reads is checked against the schema beside it, so `{{ settings.headng }}` is an error with a line number, not a blank space on the page.\n4. **Scope the CSS.** Every selector is rewritten to apply to this section's instances only.\n5. **Check the script.** Forbidden names and undeclared capabilities are refused.\n\nIf any step fails, the file is not saved, and each error is shown with its file and line. A file that saves is a draft, and reaches visitors when the website is published. See [Sections](/developers/sections#errors) for the error format.\n\n## What happens when a visitor opens a page\n\nThe storefront reads the published page, resolves each section's settings (saved value, then schema default, then the control's own fallback), renders the compiled template to HTML on the server, and sends that HTML with each section's scoped CSS. A visitor's browser receives finished markup and never downloads the template engine.\n\nA hidden section is not rendered. A section whose file does not compile is left out of the published page rather than breaking it, and shown in the builder as a card naming the file and the line.\n\n## Two kinds of section\n\nThe library is written in the framework, and so is anything you or an AI write. A few long-standing sections, such as the header and footer, are still drawn by the platform's own React components. Both kinds are placed, reordered, hidden and edited in exactly the same way, and both read the same colour schemes and text styles, so a creator cannot tell them apart. Everything you write is the first kind.\n\n## What keeps a site safe\n\nA theme can be exported to a single file and imported into another account, which makes it something creators hand each other. So the framework treats every section as code somebody else might have written.\n\n- **Markup is escaped.** `{{ }}` always HTML-escapes. The only way to print markup is the `raw` filter, which is for `richtext` settings; rich text is cleaned against an allow list on save, on import, on publish and on every read. See [Output](/developers/sections#output) for which settings that covers.\n- **CSS cannot leave its section.** Scoping is done by the compiler, not by convention.\n- **No inline script.** `<script>` in a template, `on*=` attributes and `javascript:` URLs are refused at compile time. What a section draws is read once more as it is drawn, so one that only appears once values are filled in, such as a handler split around a `{{ }}` or a `javascript:` address typed into a setting, is taken out there.\n- **Frames are allow-listed.** An `<iframe>` needs the `embed` capability and takes its address from the `embed` filter, which only answers for known video and audio players.\n- **Scripts cannot reach the page.** `window`, `document`, `fetch` and the rest are refused by name. See [JavaScript](/developers/javascript).\n\n## Moving a theme\n\n**Export** writes the theme's settings, its pages as last published, its section files and its media into one `.storelib-theme.zip` file, with a manifest listing the SHA-256 of every file. **Import** reads it as a whole or not at all: every path is checked, every hash is recomputed, every section is compiled, and every page is checked against the sections it uses. A package that fails any check is refused with the list of reasons, and nothing is written. Anything creator-written is cleaned on the way in. [Sharing themes](/developers/sharing-themes) has what travels and what does not.\n\nLimits for a package:\n\n@[limits]\n",
  "changelog": "# Changelog\n\nChanges to the Storelib Builder Framework: the section format, the template language, the schema, the controls and the rules the compiler enforces. Changes to the builder's interface that do not change what a section can do are not listed.\n\n## Versions\n\nFive things carry a version, and they move independently so that one changing does not force the others.\n\n@[versions]\n\nA file written for a newer version is **refused, not misread**: a schema declaring `\"schema_version\": 2` against an engine that reads 1 is rejected with both numbers named. A file written for an older version is always read, and keys the engine does not recognise survive every read and write.\n\nEvery change since 1.0 has been additive. Nothing a section could do on 11 September 2026 has stopped working since, which is why every version above is still 1.\n\n## 3 October 2026\n\n**Added**\n\n- [Theme blocks](/developers/blocks#theme-blocks), the way Shopify has them: a file each in `blocks/`, accepted by a section with `{ \"type\": \"@theme\" }` or by name, drawn with `{% content_for \"blocks\" %}`, private when the file name starts with `_`. Heading, Text, Button, Image, Video, Spacer and Group ship with every theme.\n- Blocks inside blocks, eight levels deep: a theme block that accepts blocks draws them with the same tag. Group makes rows and columns out of any blocks.\n- [Static blocks](/developers/blocks#static-blocks), placed by the template with `{% content_for \"block\", type: \"…\", id: \"…\" %}`: edited and hidden, never moved or deleted.\n- [Presets](/developers/blocks#presets) in Shopify's keyed form, `blocks` by id with `block_order`, nested, with `static` and a `category` that files the card in Add section. A theme block's presets are its entries in Add block.\n- Two sections built from theme blocks: Section, which takes any of them, and Image grid, which takes pictures and places its title as a static block.\n- A block can be hidden from the sidebar's eye, and is kept.\n- Agents may write theme blocks: `blocks/<type>.storelib` is writable over MCP, the agent API and GitHub, except the ones Storelib ships. Page tools take `parent_block_id` to add inside a block, and a block's path (`outer/inner`) as its id.\n\n- A `color` setting's default may name a scheme colour, `\"default\": \"scheme.accent\"`. The setting follows the section's scheme until the creator picks a colour, and a section's own colour settings are now rows in its panel. See [Styling](/developers/styling#colours-of-the-sections-own).\n- [The header and footer](/developers/header-and-footer): what the one header and one footer do, and every setting they take, generated from the files the builder draws them with.\n- A `video_picker` uploads a video file or takes a link to one, and shows it.\n- Website settings › Navigation: the header's links, the footer's menu columns and every menu, in one place.\n\n**Changed**\n\n- A section file that is a header, a navigation bar or a footer is refused when it is saved, by the code editor, Ask AI, an agent or a GitHub push, and left out of an imported theme. Recognised by `\"group\"`, by Shopify's `enabled_on.groups`, by its name, or by an outermost `<header>` holding a `<nav>`. A site has one of each, and they are changed through their settings.\n- The YouTube and Vimeo video section's link is a `video_url` setting, which checks the link as it is pasted.\n- A section's 50 blocks are counted at every depth, and saving a page keeps the blocks inside blocks, whether they are hidden and whether the template placed them.\n- Adding the same preset twice gives each section its own block ids. Before, the two shared them, and a click on a block in either selected the first.\n\n## 25 September 2026\n\n**Added**\n\n- Eight setting types: `inline_richtext`, `html`, `color_background`, `datetime`, `focal_point`, `video_url`, `product_list` and `link_list`. See [Schema](/developers/schema#every-control-type).\n- `visible_if` on a setting, written as Shopify writes it, so the panel shows a setting only when it matters. See [Showing a setting only when it matters](/developers/schema#showing-a-setting-only-when-it-matters).\n- Shop data in a setting: `{{ shop.name }}`, `{{ creator.avatar }}`, `{{ product.price }}` and the rest of a closed list, filled in on the server. See [Shop data in a setting](/developers/schema#shop-data-in-a-setting).\n- `shop`, `creator` and `product` in a template, beside `settings` and `blocks`.\n- A `product_picker` carries the product's title, price and picture as well as its address.\n- Menus, made in Theme settings, for a `link_list`. `main-menu` is the header's own links, and the header can show any menu.\n- The `lines` filter, for a `textarea` typed one item per line.\n- Three sections: Plans, Rows and Course Outline.\n- The 404 page: every site draws its own inside its header and footer for an address it does not have, and a creator can arrange it in the builder like the shop and search pages.\n- The Product Grid can show products the creator chooses from the shop.\n- [Coming from Shopify](/developers/shopify).\n- [Themes](/developers/themes): what a theme holds, the website library, every theme setting key, colour schemes and typography, and the package format with `theme.json`, `manifest.json` and `templates/*.json`.\n- [Pages and page settings](/developers/pages), [Sharing themes](/developers/sharing-themes), [Publishing and checks](/developers/publishing), [Dynamic values](/developers/dynamic-values) and [Data model](/developers/data-model).\n- The hub is grouped the way it is used: Get started, Themes, Sections, Data and logic, Tools and AI, and Reference. Every page keeps its address.\n- Four objects every section can read: `shop`, `routes`, `customer` and `wishlist`. See [What a template can read](/developers/sections#what-a-template-can-read).\n- Contact forms: `data-storelib-form=\"contact\"` sends a message to the creator, keeps it in Emails, and adds the sender to Subscribers only when they tick the box. See [Contact forms](/developers/javascript#contact-forms).\n- Saving products: `data-storelib-wishlist` on a button saves a product to the visitor's Wishlist. See [Saving products](/developers/javascript#saving-products).\n- Every website has an account page, a sign in page, a Wishlist and a contact page, drawn by the `main_account`, `main_login`, `main_wishlist` and `contact` sections.\n\n**Changed**\n\n- Every saved value is held to its type as a section is drawn. A value the type does not allow is drawn as the default and left as it was in storage.\n- The countdown's exact time is a `datetime`, written out when no end is typed.\n- The header's phone menu can slide in from either side or cover the screen, and its see-through setting can apply to the home page only.\n- The announcement bar can hold several messages that take turns.\n- `migrations` are applied: a section's own setting stored under `from` is read under `to` as the section is drawn.\n- The theme export downloads media from public addresses only, checked at every redirect.\n\n**Security**\n\n- A creator's own sections are cleaned by the schema their file declares, not only by the built-in ones, when a page is saved, read in the builder and published.\n- The builder's routes no longer return database error text.\n\n\n**Fixed in these pages**\n\n- `allowed_templates` is described as it ships: validated and kept, and not used yet.\n- An unknown filter is not refused by the compiler. It prints the value unchanged.\n- There is no `layout/theme.storelib`. A theme has no stylesheet for the whole site that you can write.\n- An export names its page files by page key, `templates/home.json` and `templates/about.json`, and takes the theme settings from the draft and the pages as last published.\n- Media settings hold public URLs on a site. `asset://` references exist only inside a theme package.\n- The importer holds blocks to the ceiling of 50. `limit` and `max_blocks` are held by the builder's buttons, and `max_blocks` by the agent page tools.\n- A page picker and a product picker store the address they link to.\n- A section's script receives the settings stored on the instance, without schema defaults.\n- A product's `tag` is always empty today.\n- A page stores its preset's values and everything the creator sets, not only what differs from the defaults.\n\n## 24 September 2026\n\n**Added**\n\n- [Rules before you ship](/developers/rules): what every agent follows before writing code on a Storelib site, and the `storelib_rules` tool that returns them. `storelib_guide` now starts with them.\n- A navigation bar example, with menu links as blocks and a phone menu that opens with `popover`. Replaced on 3 October 2026 by [Page links](/developers/examples#page-links), because a site's navigation is its built-in header.\n\n**Changed**\n\n- An agent can no longer change a built-in section. A write to one over MCP, the agent API or a GitHub push is refused, and `storelib_list_files` marks each file `writable`. A different version is written as a new section.\n- The builder's AI saves a section it rewrites as a draft with a version, like the code editor, instead of writing it straight to the live site.\n- Connections has its own place in Website Settings, under Code, instead of inside Advanced. Links to `?settings=advanced` still open it.\n- In the builder, `position: fixed`, `100vh` and `100vw` answer to the canvas, width-only media queries become container queries, and a `popover` or command-opened `<dialog>` opens inside the canvas. See [The page a section sees](/developers/styling#the-page-a-section-sees).\n- A `<style>`, `<link>`, `<base>` or `<meta>` tag inside `<template>` is refused on save and removed from sections saved before.\n\n## 23 September 2026\n\n**Added**\n\n- Section scripts run. A section's `<script>` runs on the published page and in the builder, inside a sandbox with an opaque origin, against a copy of the section's own markup. Changes to classes, allowed attributes, styles and text are mirrored to the page; clicks, keys and input are sent back. See [How the sandbox works](/developers/javascript#how-the-sandbox-works).\n- `storelib.scrollTo(element, options)`, `storelib.inEditor`, and `storelib.navigate(path)` for a section that declares `navigation`.\n- [Selling](/developers/selling): the fields `products` gives a section, the checkout link `/checkout?productId={{ block.id }}&src=website`, the cart, what checkout does with the money, and what a section may never do with payments.\n- `storelib_site`, and `GET /api/agent/v1/site`: what a connected website is, its brand, fonts, colour schemes, the sections on each page and its published products, so an agent writes for the site it is connected to.\n- Agent connections. A creator connects Claude Code, Codex or any MCP client to one site from Website Settings › Advanced (first shipped as Website settings › Connections). It can list, read, compile and save that site's section files, as drafts, over MCP at `/api/mcp` or HTTP at `/api/agent/v1`. See [Connect an agent directly](/developers/ai#connect-an-agent-directly).\n- GitHub. A site can be linked to a repository. Its section files and an `AGENTS.md` are written into it, and every push to the linked branch that changes `sections/*.storelib` is compiled and saved to the site as a draft, with a Storelib status on the commit. See [Connect through GitHub](/developers/ai#connect-through-github).\n- [Using the website builder](/developers/website-builder): every panel, control and theme setting, drawn from the lists the builder reads.\n- The `timers` capability grants working `setTimeout`, `setInterval` and `requestAnimationFrame`.\n- Forms. A `<form>` in a section is submitted by the page, with no script. `data-storelib-form=\"subscribe\"`, or any form with an email box and no action of its own, sends each address to the creator's Subscribers; `data-storelib-form=\"native\"` leaves a form to the browser. The form carries `data-state` while it sends, and shows an element marked `data-storelib-success` when it is done. See [Forms](/developers/javascript#forms).\n- The library's Newsletter Signup section collects sign-ups with nothing set up. It previously showed no form until an outside mailing list address was pasted in.\n- The Storelib Developer Hub, [llms.txt](/llms.txt) and [llms-full.txt](/llms-full.txt), built from the same modules as the builder's AI.\n\n**Changed**\n\n- The compiler's messages for `@click`, `v-on:` and `on*=` name the section's `<script>` block, beside the HTML that needs no script.\n\n**Security**\n\n- Creator HTML in `richtext` settings is cleaned on import and publish as well as on save and read.\n- Sign-up endpoints are rate limited per visitor and per address.\n\n## 20 September 2026\n\n**Fixed**\n\n- A link is no longer styled as a button by theme rules that assumed every `<a>` in a section was one.\n\n## 19 September 2026\n\n**Added**\n\n- Shop and search pages exist on every published site from the day it is published, built from the home page's header and footer, and can be opened and edited in the builder like any other page.\n\n## 13 September 2026\n\n**Added**\n\n- A theme export carries its videos and files as well as its images, each by content hash, or the export fails rather than producing a theme with holes in it.\n\n## 12 September 2026\n\n**Changed**\n\n- A section's type is its file name. A schema whose `type` disagrees with the file it is in is refused, naming both.\n- Secondary buttons take the scheme's own secondary button colours.\n\n**Fixed**\n\n- Every section in the library was audited against its schema. Controls that changed nothing were removed rather than left in the panel.\n\n## 11 September 2026\n\n**Added**\n\n- **Storelib Builder Framework 1.0.** The `.storelib` section format with `<template>`, `<style>`, `<script>` and `<schema>` blocks; the template language; the control registry; the compiler, with every error carrying its file and line; CSS scoping; and the script checker with capabilities.\n- The section library: thirty sections in ten categories, every one a `.storelib` file.\n- Blocks, presets, `max_blocks` and per-type `limit`.\n- Responsive settings, resolved as `{ desktop, tablet, mobile }`.\n- Colour schemes and text styles as theme tokens, with per-section overrides.\n- `migrations`, a list of setting renames a section can declare. It is validated and kept; nothing applies it yet (25 September 2026).\n- Themes export to and import from a single `.storelib-theme` file, checked as a whole, with every file hashed.\n- An accessibility baseline: on every built-in colour scheme, body text meets a contrast of 4.5 to 1 against its background and headings meet 3 to 1.\n",
  "data-model": "# Data model\n\nThe tables a Storelib website is stored in, as the builder, the live site and a connected agent see them. You never write to these directly: the builder, the code editor, the agent tools and an import are the ways in, and each checks what it writes. Knowing the shape explains why a change lands where it does. The database's row level security keeps each website's rows to the creator who owns it.\n\nIn every table below, `install_id` is the website a row belongs to.\n\n## store_theme_installs\n\nOne row per website in the library.\n\n| Column | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `id` | The website's id. The builder's address is `/website-builder/editor/<id>`. |\n| `creator_id` | Whose website it is. |\n| `theme_id` | The catalogue theme it was made from, a row in `themes`. |\n| `name` | Its name in the library. Only the creator sees it. |\n| `status` | `draft`, `published` or `unpublished`, which is a website that was live and was taken offline. One website per creator is `published` at a time, and the database refuses a second. |\n| `is_custom` | Set on a website made by Duplicate or Import. |\n| `parent_install_id` | The website a duplicate was made from. |\n| `sort_order` | Its place in the library. |\n| `published_at` | When it last went live. |\n| `preview_token` | The website's [preview link](/developers/sharing-themes#preview-links) token, or empty when there is none. |\n| `snapshot_url`, `snapshot_updated_at` | The cover picture on its library card, and when it was taken. |\n\n## store_theme_settings\n\nOne row per website: the theme settings.\n\n| Column | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `settings` | The live theme settings, as one JSON object. |\n| `draft_settings` | The draft, or empty when nothing has changed since the last publish. |\n\nThe keys are in [Themes](/developers/themes#theme-settings).\n\n## store_theme_pages\n\nOne row per page of a website, unique by `install_id` and `page_key`.\n\n| Column | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `page_key` | `home`, `product`, `about`, `contact`, `products`, `search`, or a key the creator made. See [Pages](/developers/pages). |\n| `sections` | The live page: a list of section instances with their settings and blocks. |\n| `draft_sections` | The draft, or empty when the page has not changed since the last publish. |\n\nA page with nothing in `sections` is not on the live site. The shape of a section instance is in [Pages and page settings](/developers/pages#what-a-page-stores).\n\n## store_theme_custom_files\n\nOne row per file the creator saved, unique by `install_id` and `path`. The theme's own 33 section files ship with Storelib and have a row only once someone saves one.\n\n| Column | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `path` | Where the file is in the theme, such as `sections/pricing_table.storelib`. |\n| `content` | The live file. Empty for a file that has been created and never published, which the live site skips, so the theme's own file of that name is still drawn. |\n| `draft_content` | The saved, unpublished version, or empty. |\n| `updated_at` | When it last changed. A save that says when it read the file is refused as a conflict if this has changed since. |\n\nThe live site reads only the rows whose path ends in `.storelib`, draws a section from `sections/<type>.storelib` and a theme block from `blocks/<type>.storelib`. Other paths are stored and read by nothing.\n\n## store_theme_file_versions\n\nA row for every save of a file, every publish of it and every restore, so the code editor's **History** can put any of them back.\n\n| Column | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `path` | The file. |\n| `content` | The whole file as it was. |\n| `kind` | `save`, `publish` or `restore`. |\n| `created_at` | When. |\n\nThe newest 50 of each file are kept.\n\n## store_theme_backups\n\nThe website's restore points, shown in Website settings › Backups & Versions.\n\n| Column | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `label` | What it is: Published, Manual backup, Storelib AI finished a task, `Before restoring \"…\"`, or before a connected agent's change. |\n| `source` | `publish`, `manual`, `ai` or `restore`. |\n| `snapshot` | The theme settings and every page, drafts where there are drafts. Section files are not in it. |\n| `fingerprint` | The SHA-256 of the snapshot, so the same state is not stored twice in a row. |\n\nThe newest 30 of each website are kept. See [Publishing and checks](/developers/publishing#backups).\n\n## theme_publish_history\n\nA row every time a website is published or taken offline: which one, by whom, when, and for a publish, the theme settings it went live with.\n\n## themes\n\nThe catalogue: every theme Storelib ships, with its name, description, author, version and tags. It is the same for every account. Each website points at one through `theme_id`.\n\n## Connections\n\nThree tables hold what an agent or a repository is connected to. See [Building with AI agents](/developers/ai).\n\n| Table | One row per | What it holds |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `theme_agent_tokens` | Connection | Its name, the website it reaches, when it was last used and when it was disconnected. The token itself is never stored: only its SHA-256 and its first few characters, to recognise it in the list. |\n| `theme_agent_activity` | Change an agent made | Which agent, which file, and whether it created or updated it. An open builder reads this to show the change without a reload. |\n| `theme_github_links` | Linked website | The repository, the branch, the folder, and the last commit it synced. |\n\n`theme_editor_focus` holds the page, section and block open in the builder right now, which is how a connected agent knows what \"the selected section\" is.\n\n## What is not here\n\nA website's products, orders, customers and subscribers are the creator's account, not the website's, and live elsewhere. A theme's settings and pages never hold a price or a buyer: a section reads the creator's published products through `products` when it is drawn. See [Selling](/developers/selling).\n",
  "dynamic-values": "# Dynamic values\n\nA section's template prints values with `{{ }}` and decides what to draw with `{% if %}` and `{% for %}`. This page is every value a template can read, what each one holds and where it comes from, how the language compares and loops, and every filter. The syntax itself is in [Sections](/developers/sections#the-template-language).\n\n## What a template can read\n\n| Name | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `settings` | This instance's settings, one per setting the schema declares, each already worked out. |\n| `blocks` | This instance's blocks, in order, each `{ id, type, settings }`. |\n| `section` | This instance: `section.id`, `section.type`, and `section.settings` and `section.blocks`, which are the two above under longer names. |\n| `page` | Which page this is and where it is drawn: `page.template`, `page.in_editor`, and on the search page `page.query`. |\n| `scheme` | The colour scheme this instance uses, with its own colour overrides applied. |\n| `theme` | The theme's tokens and every theme setting. |\n| `products` | The creator's published products, shaped like blocks. |\n| `shop` | The store: its name, tagline, logo, social links and, where a section asks for it, its support email; with `shop.description` and `shop.product_count` on a published page. |\n| `routes` | The addresses every website answers on, such as the cart, the account and the contact page. |\n| `customer` | The signed-in visitor and their orders, on the account page. `null` everywhere else. |\n| `wishlist` | The visitor's saved products, on the account and Wishlist pages. `null` everywhere else. |\n| `creator` | The creator's public profile: `creator.name`, `creator.headline`, `creator.bio`, `creator.avatar`. |\n| `product` | On a product page, the product it shows: `title`, `price`, `compare_price`, `description` (words), `description_html` (cleaned markup, printed with `raw`), `image`, `images`, `url`, `includes`, `id`. Empty everywhere else, so `{% if product %}` tells a section where it is. |\n| `forloop` | The loop being drawn: `index`, `index0`, `first`, `last`, `length`. |\n\nNothing else can be read. The compiler checks every path against this list and against the schema beside it, so `{{ shop.nmae }}` and `{{ settings.headng }}` are both errors with a line number, not an empty space on the page. A loop variable, `block` in `{% for block in blocks %}`, is checked against the block settings the schema declares.\n\n## settings\n\nOne value for every setting that holds one. `header`, `paragraph` and `divider` draw in the panel and hold nothing.\n\nEach value is worked out the same way every time the section is drawn:\n\n```text\nvalue = the value stored on this instance\n     ?? the schema's default\n     ?? the control's own value for \"nothing\" (\"\" for text, 0 for a number, false for a toggle, [] for a gallery)\n```\n\nA page stores what the section's preset set and what the creator changed since. Everything else comes from the schema, so a default changed in the file reaches every instance that does not store that setting. The control's own values are in the table in [Schema](/developers/schema#every-control-type).\n\nWhat each control gives a template:\n\n| Control | The value |\n| --- | --- |\n| `text`, `textarea`, `url` | Text. A `url` holds whatever the link picker chose: `/about`, `/products/preset-pack`, `https://…`, `mailto:…` or `tel:…`. |\n| `richtext` | HTML, cleaned when it was saved. Print it with `raw`. |\n| `number`, `range` | A number. |\n| `toggle`, `checkbox` | `true` or `false`. |\n| `select`, `radio` | The chosen option's `value`, never its label. |\n| `color` | A hex colour, or `\"\"`. |\n| `color_scheme` | The scheme's key, such as `dark`. The colours themselves are in `scheme`. |\n| `font_picker` | A font family name. |\n| `alignment` | `left`, `center` or `right`. |\n| `image_picker`, `video_picker`, `file_picker` | The file's URL, or `\"\"` when nothing is chosen. |\n| `image_gallery` | A list of URLs. |\n| `icon_picker` | An icon's name, such as `zap`. |\n| `page_picker` | The page's address, such as `/about`. |\n| `product_picker` | Prints as the product's address, such as `/products/preset-pack`, and carries the product: `.title`, `.price`, `.compare_price`, `.image`, `.url`, `.tag`. A product that is not this creator's and published is an address with no fields. |\n| `product_list` | The chosen products, in order, shaped like `products`. |\n| `link_list` | A menu: `{ handle, title, links }`, each link `{ title, url, links }`. |\n| `inline_richtext`, `html` | Cleaned HTML. Print it with `raw`. |\n| `color_background` | A colour or a CSS gradient, checked piece by piece. |\n| `datetime` | `2026-03-14` or `2026-03-14T23:00`, as written. Print it with the `date` filter. |\n| `focal_point` | Two percentages, such as `30% 70%`, for `object-position`. |\n| `video_url` | A YouTube or Vimeo link. Print it through `embed`. |\n| `community_picker` | Prints as the community page's address, such as `/community/morning-runners`, and carries the community: `.name`, `.tagline`, `.image`, `.logo`, `.members`, `.members_label`, `.posts`, `.events`, `.price`, `.join_label`, `.url`. A community that is not this creator's, published and public, or any community while communities are off, is empty. |\n\nA stored value the control does not allow is read as the default, as if it had never been set: a word in a `range`, an option a `select` does not list, a link to `javascript:`. The stored value is kept. A setting may also hold a placeholder such as `{{ shop.name }}`, filled in before the section is drawn: see [Shop data in a setting](/developers/schema#shop-data-in-a-setting).\n\n### Responsive values\n\nA setting with `\"responsive\": true` on `number`, `range` or `alignment` arrives as an object with all three breakpoints, filled from the top down:\n\n```json\nstored:  { \"columns\": { \"desktop\": 4, \"mobile\": 1 } }\nread:    { \"columns\": { \"desktop\": 4, \"tablet\": 4, \"mobile\": 1 } }\n```\n\nRead one breakpoint with `settings.columns.desktop`. Printing the whole object prints it as JSON text, which is never what a stylesheet wants. Hand all three to CSS as custom properties and choose with container queries:\n\n```storelib\n<div class=\"grid\" style=\"--cols: {{ settings.columns.desktop }}; --cols-tablet: {{ settings.columns.tablet }}; --cols-phone: {{ settings.columns.mobile }}\">\n```\n\n```css\n.grid { container-type: inline-size; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols-phone), minmax(0, 1fr)); }\n@container (min-width: 640px) { .grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols-tablet), minmax(0, 1fr)); } }\n@container (min-width: 1024px) { .grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols), minmax(0, 1fr)); } }\n```\n\n## blocks\n\nThe instance's blocks in order. Each is `{ id, type, settings }`, and each block's settings are worked out like a section's, against its block type's defaults.\n\n```storelib\n{% for block in blocks %}\n  {% if block.type == \"qa\" %}\n    <details data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\"><summary>{{ block.settings.question }}</summary>{{ block.settings.answer | raw }}</details>\n  {% endif %}\n{% else %}\n  {% if page.in_editor %}<p>Add a question in the sidebar</p>{% endif %}\n{% endfor %}\n```\n\n- `blocks.size`, `blocks.first`, `blocks.last` and `blocks[0]` work on the list.\n- A block whose type the schema no longer declares is still in the list, with its stored settings as they are. Test `block.type` and it is drawn by nothing.\n- A block marked hidden in a theme package is left out. The builder has no way to hide a block.\n- `data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\"` on each block's outermost element is what lets the creator click the block in the builder to open it.\n\n## section\n\n| Path | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `section.id` | This instance's id, the same for as long as the instance exists. Build element ids from it, `id=\"{{ section.id }}-menu\"`, so the section can be on a page twice. |\n| `section.type` | The section type, which is its file name. |\n| `section.settings` | The same as `settings`. |\n| `section.blocks` | The same as `blocks`. |\n\n## page\n\n| Path | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `page.template` | Which page this is. `index` for home and `page.<key>` for the others, with the differences below. |\n| `page.in_editor` | `true` while the builder draws the page, and absent on the live site. |\n| `page.query` | On the live search page, what was searched for, up to 120 characters. Absent everywhere else. |\n| `page.product` | On a product page, the product it is about: the fields of a product in `products` and its description. Empty everywhere else. See [Selling](/developers/selling#the-product-page-pageproduct). |\n\n`page.template` on each page:\n\n| Page | On the live site | In the builder |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Home | `index` | `index` |\n| A product page | `product` | `page.product` |\n| The shop | `page.products` | `page.products` |\n| Search results | `page.search` | `page.search` |\n| The 404 page | `page.404` | `page.404` |\n| The account, sign in and Wishlist pages | `page.account`, `page.login`, `page.wishlist` | the same |\n| Any other page | `page.<key>` | `page.<key>` |\n\n`page.in_editor` is the one sanctioned difference between the builder and the live site. Put anything meant for the person editing behind it, and a visitor never sees it:\n\n```storelib\n{% if blocks.size == 0 and page.in_editor %}\n  <p class=\"empty\">Add a logo in the sidebar</p>\n{% endif %}\n```\n\n## scheme\n\nThe colour scheme this instance uses: the one its `color_scheme` setting names, with the colours it overrides in the builder's Advanced colors applied on top. See [Themes](/developers/themes#colour-schemes) for how a scheme is chosen when the key is missing.\n\nIt has the scheme's 21 colours under their names, such as `scheme.background`, `scheme.heading` and `scheme.primary_button_background`. The full list, with the CSS variable for each, is in [Styling](/developers/styling#colour-schemes). The library's own sections read a scheme by shorter, older names, and both work:\n\n@[scheme-names]\n\nIn CSS, read the same colours as custom properties, which the renderer sets on the section's root: `var(--scheme-background)`, `var(--scheme-heading)`. A template needs `scheme` only where a colour goes into markup.\n\n## theme\n\nThe theme's tokens, which a section reads directly, and every theme setting as it was saved:\n\n@[theme-tokens]\n\n`theme.colors` are the page's own colours, behind and between sections, not a section's. Paint a section from `scheme`, which follows the colour scheme the creator chose for it.\n\n`theme.settings` has everything else: `theme.settings.brand_name`, `theme.settings.logo_url`, `theme.settings.tagline`, `theme.settings.social_instagram`. A key a website has never set may be absent, so give it a fallback:\n\n```storelib\n<a class=\"brand\" href=\"/\">{{ theme.settings.brand_name | default: \"Shop\" }}</a>\n```\n\n## products\n\nThe creator's published products, newest first, in the same shape as `blocks`, so one card is written once for both:\n\n| Path | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `block.id` | The product's id. [Checkout](/developers/selling#two-ways-to-sell-from-a-section) takes it. |\n| `block.type` | Always `product`. |\n| `block.settings.title` | Its name. |\n| `block.settings.price` | Its price, formatted, such as `$48.00`. Empty when it is free. |\n| `block.settings.compare_price` | The price before a sale, formatted, only when it is higher than `price`. Otherwise empty. |\n| `block.settings.image` | Its main picture's URL, or empty. |\n| `block.settings.url` | Its page, `/products/<slug>`. |\n| `block.settings.tag` | Meant to read `Sold out` when none are left. Today it is always empty, because stock is not read yet. |\n\nIt has more than these: every picture, the options, the buy link and whether it can be bought. The full list is in [Selling](/developers/selling#products-reach-a-section-as-products).\n\nWhich products a page has:\n\n| Where | `products` |\n| --- | --- |\n| Home, the shop and your own pages, live | The newest 24 published products. A change to a product reaches the site within about a minute. |\n| Search results, live | Those of the 24 that match `page.query`. |\n| A product page, live | The newest 24 published products. The product the page is about is `page.product`. |\n| The builder | Up to 100 of the newest published products, on every page. |\n\n```storelib\n{% for block in products %}\n  <a class=\"card\" href=\"{{ block.settings.url | url }}\">{{ block.settings.title }} <span>{{ block.settings.price | default: \"Free\" }}</span></a>\n{% else %}\n  {% if page.in_editor %}<p>Publish a product and it appears here</p>{% endif %}\n{% endfor %}\n```\n\nA template never does money: print `price` as it arrives. See [Selling](/developers/selling).\n\n## shop\n\nThe store the website belongs to, from what the creator has already set, so a section never asks for it twice:\n\n| Path | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `shop.name` | The brand name from the theme settings, else the creator's name. |\n| `shop.tagline` | The tagline from the theme settings, or empty. |\n| `shop.logo` | The logo's URL, or empty. |\n| `shop.url` | `/`, the website's home. |\n| `shop.socials` | The theme's social links that are set, each `{ key, label, url }`, such as `{ key: \"instagram\", label: \"Instagram\", url: \"https://…\" }`. Only `https`, `http` and path links are kept. |\n| `shop.email` | The support email from Settings, on a page where a section has a `show_email` setting turned on. Empty everywhere else, so an address is never printed by a section that did not ask for it. |\n\n```storelib\n<h2>{{ settings.heading | default: shop.name }}</h2>\n{% for link in shop.socials %}<a href=\"{{ link.url | url }}\">{{ link.label }}</a>{% endfor %}\n```\n\n## routes\n\nThe addresses every Storelib website answers on. Link to them by name rather than typing the path:\n\n| Path | Where it goes |\n| --- | --- |\n| `routes.root_url` | `/` |\n| `routes.products_url` | `/products`, every product |\n| `routes.search_url` | `/search` |\n| `routes.cart_url` | `/cart` |\n| `routes.contact_url` | `/contact` |\n| `routes.wishlist_url` | `/wishlist` |\n| `routes.account_url` | `/account`, which sends a visitor who is not signed in to the sign in page |\n| `routes.account_login_url` | `/account/login`, the sign in page: an email, then the code sent to it. A new email makes the account there, so there is no separate sign up. Add `?return=/a/path` to land somewhere else afterwards. |\n| `routes.account_signin_url` | The sign in page too. |\n| `routes.account_register_url` | The sign in page too: a new email makes the account. |\n| `routes.account_code_url` | Where the sign in page's email form posts `email` (and `return`). Sends the code. |\n| `routes.account_verify_url` | Where the code form posts `code`. Signs the visitor in. |\n| `routes.account_profile_url` | Where the account page's form posts `name`. |\n| `routes.account_logout_url` | `/account/logout`. Post a form to it: `<form method=\"post\" action=\"{{ routes.account_logout_url }}\" data-storelib-form=\"native\">` |\n\nPost these forms with `data-storelib-form=\"native\"`, so the browser sends them and the page follows where the answer leads.\n\n## customer\n\nThe customer who is signed in, on the account page. A customer account belongs to your website alone, the way a Shopify store's does: it is made the first time somebody signs in with an email, and it is not a Storelib login. `null` on every other page, and on the account page for a visitor who is not signed in, so test it before reading it:\n\n| Path | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `customer.name` | The name they gave on their profile, or empty. |\n| `customer.first_name` | The first word of it. |\n| `customer.email` | The email they signed in with. |\n| `customer.orders_count` | How many orders they have placed with you. |\n| `customer.orders` | Those orders, most recent first, one per payment, each `{ number, date, status, total, items, item_count, action_label, action_url }`. `items` is a list of `{ title, image, url, variant }`. `status` is in words (`Complete`, `Confirmed`, `Shipped`, `Delivered`, `Partly refunded`, `Refunded`, `On hold`). `action_url` opens the order's receipt on Storelib, which is also where its files are, and `action_label` says what it does: `Download`, `Access` or `View order`. |\n| `customer.sample` | `true` in the builder only, where there is no customer: the name and email are the words \"Customer name\" and \"Customer email\", and the orders are your own products. Style them as placeholders. |\n\n```storelib\n{% if customer %}\n  <p>{{ customer.orders_count }} orders</p>\n{% else %}\n  <a href=\"{{ routes.account_login_url }}\">Sign in</a>\n{% endif %}\n```\n\n## wishlist\n\nThe products the visitor saved, on the account page and the Wishlist page. `null` on every other page: a cached page cannot know who is looking. A section anywhere can still draw a heart, and the page fills it in; see [Saving products](/developers/javascript#saving-products).\n\n| Path | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `wishlist.items` | The saved products, most recently saved first, each `{ id, title, price, compare_price, image, url, tag }`, the same fields as a product's `settings`. |\n| `wishlist.count` | How many. |\n| `wishlist.signed_in` | `true` when the list is the visitor's account's and follows them to any device, `false` when it is kept in this browser. |\n\n## forloop\n\nInside `{% for %}`, the innermost loop:\n\n| Path | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `forloop.index` | 1 for the first item. |\n| `forloop.index0` | 0 for the first item. |\n| `forloop.first` | `true` on the first item. |\n| `forloop.last` | `true` on the last item. |\n| `forloop.length` | How many items the loop has. |\n\nOutside a loop, `forloop` is empty.\n\n## How values compare\n\n- **Printing.** `{{ }}` escapes what it prints. `null`, `false` and a missing value print nothing. A list or an object prints as JSON text.\n- **Truth.** An empty string, `null`, `false` and an empty list are false. Everything else is true, and that includes `0` and `\"0\"`.\n- **Equality.** `==` and `!=` compare as text, so `1 == \"1\"` is true. `nil`, `null` and `blank` are the same empty value, and an empty text setting is `\"\"`, which is not equal to it: test `settings.x != \"\"`, or just `settings.x`.\n- **Order.** `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=` compare as numbers.\n- **`contains`** looks for a piece of text in text, or an item in a list.\n- **`and`, `or`, `not`** and parentheses combine conditions. `not` takes everything after it, so `not settings.a == \"x\"` is `not (settings.a == \"x\")`.\n- **Paths** are dots and literal indexes: `blocks[0]`, `settings[\"key\"]`. There are no computed lookups such as `items[forloop.index0]`. A list has `.size`, `.length`, `.first` and `.last`; text has `.size` and `.length`.\n- **Loops.** `{% for x in list %}` runs its `{% else %}` when the list is empty or is not a list.\n\n## Filters\n\n`{{ value | filter: argument, argument }}`, chained left to right: `{{ settings.heading | strip | truncate: 60 }}`.\n\n@[filters]\n\n## Values in CSS\n\nA section's `<style>` can print values with `{{ }}` too, and reads the same names. A value going into CSS loses any `<`, `>`, `{`, `}` and `;` and any `@import`, so it cannot end the rule it is in. Where a value differs per instance, prefer a custom property set in the markup and a static rule that reads it: the stylesheet stays the same for every instance. See [Styling](/developers/styling#settings-in-css).\n\nA setting read only in `<style>` still counts as unread for the compiler's warning about settings a template never reads. The warning does not stop a save.\n\n## In a script\n\nA section's `<script>` receives `settings`, `blocks`, `theme` and `page` too, but not worked out the way a template's are: see [JavaScript](/developers/javascript#the-scripting-api).\n\n## What the language does not have\n\nIt is not Liquid, although it looks like it. These are not in the language, and an unknown tag is a compile error:\n\n- `assign`, `capture`, `include`, `render`, `unless`, `case`, `break`, `continue` and a `raw` tag,\n- whitespace control, `{%-` and `-%}`,\n- ranges such as `(1..3)`, and arithmetic with `+` or `-`: use the `plus` and `minus` filters,\n- filters for money. Prices arrive formatted. A date is a `datetime` setting, printed with the `date` filter.\n\nAn unknown **filter** is not refused: it prints the value unchanged. Check a filter's name against the table above.\n",
  "examples": "# Examples\n\nEight complete sections. Each is a real `.storelib` file that the test suite compiles, and runs through the same quality checks the builder applies to AI-written sections, on every change to the platform. If one of these stopped compiling, the change that broke it would not ship. Copy any of them into the code editor as `sections/<type>.storelib` and it works as shown.\n\nEvery example follows the same conventions, which are the library's own:\n\n- The root reads the colour scheme with `data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\"`, and every colour comes from a `--scheme-*` variable.\n- Spacing is two `range` settings, passed to CSS as custom properties on the root.\n- `container-type: inline-size` on the root, a phone layout as the base, and `@container` for wider.\n- Ids are built from `{{ section.id }}`, so each section can appear on a page twice.\n- Anything that moves stops under `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n- Empty states sit behind `page.in_editor`.\n\n## Sign-up card\n\nA sign-up box that sends every address to the creator's Subscribers with no script and no setup. Shows the explicit `data-storelib-form=\"subscribe\"`, a success message the creator can edit, a visually hidden label, and a form that stacks on a phone and sits in one row wider. See [Forms](/developers/javascript#forms).\n\n@[example:signup_card]\n\n## FAQ, one open at a time\n\nBlocks, a `richtext` answer printed with `raw`, and `<details>` elements sharing a `name` so opening one closes the others, all without script.\n\n@[example:faq_exclusive]\n\n## Logo strip\n\nImage blocks that fall back to a text name, a row that swipes on a phone with scroll snapping, and wraps into a centred row once there is room.\n\n@[example:logo_strip]\n\n## Video with text\n\nThe `embed` capability and the `embed` filter: a creator pastes whatever link they have, and the frame only ever points at a known player. See [Embeds](/developers/javascript#embeds).\n\n@[example:video_feature]\n\n## Product cards\n\nThe creator's own published products through `products`, in the same `{ id, type, settings }` shape as blocks. Prices arrive formatted, and a sale shows the old price struck through.\n\n@[example:product_cards]\n\n## Product page\n\nThe product a product page is about, through `page.product`: a gallery that swipes on a phone, the price and the sale price, options chosen with radio inputs and `:checked` with a buy button for the chosen one, and the sold out state. Every buy button is the product's own `checkout_url`, and checkout brings the buyer back to the site. See [Selling](/developers/selling).\n\n@[example:product_page]\n\n## Tabs\n\nBlocks as tabs, switched with radio inputs and `:checked`, keyboard reachable, with the first tab open. The same pattern works for any \"one of these at a time\" layout.\n\n@[example:tabs_panel]\n\n## Page links\n\nA row of links inside a page, to a shop's topics or a course's parts: each link a block with a label and a `url`, chips on a computer, and on a phone a button that opens them as a sheet with `popover`, with no script. It is a band on a page, not the site's navigation: the site's header and its menu are built in, and changed through their settings. See [The header and footer](/developers/header-and-footer).\n\n@[example:page_links]\n",
  "header-and-footer": "# The header and footer\n\nEvery Storelib website has exactly **one header and one footer**. They are the built-in sections of type `header` and `footer`, the same on every page, and they are the parts of a shop that a section file cannot rebuild on its own: the cart drawer and its count, the search, the account and wishlist links for a signed-in visitor, the phone menu with its sub-menus, sticky behaviour, the logo from Theme settings and the menus from Website settings.\n\nSo they are **customised, never replaced**. This page lists everything they do and every setting that changes them, for a person in the editor and for an AI writing code.\n\n> **Warning** Do not write a section file that is a header, a navigation bar or a footer, and do not put one in a page. It is refused when it is saved, by a person or an agent, and left out of an imported theme. It would be a second header under the real one, with a cart icon that opens nothing and a menu that is a typed copy of the real one. Change the real header's settings instead.\n\n## Where they live\n\nIn the Sections panel a page is three groups, the way a shop's theme editor shows it:\n\n| Group | Holds | Add section |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Header | The site's header, and nothing else. | Under it: adds at the top of the page. |\n| Template | The page's own sections, in order. | At the end: adds at the bottom of the page. Between two rows: adds there. |\n| Footer | The site's footer, and nothing else. | None. |\n\nThe header and footer can be hidden, which hides them on every page, but not dragged, copied or deleted. A section can never be moved above the header or below the footer, by a drag, by Ask AI or by an agent. Every page stores a copy of both, and the live site draws the home page's on every page, so they cannot drift apart.\n\n## What the header does\n\n- **Logo or store name.** The logo image, its height, a separate logo for phones, and a nudge left or right. With no logo it prints the store name from Theme settings › Branding.\n- **Links.** Its own links (its `nav_link` blocks, each with a label, an address and up to one level of sub-links), or one of the site's menus. Both are edited in Website settings › Navigation, and the links in the header's own panel too.\n- **Layouts.** Five on a computer and two on a phone. On a phone the menu slides in from the left or the right, or covers the screen, with each sub-menu pushed in over the top level.\n- **Sticky.** Normal (scrolls away), always on top, or on top only while scrolling up. Optionally transparent over the top of the page, on every page or on the home page only.\n- **Icons.** Search (opens a search box in the header, or goes to `/search`), account, wishlist and cart. The cart opens the cart drawer with the visitor's basket and its count, and checkout from there.\n- **A button**, with its own words and link, painted from the scheme's primary button.\n- **Its colours** come from its colour scheme, with Advanced colors for this section alone, and the drop-down menus are painted by the same scheme.\n\nEvery setting, read from the file the builder draws it with:\n\n@[chrome-settings:header]\n\n## What the footer does\n\nThe logo, tagline and copyright line; two menu columns chosen from the site's menus; its own link columns (`footer_column` and `footer_link` blocks); the policy pages and Contact, linked automatically; a newsletter sign up that collects for the creator; social icons; payment icons; a layout and a colour scheme.\n\n@[chrome-settings:footer]\n\n## Changing them\n\n| You are | Do this |\n| --- | --- |\n| In the editor | Sections › Header (or Footer). Links and menus are also in Website settings › Navigation. |\n| Ask AI in the builder | Ask in words: \"make the header sticky\", \"add a Courses link with three sub-links\". It reads the header and changes its settings and `nav_link` blocks. |\n| An agent over MCP | `storelib_create_safe_patch` with `update_section` on the section of type `header` or `footer`, and `add_block`, `update_block`, `move_block` or `remove_block` for the header's `nav_link` links (a sub-link names its parent in `parent_id`). `storelib_update_styles` with scope `section` for its colours. |\n| Editing an exported theme | Change the `header` and `footer` entries in `templates/*.json`: their `settings` and `blocks`. Do not add a `sections/*.storelib` file for either. |\n\nA `nav_link` block:\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"nav-courses\", \"type\": \"nav_link\", \"settings\": { \"label\": \"Courses\", \"url\": \"/products\", \"link_type\": \"custom\" } }\n```\n\nand a sub-link under it:\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"nav-courses-1\", \"type\": \"nav_link\", \"settings\": { \"label\": \"Beginner\", \"url\": \"/products/beginner\", \"link_type\": \"custom\", \"parent_id\": \"nav-courses\" } }\n```\n\n## How a file is recognised as a header or footer\n\nThe rule is in `lib/storelib-theme/chrome.ts` and runs at every door a file comes through: the code editor's save, Ask AI, the MCP and agent tools, a GitHub push and a theme import. A file is a header or footer when any of these holds:\n\n1. Its schema says so: `\"group\": \"header\"` or `\"footer\"`, or Shopify's `\"enabled_on\": { \"groups\": [\"header\"] }`.\n2. Its type or name is one: Header, Site header, Navbar, Navigation bar, Top bar, Footer, with a word like Main or Custom in front or a version after. A heading band called \"Page header\" or \"Collection header\" is not.\n3. Its markup is one: the outermost element is a `<header>` holding a `<nav>`, a `<nav>`, or a `<footer>`, and its name says header, nav, menu or footer.\n\nSaved, it is refused with what to change instead. In an imported theme it is left out, its uses are taken off the pages, and the import says so; the site keeps its own header and footer.\n\n## The rest of a shop\n\nThe pages every website has from the start, and where each is:\n\n@[default-pages]\n\nThe cart drawer, the cart page, checkout, the account and sign in pages and the wishlist are the platform's, so they work the same on every site and every theme. A section reaches them through links (`/products`, `/cart`, `/account`, `/wishlist`, `/search`) and through the product data in [Dynamic values](/developers/dynamic-values), never by building its own.\n",
  "": "# Storelib Developer Hub\n\nStorelib websites are built from sections, and every section is written in one language: the **Storelib Builder Framework**. This hub is its complete reference, a guide to the website builder that runs it, and everything around it: themes and how they are shared, the values a section reads, publishing, and connecting an AI agent to a site.\n\nThe same language is read by everyone who builds here. A creator in the code editor, the builder's own AI, and whatever you bring with you, whether that is Claude Code, Codex, ChatGPT or Cursor, all write the same files through the same compiler and get the same errors back. There is no second format for machines and no private dialect for the platform's own sections: the sections in the library are written in exactly what these pages describe.\n\n@[hub-map]\n\n## Build your first section\n\n1. **Open the code editor.** In your dashboard, open **Websites**, then **Edit website**, and choose **Code** in the builder's top bar. On a phone, it is **View code** in the **…** menu.\n2. **Make the file.** Choose **New file** and call it `sections/notice.storelib`. The editor saves a starter section that already compiles.\n3. **Write the section.** Replace the starter with the file below and press **Save**. If it does not compile, nothing is saved, and each error is listed with its line.\n4. **Place it.** Switch back to **Preview**, press **Add section**, and pick **Notice bar** under **Created**. Its settings panel is its schema, drawn.\n5. **Publish.** The file and the page are drafts until you press **Publish**, and then they go live together.\n\nThis is a complete, working section. It reads its colours from the colour scheme the creator picks, its message and link from settings, and it appears in Add Section because its schema has a preset.\n\n```storelib\n<template>\n<section class=\"notice\" data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\">\n  <p class=\"notice__text\">{{ settings.message }}</p>\n  {% if settings.link_url != \"\" %}\n    <a class=\"notice__link\" href=\"{{ settings.link_url | url }}\">{{ settings.link_text }}</a>\n  {% endif %}\n</section>\n</template>\n\n<style>\n.notice { container-type: inline-size; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px 16px;\n  justify-content: center; padding: 14px 24px; background: var(--scheme-background); }\n.notice__text { margin: 0; color: var(--scheme-text); font-size: 14px; }\n.notice__link { color: var(--scheme-link); font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; }\n</style>\n\n<schema>\n{\n  \"type\": \"notice\",\n  \"name\": \"Notice bar\",\n  \"category\": \"Promotion\",\n  \"settings\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"message\", \"label\": \"Message\", \"default\": \"Free shipping on orders over $50\" },\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"link_text\", \"label\": \"Link text\", \"default\": \"Shop now\" },\n    { \"type\": \"url\", \"id\": \"link_url\", \"label\": \"Link\", \"default\": \"\" },\n    { \"type\": \"color_scheme\", \"id\": \"color_scheme\", \"label\": \"Colour scheme\", \"default\": \"dark\" }\n  ],\n  \"presets\": [{ \"name\": \"Notice bar\" }]\n}\n</schema>\n```\n\nFour blocks: markup, scoped CSS, an optional script, and a schema that declares every setting the markup reads. That is the whole format. Next, read [Sections](/developers/sections) for the template language and [Schema](/developers/schema) for every setting type, or copy one of the [Examples](/developers/examples).\n\n## Building with an AI agent\n\nPoint the agent at [storelib.com/llms.txt](/llms.txt). It is a map of this hub in the plain format agents read, and [llms-full.txt](/llms-full.txt) is every page in one file. [Rules before you ship](/developers/rules) is what an agent follows on a Storelib site, and [Building with AI agents](/developers/ai) connects Claude Code or Codex to one, through GitHub or directly over MCP.\n\n## Not built yet\n\nWhere part of the framework is specified but not live, the page says so plainly. In one place:\n\n- **A public theme store.** Themes are shared as files. See [Sharing themes](/developers/sharing-themes).\n- **`allowed_templates` and `disabled_templates`.** Validated and kept; Add Section offers every section on every page.\n- **`migrations`.** Validated and kept; nothing renames a stored setting yet. See [Changing a section safely](/developers/schema#changing-a-section-safely).\n- **The `commerce`, `analytics` and `forms` script capabilities.** Declaring them is accepted; their functions are not on `storelib` yet. See [JavaScript](/developers/javascript#capabilities).\n- **A stylesheet for the whole site**, and the `layout`, `snippets` and `locales` folders. Every section's CSS is scoped to it.\n- **Preview links for a website that is not live.** A preview link shows the drafts of the live website. See [Preview links](/developers/sharing-themes#preview-links).\n- **Sold out on product cards.** A product's `tag` is always empty today. See [Dynamic values](/developers/dynamic-values#products).\n\nLimits are in [Publishing and checks](/developers/publishing#limits), and changes to the framework in the [changelog](/developers/changelog).\n",
  "javascript": "# JavaScript and forms\n\n## Status: scripts run, in a sandbox\n\nA section's `<script>` block is compiled and checked when the file is saved, and **run on the page, and in the builder, inside a sandbox**. It works for sliders that advance, counters, tabs with custom behaviour, and anything else built from listeners, classes, styles and text.\n\n**Prefer HTML and CSS where they do the job alone.** They work before any script has loaded, they are faster, and they come with keyboard support. The patterns in the next part cover accordions, tabs, carousels, pop-ups and motion with no script at all. Reach for a script for what they cannot do: autoplay, a counter, a slider's dots.\n\n### How the sandbox works\n\nA section's script does not run in the page it is on. That page may have a visitor who is signed in, has a cart, or is halfway through a checkout, and a section's code is written by whoever wrote the section. So it runs in one hidden `<iframe sandbox=\"allow-scripts\">` per page, whose origin is opaque: it cannot read the visitor's cookies, storage or session, and it cannot see the page.\n\nInside, `root` is **a copy of your section's own markup**, in a real document. `querySelector`, `classList`, `addEventListener`, `style` and `textContent` all work the ordinary way. Every change you make to the copy is sent to the page, checked, and applied to the real section. Every click, key and input on the real section is sent to the copy, so your listeners run.\n\n| Crosses to the page | Does not |\n| --- | --- |\n| Classes, `hidden`, `open`, `aria-*`, `data-*`, and the other attributes that describe rather than load | `src`, `href`, `action`, `on*` and anything else that loads or runs something |\n| Style properties, one at a time | A style value with `url(`, `expression(`, `javascript:` or a backslash |\n| Text of an element that holds only text | Markup. Text arrives as text, never as HTML |\n| Scrolling, through `storelib.scrollTo` | Adding or removing elements |\n| | Measurements. The copy is not laid out, so `getBoundingClientRect` and `offsetWidth` return 0 |\n\n**Show and hide what the template drew.** A script cannot add an element, because the page's copy belongs to the renderer and would be put back. Draw every slide, message or panel in the template, and switch between them with `hidden` or a class.\n\n**Scroll with `storelib.scrollTo`.** Setting `scrollLeft` changes the copy, which is not what the visitor sees:\n\n```js\nstorelib.scrollTo(track, { left: slideWidth * index, behavior: \"smooth\" })\n```\n\n**Know where you are.** `storelib.inEditor` is `true` in the builder's canvas, so an autoplaying slider can hold still while somebody is designing it.\n\nA script that throws stops on its own, and the section keeps whatever it had drawn. A script that changes the page thousands of times a second is stopped.\n\n## Interaction without script\n\n### Accordion\n\n`<details>` and `<summary>` are an accordion with no script, keyboard support included. Give several the same `name` and opening one closes the others. Build the name from `section.id` so two FAQ sections on one page stay independent.\n\n```storelib\n{% for block in blocks %}\n  <details class=\"faq__item\" name=\"{{ section.id }}-faq\" data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\">\n    <summary class=\"faq__q\">{{ block.settings.question }}</summary>\n    <div class=\"faq__a\">{{ block.settings.answer | raw }}</div>\n  </details>\n{% endfor %}\n```\n\n```css\n.faq__q { cursor: pointer; list-style: none; padding: 18px 0; font-weight: 600; }\n.faq__q::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }\n.faq__q::after { content: \"+\"; float: right; transition: transform 200ms ease; }\n.faq__item[open] .faq__q::after { transform: rotate(45deg); }\n```\n\n### Tabs\n\nRadio inputs sharing a name, each followed by its label and panel. `order` puts the labels in a row and the panels beneath them; `:checked` shows the chosen one.\n\n```storelib\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n  {% for block in blocks %}\n    <input class=\"tabs__radio\" type=\"radio\" name=\"{{ section.id }}-tab\" id=\"{{ section.id }}-{{ block.id }}\"{% if forloop.first %} checked{% endif %}>\n    <label class=\"tabs__label\" for=\"{{ section.id }}-{{ block.id }}\" data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\">{{ block.settings.title }}</label>\n    <div class=\"tabs__panel\">{{ block.settings.content | raw }}</div>\n  {% endfor %}\n</div>\n```\n\n```css\n.tabs { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; }\n.tabs__radio { position: absolute; opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }\n.tabs__label { order: 0; padding: 10px 16px; cursor: pointer; border-bottom: 2px solid transparent; color: var(--scheme-text); }\n.tabs__panel { order: 1; width: 100%; display: none; padding-top: 20px; }\n.tabs__radio:checked + .tabs__label { border-color: var(--scheme-accent); color: var(--scheme-heading); }\n.tabs__radio:checked + .tabs__label + .tabs__panel { display: block; }\n.tabs__radio:focus-visible + .tabs__label { outline: 2px solid var(--scheme-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }\n```\n\n### Carousel\n\nA row that scrolls sideways and snaps to each slide. It swipes on a phone, scrolls with a trackpad and a keyboard, and needs no script.\n\n```css\n.slides {\n  display: flex; gap: 16px;\n  overflow-x: auto; overscroll-behavior-x: contain;\n  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory; scrollbar-width: none;\n}\n.slides::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }\n.slide { flex: 0 0 85%; scroll-snap-align: start; }\n@container (min-width: 768px) { .slide { flex-basis: calc((100% - 32px) / 3); } }\n```\n\n### Pop-up\n\nThe `popover` attribute gives a panel the browser opens, closes and dismisses, with Escape and a tap outside, and no script. In the builder it opens inside the canvas, with the same Escape and tap outside; style its open state with `:popover-open`.\n\n```storelib\n<button class=\"more\" popovertarget=\"{{ section.id }}-details\">Size guide</button>\n<div class=\"sheet\" popover id=\"{{ section.id }}-details\">\n  {{ settings.size_guide | raw }}\n  <button popovertarget=\"{{ section.id }}-details\" popovertargetaction=\"hide\">Close</button>\n</div>\n```\n\n### Motion\n\nCSS transitions and `@keyframes`, on `opacity` and `transform`, which are cheap to animate. Height from `0` to `auto` does not transition; animate `grid-template-rows` from `0fr` to `1fr` instead. Every animation stops for a visitor who has asked for reduced motion; see [Styling](/developers/styling#responsive).\n\n## Forms\n\n**An email box on a Storelib site collects emails for that site's creator.** A form in a section is submitted by the page that hosts it, not by a script, and every address lands in the creator's Subscribers, where their welcome email and automations pick it up. The section's author wires nothing, and the creator sets nothing up.\n\n```storelib\n<form class=\"signup\" data-storelib-form=\"subscribe\">\n  <label class=\"signup__label\" for=\"{{ section.id }}-email\">Email</label>\n  <input id=\"{{ section.id }}-email\" type=\"email\" name=\"email\" required autocomplete=\"email\" placeholder=\"you@example.com\">\n  <button type=\"submit\">{{ settings.button_text }}</button>\n  <p class=\"signup__done\" data-storelib-success hidden>{{ settings.success_message }}</p>\n</form>\n```\n\n### How the page decides what a form means\n\n| The form has | It is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `data-storelib-form=\"subscribe\"` | Sent to the creator's Subscribers. Saying so explicitly always wins. |\n| `data-storelib-form=\"contact\"` | A message to the creator. See [Contact forms](#contact-forms). Never the default: a form has to say it is one. |\n| `data-storelib-form=\"native\"` | Left to the browser, action and all. |\n| No attribute, no `action`, and an `<input type=\"email\">` | Sent to the creator's Subscribers. This is the default, so a sign-up box works even when its author did not say. |\n| No attribute and a real `action` | Left to the browser, such as a search box posting to `/search`. |\n| Anything else | Stopped, so a form with nowhere to go does not reload the page. |\n\nThe email is read from the form's `<input type=\"email\">`, and an optional name from `name=\"name\"`.\n\n### States\n\nWhile it sends, the form carries `data-state=\"sending\"` and its buttons are disabled. It then carries `data-state=\"done\"` or `data-state=\"error\"`, so your CSS can style each:\n\n```css\n.signup[data-state=\"sending\"] button { opacity: 0.6; }\n.signup[data-state=\"error\"] input { border-color: #b91c1c; }\n```\n\nOn success, an element inside the form marked `data-storelib-success` is shown and the rest of the form is hidden. Without one, a plain \"Thanks, you're on the list.\" is shown instead. On error, the reason is shown under the form, which stays filled in so nobody has to type their address again.\n\n### In the builder\n\nNothing is sent from the builder's canvas. A sign-up form there shows its success state with the line \"Sign-ups go to Subscribers on your live site\", because designing a page is not signing up to it.\n\n### Sending to another mailing service\n\nTo post to Mailchimp, Kit, Buttondown or anything else instead, give the form that service's `action` and mark it native. A setting lets the creator choose:\n\n```storelib\n<form method=\"post\"\n  action=\"{% if settings.list_url %}{{ settings.list_url | url }}{% endif %}\"\n  data-storelib-form=\"{% if settings.list_url %}native{% else %}subscribe{% endif %}\">\n```\n\n### Consent\n\nA sign-up form is a request to be emailed, and the platform records where on the page it was given. A **contact** form is not: somebody asking a question has not agreed to a mailing list. Never make a contact form add its sender to Subscribers. Offer an unticked \"Keep me updated\" checkbox instead, and add the sender only when it was ticked.\n\nSign-ups are rate limited per visitor and per address, so a script cannot fill a creator's list.\n\n### Contact forms\n\nA form marked `data-storelib-form=\"contact\"` sends a message to the creator. It is emailed to them, with Reply going to the sender, and kept on the sender's page in Emails. The page reads the fields by name:\n\n| Field | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `email` | Required. |\n| `message` | Required, up to 5,000 characters. |\n| `name`, `subject` | Optional. |\n| `consent` | An unticked checkbox. Only a ticked one adds the sender to Subscribers; otherwise they are kept as a contact who wrote, and never sent news. |\n| `company` | Leave it empty and hidden from people. A value in it is a script, which is told the message went and nothing is sent. |\n\n```storelib\n<form class=\"cf\" data-storelib-form=\"contact\">\n  <label>Name <input name=\"name\" autocomplete=\"name\"></label>\n  <label>Email <input name=\"email\" type=\"email\" autocomplete=\"email\" required></label>\n  <label>Message <textarea name=\"message\" required></textarea></label>\n  <div class=\"cf__trap\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><input name=\"company\" tabindex=\"-1\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div>\n  <label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"consent\" value=\"yes\"> Email me news from {{ shop.name }}</label>\n  <button type=\"submit\">Send</button>\n  <p data-storelib-success hidden>Thanks, your message has been sent.</p>\n</form>\n```\n\nA form sent within a couple of seconds of the page appearing is treated as a script too, and messages are rate limited per visitor, per address and per store.\n\n## Saving products\n\nA button with `data-storelib-wishlist=\"<product id>\"` saves that product to the visitor's Wishlist, and takes it out again. The page sets `aria-pressed` to `\"true\"` or `\"false\"` on every such button to say whether the product is saved, so your CSS draws both states. Put it inside the card, above the card's link:\n\n```storelib\n{% for block in products %}\n  <article class=\"card\">\n    <button type=\"button\" class=\"heart\" data-storelib-wishlist=\"{{ block.id }}\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Save {{ block.settings.title }}\">♡</button>\n    <a class=\"card__link\" href=\"{{ block.settings.url | url }}\">{{ block.settings.title }}</a>\n  </article>\n{% endfor %}\n```\n\n```css\n.heart { position: relative; z-index: 2; }\n.heart[aria-pressed=\"true\"] { color: var(--scheme-accent); }\n```\n\nOnly `products` carry an id that can be saved. A visitor who is not signed in keeps the list in their browser; signing in moves it to their account. On the Wishlist page, a card marked `data-storelib-wishlist-card=\"<product id>\"` is hidden when its product is taken out, and an element marked `data-storelib-wishlist-empty` is shown once none are left.\n\n## Embeds\n\nAn `<iframe>` hands part of the page to somebody else's code, so a section does not get one by asking. It declares the `embed` capability, and every frame takes its address from the `embed` filter:\n\n```json\n\"capabilities\": [\"embed\"]\n```\n\n```storelib\n{% if settings.video_url != \"\" %}\n  <iframe class=\"player\" src=\"{{ settings.video_url | embed }}\" title=\"{{ settings.video_title }}\"\n    loading=\"lazy\" allow=\"autoplay; encrypted-media; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen></iframe>\n{% endif %}\n```\n\n```css\n.player { display: block; width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; border: 0; border-radius: {{ theme.radius.medium }}px; }\n```\n\nThe filter turns what people actually paste, a `youtu.be` link, a `watch?v=` link, a Vimeo page, into the player's address, and returns an empty string for everything else. A setting therefore cannot point a frame at an arbitrary site, whatever somebody types into it. It answers for:\n\n@[embed-hosts]\n\nYouTube links are served from `youtube-nocookie.com`. An `<iframe>` without the capability, or whose `src` comes from anywhere but the filter, is refused when the file is saved.\n\n## The scripting API\n\nThe script is wrapped as a function and handed six things:\n\n```js\n\"use strict\";\n(function (root, settings, blocks, theme, page, storelib) {\n  // your <script> block\n})\n```\n\n| Name | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `root` | A copy of this instance's markup, in the sandbox. Query inside it; it is the whole of the page the script can see. |\n| `settings`, `blocks` | The values **stored** on this instance, as plain objects: what its preset set and what the creator changed. Schema defaults are not filled in, and a responsive value is as stored. |\n| `theme` | The theme settings as saved, not the tokens a template reads. |\n| `page` | `{ template }`. |\n| `storelib` | The platform API: `scrollTo`, `inEditor`, `capabilities`, and whatever the section's declared capabilities add. |\n\nSo a setting the creator never touched is `undefined` in a script, even though the template drew its default. For a value the template has worked out, write it into the markup and read it back from there:\n\n```storelib\n<div class=\"slider\" data-autoplay=\"{{ settings.autoplay }}\" data-interval=\"{{ settings.interval }}\">\n```\n\nA slider that advances on its own and on a click. Every slide is in the template; the script only chooses which one shows. It declares `\"capabilities\": [\"timers\"]` for `setInterval`:\n\n```js\nconst box = root.querySelector(\".slider\")\nconst autoplay = box.dataset.autoplay === \"true\"\nconst seconds = Number(box.dataset.interval) || 5\nconst slides = root.querySelectorAll(\".slide\")\nconst dots = root.querySelectorAll(\".dot\")\nlet current = 0\nfunction show(i) {\n  current = (i + slides.length) % slides.length\n  slides.forEach((el, j) => el.toggleAttribute(\"hidden\", j !== current))\n  dots.forEach((el, j) => el.classList.toggle(\"is-on\", j === current))\n}\nroot.querySelector(\"[data-next]\")?.addEventListener(\"click\", () => show(current + 1))\nroot.querySelector(\"[data-prev]\")?.addEventListener(\"click\", () => show(current - 1))\ndots.forEach((el, j) => el.addEventListener(\"click\", () => show(j)))\nshow(0)\nif (!storelib.inEditor && autoplay) setInterval(() => show(current + 1), seconds * 1000)\n```\n\n### What a script cannot name\n\nThese are refused by name when the file is saved. The check skips strings, comments and property access, so `\"window\"` in a string and `obj.window` are fine; only the bare name is refused.\n\n@[forbidden]\n\nQuerying inside `root`, never the document, is also what makes a section safe to place twice on one page.\n\n### Capabilities\n\nA section declares what it needs in its schema, under `capabilities`. The builder does not show a creator what a section declares yet, so declare only what the section uses. Everything a capability exposes goes to Storelib's own endpoints for that site; a section never gets an arbitrary host.\n\n@[capabilities]\n\nIn a section with a script, an unknown capability is refused, listing the ones that exist. A script is limited in size:\n\n@[limits]\n",
  "pages": "# Pages and page settings\n\nA website is a set of pages, and a page is an ordered list of sections. Each page is stored under a **page key**, and the key is also its address: the page `size-guide` is at `/size-guide`. This page covers which keys exist, which a page cannot take, what a page stores, and the settings each page has of its own.\n\n## The pages every website has\n\n| Key | Address | What it is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `home` | `/` | The home page. Its header and footer are the site's. |\n| `product` | `/products/<slug>` | The one template every product page is drawn with. The product comes from the address. |\n| `about` | `/about` | In the page list from the start, and stored the first time it is saved. |\n| `contact` | `/contact` | The same. |\n| `products` | `/products` | The shop. Until it is edited, it is built from the home page's header and footer and a product grid. |\n| `search` | `/search?q=` | Search results over the newest 24 published products. Built the same way until it is edited. |\n| `404` | any address the site does not have | The page a visitor gets for a missing address: the header and footer around the Page not found section, with a search box and a few products. Built the same way until it is edited, and opened from the page switcher as **404 page**. |\n\nA new website starts with its home and product pages stored. The shop, search and 404 pages exist on the live site from the first publish, whether or not anyone has opened them. Generated, each takes the header's colour scheme, so a dark site's shop is dark too.\n\nThe 404 page keeps the real 404 status. The site's missing address is answered by Storelib's one not-found page, which on a creator's own address asks the site for its 404 page (at `/not_found`, published content only) and draws it in place of a plain message. `404` is a reserved key, so `/404` is one more address the site does not have.\n\nThree **policy pages** are added the first time a website opens in the builder, as ordinary pages made of ordinary sections, linked from the footer. Their text is a template with the parts only the business can fill in written in [square brackets], so an unedited page reads as unfinished.\n\n@[policy-pages]\n\n## Pages you make\n\n**Create page**, in the page switcher at the top of the builder, and **Add page** in Website settings › Pages, ask for a name and make the key from it: letters with accents plain, lower case, and every run of anything that is not a letter or digit turned into one `-`. \"Size Guide!\" becomes `size-guide`. A key is 1 to 63 characters of `a` to `z`, `0` to `9` and `-`, starting with a letter or digit. The name you type is not stored: the builder lists the page by its key, so `size-guide` reads as Size guide.\n\nA new page starts with the site's header and footer and nothing between, saved as a draft. It is on the live site after the next publish.\n\nThese keys are never a page you make, because the platform answers at those addresses or keeps them for later:\n\n@[page-keys]\n\nThe built-in keys above are taken too, and so is any key another page has.\n\n**Deleting a page.** Pages you made, the policy pages among them, have Delete in the page switcher, which asks first. Deleting takes the page off the live site at once; it does not wait for Publish, and Undo does not bring it back. The home and product pages cannot be deleted.\n\n## The header and footer\n\nEvery page stores the header and the footer, and the builder keeps them the same everywhere: when a page is saved, its header and footer are copied onto every other page of the website. The live site goes further and draws the home page's header and footer on every page, whatever a page's own copy says. Change the menu on one page and it has changed on all of them. Both can be hidden, which hides them everywhere; neither can be moved, copied or deleted.\n\nThe header and footer are drawn by the platform's own components, with a panel of their own in the builder, rather than by `.storelib` files, and a section file can never be another one. Everything they do and every setting they take is in [The header and footer](/developers/header-and-footer).\n\n## What a page stores\n\nA page is a list of section instances, each with its own blocks:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"faq_accordion-1727200000000\",\n    \"type\": \"faq_accordion\",\n    \"name\": \"Accordion FAQ\",\n    \"settings\": { \"heading\": \"Questions\", \"color_scheme\": \"light\" },\n    \"blocks\": [\n      { \"id\": \"block-m1x2-1-ab3cd\", \"type\": \"qa\", \"settings\": { \"question\": \"Do you ship abroad?\", \"answer\": \"<p>Yes, everywhere.</p>\" } }\n    ],\n    \"hidden\": false\n  }\n]\n```\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `id` | Made once, when the section is added, and never changed. Reordering moves the section; duplicating gives the copy a new id and new block ids. Treat it as an opaque string. |\n| `type` | The section type, which is the name of its file, `sections/<type>.storelib`. |\n| `name` | What the Sections panel calls it. A copy is named with `(copy)` after it. |\n| `settings` | The values this instance holds: what its preset set, and every value the creator has set since. A value that is not stored falls back to the schema's default when the section is drawn. |\n| `blocks` | The instance's blocks in order, each `{ id, type, settings }`. A block added with Add stores its block type's defaults at that moment. |\n| `hidden` | `true` leaves the section out of the page. It stays in the list, faded. |\n\nThe builder writes a few keys into `settings` itself, from the section's design panel rather than from its schema: `color_scheme` and `color_overrides` for its colours, and `heading_style`, `body_style`, `custom_heading` and `custom_body` for its text. Declare a `color_scheme` setting in a schema; leave the other five to the panel.\n\nA setting the schema no longer declares is kept in `settings` and ignored by the template, so taking a setting out of a file and putting it back restores the value. A block whose type the section no longer declares is kept too.\n\nA page holds at most 60 sections, and a section at most 50 blocks. Every save is refused past either.\n\n## Which template a page is\n\nA section can ask which page it is on through `page.template`. Home is `index`, and every other page is `page.` and its key, such as `page.about` or `page.size-guide`. Two differ between the live site and the builder:\n\n| Page | On the live site | In the builder |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Home | `index` | `index` |\n| A product page | `product` | `page.product` |\n| The shop | `page.products` | `page.products` |\n| Search results | `page.search`, with the search as `page.query` | `page.search` |\n| The 404 page | `page.404` | `page.404` |\n| Any other page | `page.<key>` | `page.<key>` |\n\nSo a section that should draw differently on product pages tests for both: `{% if page.template == \"product\" or page.template == \"page.product\" %}`. See [Dynamic values](/developers/dynamic-values#page).\n\nA section's schema may list `allowed_templates` or `disabled_templates`. Both are validated and kept, and **nothing uses them yet**: Add Section offers every section on every page.\n\n## Each page's search settings\n\nEvery page except home has its own entry under Website settings › Pages & Navigation › Search:\n\n| Field | Stored as | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Title | `page_seo.<key>.title` | What a search result shows. About 70 characters are shown; up to 120 are kept. |\n| Description | `page_seo.<key>.description` | The line under it. About 160 characters are shown; up to 320 are kept. |\n| Show in search engines | `page_seo.<key>.hidden` | Off gives the page `noindex` and leaves it out of the sitemap. |\n\nThey are theme settings, so they are part of the draft: undone with it, and live when the website is published. The home page's title and description are the site's own, under Website settings › SEO & Social, and the switch there that keeps search engines off applies to every page.\n",
  "publishing": "# Publishing and checks\n\nEverything you change on a Storelib website is a draft until you publish, and visitors only ever see what was published. This page is what counts as a draft, what Publish does step by step, which checks run at which moment, the limits, and what keeps a section off a page.\n\n## Drafts and the live site\n\nA website's theme settings, each of its pages and each section file you saved has two halves: a draft and a live copy.\n\n| What | The draft | The live copy | Goes live |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Theme settings | `draft_settings` | `settings` | On Publish |\n| A page | `draft_sections` | `sections` | On Publish |\n| A section file you saved | `draft_content` | `content` | On Publish |\n\nThe builder shows drafts, where there are any, and the live copy everywhere else. The live site shows live copies only. A [preview link](/developers/sharing-themes#preview-links) shows the draft pages and theme settings with the live section files.\n\nA few things are not drafts and change the live site at once: deleting a page you made, taking the website offline, and the address and domain settings in Website settings.\n\n## Saving\n\n**The builder saves by itself.** A second and a half after your last change it writes the draft: first the theme settings, then the page you are on, then it copies the page's header and footer onto every other page. It waits while a save or a publish is already running, and while the builder is switching page. A save that fails says so; it is never silent. After a save the builder asks the server for a new picture of the website for the library card.\n\n**The code editor saves when you press Save**, or ⌘S or Ctrl+S. A section file is compiled first and stored only if it compiles, as a draft, with a new version in its history.\n\n**Ask AI, a connected agent and a GitHub push** save drafts too, through the same routes. Before a connected agent changes a page or the theme settings, a backup of the whole website is taken.\n\n## What Publish does\n\nOn the live website, Publish is in the builder's top bar. A website that is not live has **Save** there instead, and is published from Website settings › Publishing or from its menu in the library. Either way, publishing does this, in order:\n\n1. The builder saves the page you are on and the theme settings as drafts, and copies the header and footer onto every page, exactly as an autosave does.\n2. The website that was live, if it is another one, is taken offline. Only one website is live at a time.\n3. This website is marked live, with the time.\n4. The draft theme settings become the live ones, and every page's draft becomes its live copy. Both are cleaned against the HTML allow list on the way.\n5. Every section file's draft becomes its live copy, and each is recorded in that file's history as a publish.\n6. A backup is taken, labelled Published, unless nothing has changed since the newest backup.\n7. The copy of the site held for visitors is thrown away, so the next visitor gets the new one, and the search engines that take IndexNow are told the pages changed.\n\n**Publish checks nothing.** It compiles no file and holds no page against its sections. Every check happens earlier, when something is saved, and a section that cannot be drawn is left out when the page is drawn, below.\n\n## What is checked, and when\n\n### When a section file is saved\n\nFrom the code editor, Ask AI, a connected agent or a GitHub push:\n\n- **It compiles.** Parse, schema, template, CSS and script, each error with its file and line. A file that does not compile is not stored. See [Sections](/developers/sections#errors).\n- **It is not too big.** A file saved from the code editor or an agent is 1 MB at most, and a section file 512 KB, which the compiler checks.\n- **No other section has its name.** The `name` in the schema is what Add Section and the Sections panel show, so two sections called FAQ are refused, drafts included.\n- **It has not moved underneath you.** The code editor sends the time it read the file, and an agent can, and a file saved somewhere else since then is refused rather than overwritten.\n- **A new file fits.** A website holds 2000 files.\n\nWritten by Ask AI, a connected agent or a GitHub push, a section file must also pass the **quality and integration checks**: it takes its colours from the colour scheme, its type from the theme's text styles, its buttons from the scheme's button colours, its spacing from `padding_top` and `padding_bottom` settings, and its words, pictures and links from settings or blocks; it works at phone width with container queries; it stops moving for a visitor who asked for less motion. The code editor does not hold a person to those. See [Rules before you ship](/developers/rules#native-not-pasted-in).\n\nA section's type is its file name. Ask AI and the agent tools `storelib_create_section` and `storelib_edit_section` also refuse a file whose schema says a different `type`. `storelib_write_section`, the HTTP API and a GitHub push save such a file under its file name without the quality and integration checks, so keep the two the same.\n\n### When a page is saved\n\nFrom the builder, including every autosave:\n\n- The page is cleaned against the HTML allow list: script, event handlers and `javascript:` links are removed from rich text and HTML settings.\n- A page holds 60 sections at most, and a section 50 blocks.\n\nThat is all. A page save does not check that each section's type exists, or that each setting is one its section declares. The builder's panels only offer what a section declares, and the Add block button keeps to the section's `limit` and `max_blocks`.\n\n### When an agent changes a page\n\nThe page tools a connected agent uses (`storelib_update_styles`, `storelib_create_page`, `storelib_create_safe_patch`) refuse a setting its section does not declare, a value its control cannot take, a block type the section does not accept, more blocks than its `max_blocks`, and more than 60 sections on a page. A safe patch applies all of its changes or none. Ask AI reads its own changes back before it finishes, for text that cannot be read against its background, a heading left empty, a preset's demo copy left in place and the same item written twice.\n\n`storelib_run_tests` runs the checks on demand and reports each one: `compile` and `quality` for a file, and `page-types`, `page-settings`, `contrast` and `limits` for a page. Contrast is 4.5 to 1 or better for text against its background.\n\n### When a theme is imported\n\nThe whole package is checked before anything is written, and one failure refuses it. The list is in [Sharing themes](/developers/sharing-themes#import): sizes, paths, `theme.json`, every hash in the manifest, every section compiled, and every page against the sections the package has.\n\n### When a visitor opens a page\n\nEach section is drawn from its live file, compiled once and kept. A file saved before a newer rule existed is drawn with that rule relaxed rather than refused: a `<style>`, `<link>`, `<base>` or `<meta>` tag in its template is taken out of the page instead of stopping the section.\n\nA section is **left out of the page**, and the rest of the page is drawn as normal, when:\n\n- it is hidden,\n- its section file does not compile,\n- there is no file for its type and it is not one of the platform's own sections, such as the header and footer.\n\nIn the builder the last two are drawn as a red card in the section's place, naming the file and the line, so whoever is editing can fix it. A section whose script throws keeps what its template drew; the script stops and the section stays.\n\n## Limits\n\nThe framework's own limits, read from the engine:\n\n@[limits]\n\nAround the builder:\n\n| What | Most allowed |\n| --- | --- |\n| A file saved from the code editor or an agent | 1 MB |\n| Websites in the library | 5: one live and four drafts |\n| Backups kept for each website | The newest 30 |\n| Versions kept of each section file | The newest 50 |\n| Products a template can read | 24 on the live site |\n| Pages a connected agent can make | 60 per website |\n| Requests from one agent connection | 120 a minute, 2000 an hour |\n| Imports started | 5 a minute, 30 an hour |\n\n## Backups\n\nA backup is the website's theme settings and every page, as the builder would show them: the draft where there is one, the live copy where there is not. Section files are not in backups; each keeps its own history instead, under **History** in the code editor.\n\nA backup is taken when you publish, when Ask AI finishes a task, before a connected agent changes a page or the theme, when you press **Back up now** in Website settings › Backups & Versions, and before a restore. A backup that would be the same as the newest one is not taken twice.\n\n**Restore** puts a backup's settings and pages back as the draft, after backing up the draft it replaces. Nothing goes live until you publish.\n",
  "rules": "# Rules before you ship\n\nRead this before you write or change any code on a Storelib site. It applies to every agent: Claude Code, Codex, ChatGPT through GitHub, Cursor, and the builder's own AI. Each rule is enforced by the platform or checked by its tests, so code that breaks one is refused, or looks broken on the creator's site.\n\n## 1. What you may change\n\n- **Section files only.** You write `sections/<type>.storelib`, in lower case with digits, `_` and `-`. Templates, config, layout, snippets and assets are the theme's core. Writes to them are refused.\n- **Never a built-in section.** The sections Storelib ships (header, footer, hero, product page, FAQ and the rest of the library) are what every site is built from. You can read them. A write to one is refused. To change how one works, write a **new** section beside it, such as `sections/hero_custom.storelib`, with the same settings so nothing the creator filled in is lost. Then tell the creator they can use it in place of the built-in one.\n- **Everything you save is a draft.** Nothing reaches a visitor until the creator presses Publish, and every save keeps a version.\n- **One site.** A connection reaches one website. It cannot reach orders, buyers, subscribers or the account, and it cannot delete, rename or publish anything.\n\n`storelib_list_files` marks every file `writable: true` or `false`. Trust it.\n\n## 2. Know the framework first\n\n- Read [Building with AI agents](/developers/ai) and the full reference at [/llms-full.txt](/llms-full.txt). Call `storelib_site` to learn what this website sells, what it is called, and how it looks.\n- A section is one file with four blocks: `<template>`, `<style>`, an optional `<script>`, and `<schema>`. The template language is `{{ }}`, `{% if %}`, `{% for %}` and filters. It is not Vue, JSX or Liquid.\n- Every value the creator might change is a **setting** in the schema. Every repeated thing is a **block**. A template reads only what its schema declares.\n- Compile before you save (`storelib_compile`). Fix the exact line the compiler names; don't rewrite the file.\n\n## 3. Everything the creator sees must be editable\n\nThe creator edits a section in the builder's sidebar, as they would in Shopify. If a word, a picture or a link is typed straight into the markup, they cannot change it.\n\n- **Text** is a `text`, `textarea` or `richtext` setting. Print `richtext` with `| raw`; everything else is escaped for you.\n- **Pictures** are `image_picker` settings. Never put a URL to a picture in a default.\n- **Every link and every button** has two settings: a `text` label and a `url` link. Read the link through the filter, `href=\"{{ settings.button_url | default: '#' | url }}\"`. A `url` setting gives the creator the link picker, with their pages and products in it.\n- **Menus and link lists** are blocks, one block per link, each with its own `label` and `url`. The creator adds, removes and reorders links in the sidebar, and the section draws whatever is there.\n- **Colour** comes from the colour scheme. Declare a `color_scheme` setting and use `var(--scheme-*)`. Don't use hex codes for anything the scheme covers.\n\nThe [page links example](/developers/examples#page-links) does all of this.\n\n## Native, not pasted in\n\nA section an agent writes is checked before it is saved, and refused with the fix if it does not plug into the theme the way the built-in sections do. The same check runs for Storelib AI, Claude Code and Codex over MCP, the agent API, and a push from GitHub:\n\n- **Colour scheme.** A `color_scheme` setting, `data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\"` on the outermost element, and colours read from the scheme. No fixed colours, except white or black over a picture.\n- **Type.** No font names. Headings take `var(--section-heading-size)`, `var(--section-heading-weight)` and the theme's heading font.\n- **Buttons.** Painted from the scheme's button keys, with a `:hover` from its hover keys, so Theme settings › Buttons and the section's scheme apply.\n- **Spacing.** `padding_top` and `padding_bottom` range settings, used for the section's padding.\n- **Words, links and pictures.** Every one a setting or a block. Prompts inside `{% if page.in_editor %}` are fine.\n- **Phones.** Nothing wider than a phone without a `@container` rule that changes it.\n\n`storelib_run_tests` runs the same check, so a file that tests clean is a file that saves.\n\n## 4. Copying a page from a screenshot\n\nWhen you are given a screenshot or a URL to copy:\n\n1. **Split it into sections**, top to bottom: header, hero, features, testimonials, pricing, FAQ, footer, whatever is there. Each becomes its own file with its own schema. Never write a whole page as one section.\n2. **Look for a built-in section first.** If the site already has a section that does the job (a hero, an FAQ, product cards), its settings are the change, not a new file.\n3. **Give each new section a full schema.** Every heading, paragraph, image, button and link from section 3 above, with defaults taken from the screenshot so it looks right the moment it is added.\n4. **Do not build the header or footer.** The site has one of each, built in, with the cart drawer, search, account, phone menu and sticky behaviour already working. Copy the screenshot's header into the real one: its layout, sticky and transparent settings, its icons, its button, and its links as `nav_link` blocks. A section file that is a header, a navigation bar or a footer is refused. See [Changing the header](#changing-the-header).\n5. **Use the site's own words and products**, from `storelib_site`. Never invent a product, a price, a review or a statistic.\n\n## 5. Menus, dropdowns, modals and bottom sheets\n\nThe builder draws the site inside a canvas in the editor, and the section must work there and on the live site.\n\n- **Open things with HTML, not script**: `<details>` for disclosure, radio inputs with `:checked` for tabs, and `popovertarget` with `popover` for menus, sheets and dialogs. The browser handles Escape, tap outside and focus for you. In the builder, a popover opens inside the canvas.\n- **A phone menu is a sheet**: `popover`, positioned with `inset`, `height: 100%`, `overflow-y: auto`, `overscroll-behavior: contain`, and `env(safe-area-inset-bottom)` padding at the foot. Style its open state with `:popover-open`.\n- **A dropdown** is positioned `absolute` under the thing that opens it, inside the section. It hangs over the next section on its own; don't raise it with an enormous `z-index`.\n- **Set `aria-expanded`** on a menu button when a script opens the menu.\n- **`position: fixed`, `100vh` and `100vw`** are the canvas's in the builder and the window's on the live site. Use them for overlays and full-screen menus, and nothing else.\n- Never write `<style>`, `<link>`, `<base>` or `<meta>` inside the template. They act on the whole page, and a save refuses them.\n\n## 6. Motion\n\n- Animate `opacity` and `transform` only. They are cheap, and they don't move anything else on the page.\n- Keep it short and settled: 150 to 300ms, on an easing that lands, such as `cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1)`. Nothing bounces, loops or moves on its own.\n- An entrance plays once, when the section scrolls into view. Nothing animates on every scroll.\n- Everything that moves stops under `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)`.\n- Height from `0` to `auto` does not transition. Animate `grid-template-rows` from `0fr` to `1fr` instead.\n\n## 7. Phones and iOS\n\n- **Build the phone layout first**, then widen with `@container (min-width: …)`. Put `container-type: inline-size` on the section's root.\n- **Never scroll sideways.** Use `minmax(0, 1fr)` rather than `1fr`, `min-width: 0` on flex children, and `overflow-wrap: anywhere` on long words.\n- **Tap targets are at least 44px** high.\n- **Inputs are 16px** or larger, or iOS zooms the page when one is tapped.\n- **Respect the safe area**: `env(safe-area-inset-*)` on anything pinned to an edge of the screen.\n- **Images** carry `width`, `height` or an `aspect-ratio`, so nothing jumps as they load. Use `loading=\"lazy\"` except in the first screen.\n- **No hover-only features.** A phone has no hover, so anything that only appears on hover is missing there.\n\n## 8. What good looks like\n\nStorelib sites should look like they were made by a person with taste, not produced by a generator.\n\n- **Start from what the visitor needs.** Remove before you add: every badge, icon, divider and line of copy has to earn its place.\n- **No generic AI styling.** No purple gradients, glassmorphism, bento grids of identical cards, giant empty heroes, pills everywhere, an icon beside every label, or cards inside cards.\n- **Specific, plain copy.** Say what the thing is and what happens. Never write `unlock`, `elevate`, `seamless`, `powerful`, `effortlessly`, `supercharge` or `transform`, and don't use em dashes.\n- **Use the theme.** Its type styles (`var(--type-h2-size)`, `var(--theme-font-heading)`), its radii (`var(--theme-button-radius)`), its page width (`var(--theme-page-width)`) and its colour schemes. A section that brings its own palette looks pasted in.\n- **Real content, real states.** Empty states appear only in the builder (`{% if page.in_editor %}`), and the live site shows nothing rather than a placeholder.\n\n## 9. Before you say it is done\n\n1. It compiles with no errors, and `storelib_compile` is clean.\n2. Every visible word, picture, link and button is a setting or a block.\n3. It works at 375px, with no sideways scroll, 44px targets and 16px inputs.\n4. Menus, sheets and dialogs open and close with a tap, Escape and a tap outside.\n5. Motion stops under reduced motion.\n6. It uses the site's colour scheme and fonts, and the site's real products.\n7. You changed no built-in section and no file outside `sections/`.\n\nThen say what you changed in one or two plain sentences, and remind the creator it is a draft until they publish.\n\n## Changing the header\n\nThe header and the footer are the site's own sections, of type `header` and `footer`. They are changed, never rewritten. Over MCP, one `storelib_create_safe_patch` makes the header sticky on scroll up, gives it a button, and adds a link with a sub-link:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"page\": \"home\",\n  \"label\": \"Header like the screenshot\",\n  \"ops\": [\n    { \"op\": \"update_section\", \"section_id\": \"<the header's id>\", \"settings\": { \"header_sticky_enabled\": true, \"header_sticky_on_scroll_up\": true, \"show_cta_button\": true, \"cta_text\": \"Shop now\", \"cta_url\": \"/products\" } },\n    { \"op\": \"add_block\", \"section_id\": \"<the header's id>\", \"block_type\": \"nav_link\", \"settings\": { \"label\": \"Courses\", \"url\": \"/products\" } }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nA sub-link is a `nav_link` whose `parent_id` is its parent link's block id. Read the header first with `storelib_read_page` for its id and its links. Every setting the header and footer take is in [The header and footer](/developers/header-and-footer).\n",
  "schema": "# Schema\n\nThe `<schema>` block is JSON. It says what the section is, what a creator can change about it, which blocks it may hold, and how Add Section offers it. **The builder's settings panel for a section is generated from its schema and nothing else.** There is no per-section panel code to write: declare a setting and its control appears.\n\n## A complete schema\n\nThis is the schema of the library's Accordion FAQ, `sections/faq_accordion.storelib`, which the library keeps as its reference section. It is read from the file itself, so it is exactly what ships:\n\n@[library-schema:faq_accordion]\n\n- `header` settings divide the panel into Content, Layout, Style and Spacing. They hold nothing.\n- `color_scheme` has the id `color_scheme`. Keep that id: the scheme picker at the top of a section's panel writes to it.\n- The questions are **blocks** of type `qa`, up to 30, each with its own three settings. See [Blocks](/developers/blocks).\n- The **preset** is what Add Section places: the section with three questions already in it. Without a preset, the section is not offered at all.\n- Padding is two `range` settings in pixels, so a creator can tighten a section without new code.\n\n@[library-section:faq_accordion]\n\n## Top level\n\n| Key | Required | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `type` | yes | The section type: lowercase letters, digits, `-` and `_`, starting with a letter. The file name is what counts, `sections/<type>.storelib`; keep the two the same. |\n| `name` | yes | What the builder calls it, 1 to 80 characters. No two sections on a website may share a name. |\n| `settings` | no, none | The settings, in panel order. At most 120. |\n| `schema_version` | no, 1 | The schema format. A file asking for a newer one than the engine reads is refused rather than misread. |\n| `language_version` | no, 1 | The template language the file is written in. A file that says nothing is version 1 for good. |\n| `category` | no | Which group of Add Section lists it: `hero`, `products`, `features`, `content`, `social-proof`, `cta`, `media`, `about`, `faq` or `marketing`. A section you write is listed under Created, whatever it says. |\n| `description` | no | Found by Add Section's search box. |\n| `icon` | no | Kept. Add Section does not show it. |\n| `version` | no | The section's own version. Nothing reads it yet. |\n| `blocks` | no | The block types this section declares, or the theme blocks it accepts: `[{ \"type\": \"@theme\" }]`. See [Blocks](/developers/blocks). |\n| `max_blocks` | no | The most blocks in all, up to 50. |\n| `presets` | no | What Add Section places: its settings and its blocks, keyed by id with `block_order` the way Shopify writes them, nested as deep as blocks go, with an optional `category`. No presets means the section is not offered. |\n| `capabilities` | no | What the section may reach: `embed` for a player frame, and the script capabilities. See [JavaScript](/developers/javascript#capabilities). |\n| `supports_color_scheme` | no | `false` hides the scheme picker. Unset, it is shown when the schema has a `color_scheme` setting. |\n| `supports_color_overrides` | no | Which colours Advanced colors offers for this section: `true`, `false`, or a list. See [Styling](/developers/styling#a-sections-own-colour-overrides). |\n| `supports_typography_overrides` | no | `false` hides the Heading style and Body style controls. Shown unless it says `false`. |\n| `allowed_templates`, `disabled_templates` | no | Meant to limit the pages a section may be placed on; one or the other, never both. Validated and kept. **Nothing uses them yet**: Add Section offers every section on every page. |\n| `migrations` | no | Meant for renaming a setting across versions. Validated and kept. **Nothing applies them yet**; see [Changing a section safely](#changing-a-section-safely). |\n\nA key the engine does not recognise is kept in the schema, not stripped.\n\n## A setting\n\n| Key | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `type` | One of the control types below. Nothing else exists. |\n| `id` | Lowercase letters, digits and `_`, starting with a letter, unique within the section or within its block type. **Stable for ever**: it is the key the creator's content is stored under. |\n| `label` | What the panel shows. |\n| `info` | A short line of help under the control. |\n| `placeholder` | Shown in an empty box. |\n| `default` | Must be a valid value for the type. Omitted means the control's own value for nothing. |\n| `options` | Required for `select` and `radio`: `[{ \"label\": \"…\", \"value\": \"…\" }]`. The default must be one of the values. |\n| `min`, `max`, `step`, `unit` | For `range` and `number`. A range needs `min` and `max`, with `min` below `max`. `step` is above 0, and `unit` is at most 8 characters, such as `px`. |\n| `responsive` | `true`, on a `number`, `range` or `alignment`, makes the value `{ desktop, tablet, mobile }` and puts a Desktop, Tablet, Phone switch above the control. |\n| `content` | For `paragraph`: the line of help it shows. |\n| `visible_if` | When the panel shows the setting, such as `\"{{ section.settings.source == 'chosen' }}\"`. See [Showing a setting only when it matters](#showing-a-setting-only-when-it-matters). |\n| `limit` | For `product_list`: how many products it holds, 1 to 24. 12 unless set. |\n| `accept` | For `video_url`: the hosts it takes, from `[\"youtube\", \"vimeo\"]`. Both unless set. |\n| `image` | For `focal_point`: the id of the `image_picker` beside it, which the creator taps to choose the spot. |\n\n`header`, `paragraph` and `divider` draw in the panel and hold no value. They still need an `id`, and take no default. An id starting `hdr_` is how the library names its headers.\n\n### Showing a setting only when it matters\n\nA setting that only means something in one mode can say so, and the panel leaves it out until the section is in that mode:\n\n```json\n{ \"type\": \"select\", \"id\": \"source\", \"label\": \"Products\", \"default\": \"picked\",\n  \"options\": [{ \"label\": \"Cards I fill in\", \"value\": \"picked\" }, { \"label\": \"Products I choose\", \"value\": \"chosen\" }] },\n{ \"type\": \"product_list\", \"id\": \"chosen\", \"label\": \"Products to show\",\n  \"visible_if\": \"{{ section.settings.source == 'chosen' }}\" }\n```\n\n- The condition is an expression of the template language inside `{{ }}`, the same one an `{% if %}` takes: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`, `contains`, `and`, `or` and `not`.\n- A section's setting may read `section.settings.<id>`. A block's setting may read its own block's `block.settings.<id>` and the section's `section.settings.<id>`.\n- Each setting is read as the page would draw it: the saved value, or the default when nothing was saved. A condition on a toggle that is on by default holds until somebody turns it off.\n- It decides what the panel shows and nothing else. A hidden setting keeps its value, and the template still reads it.\n- The validator refuses a condition that does not parse, one that reads a setting the section or block does not declare, and one that reads its own setting.\n\n## Every control type\n\nThis list is generated from the builder's control registry, the same one the compiler validates against and the panel draws from. If a type is here, it exists; if it is not, it does not.\n\n@[controls]\n\n### Older type names\n\nEarly sections used different names for some types. They still validate and mean the same thing, but new sections should use the names above.\n\n@[legacy-controls]\n\n## How a value is resolved\n\nWhen a section is drawn, every setting is worked out in one order:\n\n```text\nvalue = the value stored on this instance  ??  the schema's default  ??  the control's value for nothing\n```\n\nFor a block setting, the block's stored value comes first, then the block type's default. The theme is not a step in this chain: it provides tokens a template reads directly, and never overrides a setting by name. Every value a template can read is in [Dynamic values](/developers/dynamic-values).\n\n**What an instance stores.** A section added from Add Section stores its preset's settings. Every value the creator sets after that is stored, even one they set back to the default. A block added with the Add button stores all of its block type's defaults at that moment. So a default changed in the file reaches every instance that does not store that setting, and not the ones that do.\n\n**Responsive values** arrive as `{ desktop, tablet, mobile }`, filled downwards. Set desktop only and tablet and mobile take it.\n\n```json\nstored: { \"columns\": { \"desktop\": 4, \"mobile\": 1 } }\nread:   { \"columns\": { \"desktop\": 4, \"tablet\": 4, \"mobile\": 1 } }\n```\n\nThe builder stores a single value while all three are the same and the object the moment they differ. Read one breakpoint in the template as `{{ settings.columns.desktop }}`, or pass all three to CSS as custom properties and choose with container queries.\n\n**A colour may name a scheme colour as its default**, `\"default\": \"scheme.accent\"`, and then follows the section's scheme until somebody picks a colour. See [Styling](/developers/styling#colours-of-the-sections-own).\n\n**Every value is held to its type.** A saved value the control does not allow is drawn as the default, as if it had never been set: a word in a `range`, an option a `select` does not list, a `color` that is not a colour, a link to `javascript:`, a date that is not a date. The stored value is left as it was, so nothing a creator typed is thrown away, and a schema change that makes an old value valid again brings it back.\n\n## Settings that name the shop's own things\n\nA `product_picker`, a `product_list` and a `link_list` each store a name, and each arrives in the template as the thing it names.\n\n**A `product_picker`** stores a product's address or id. It prints as the address, so `href=\"{{ settings.product | url }}\"` works as it always has, and it carries the product's own fields: `title`, `price`, `compare_price`, `image`, `url` and `tag`.\n\n```storelib\n<a class=\"pick\" href=\"{{ settings.product | url }}\">\n  {% if settings.product.image %}<img src=\"{{ settings.product.image }}\" alt=\"{{ settings.product.title }}\">{% endif %}\n  <span>{{ settings.product.title }}</span> <span>{{ settings.product.price }}</span>\n</a>\n```\n\nA product that is unpublished, deleted or somebody else's is an address with no fields, so `{% if settings.product.title %}` tells a section whether it has one. A price typed into the section can fall back to the product's with `default`: `{{ block.settings.price | default: block.settings.product.price }}`.\n\n**A `product_list`** stores up to `limit` products and arrives shaped like `products`, so one card body serves the chosen products and the whole shop:\n\n```storelib\n{% for block in settings.chosen %}\n  <a href=\"{{ block.settings.url | url }}\">{{ block.settings.title }} {{ block.settings.price }}</a>\n{% endfor %}\n```\n\nThey come in the order the creator chose them, each once. One that has since been unpublished or deleted is left out rather than drawn empty.\n\n**A `link_list`** stores a menu's handle and arrives as `{ handle, title, links }`, each link `{ title, url, links }` with one level of links under it:\n\n```storelib\n<nav>\n  {% for link in settings.menu.links %}\n    <a href=\"{{ link.url | url }}\">{{ link.title }}</a>\n    {% for child in link.links %}<a href=\"{{ child.url | url }}\">{{ child.title }}</a>{% endfor %}\n  {% endfor %}\n</nav>\n```\n\nA creator makes menus in Theme settings, Menus. `main-menu` is always there and is the header's own links, so a section showing the main menu never has them typed twice. A handle with no menu behind it is an empty menu, never an error.\n\n## Shop data in a setting\n\nA creator can put a fact about the shop into a setting rather than typing it: `{{ shop.name }}` in a heading, `{{ creator.avatar }}` in a picture, `© {{ date.year }}` in a footer. The page fills it in on the server before the section is drawn, so the template reads a finished value and needs nothing special. In the builder, **Insert shop data** under a setting offers the ones that fit it.\n\n@[dynamic-sources]\n\n- Text takes any number of placeholders among other words. A link or a picture takes one, as its whole value.\n- Each value is made safe for where it goes: words in text, cleaned markup only in a rich text setting, and only an address that passes the `url` rules in a link.\n- The list is closed. A name that is not on it is left exactly as typed, so `{{ product.cost }}` in a heading is shown as written rather than filled with something it should not show.\n- Nothing filled in is stored. The setting keeps its placeholder, so the page stays right when the shop's name or a price changes.\n- A section's own template reads the same facts directly, as `shop`, `creator` and `product`. See [Sections](/developers/sections).\n\n## Media\n\nAn `image_picker`, `video_picker` or `file_picker` setting holds the file's **public URL**, and an `image_gallery` a list of them. An upload goes to the creator's storage and the setting is its address; a pasted link is kept as it was typed. A template prints it directly:\n\n```storelib\n{% if settings.image != \"\" %}\n  <img src=\"{{ settings.image }}\" alt=\"{{ settings.image_alt }}\" width=\"1200\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\">\n{% endif %}\n```\n\nIn the builder a `video_picker` uploads an MP4, WebM or MOV of up to 200 MB, or takes a link to a video file, and shows the video itself. A YouTube or Vimeo address is a page, not a file, so it belongs in a `video_url` setting, printed through the `embed` filter.\n\nAn empty value means nothing is chosen yet, so put the element behind `{% if %}`. Give every image a width and height, or an `aspect-ratio`, so nothing moves as it loads.\n\n**In a theme package**, the same settings hold `asset://` and the SHA-256 of the file, and the file travels in `assets/`. An import stores each file in the importing account and writes its new URL back, so on a site a setting is always a URL. See [Sharing themes](/developers/sharing-themes). The `asset` filter passes a URL through, and prints an `asset://` reference as nothing.\n\n## Changing a section safely\n\nSetting ids and block types are the contract between a section file and every page that uses it. An update may freely:\n\n- add settings and block types; existing instances get their defaults,\n- change defaults; instances that do not store the setting follow,\n- change markup and CSS.\n\n**Renaming or removing an id loses what was stored under it**, from the section's point of view. The value stays on each page under the old id, where the template cannot read it, and the new id starts at its default. Putting the old id back brings the value back. Declare the rename in `migrations`, a list of `{ \"from\", \"to\", \"since\" }`, and a section's own setting stored under `from` is read under `to` whenever `to` holds nothing, as the section is drawn. It applies to the section's settings, not to its blocks' settings, and the stored page is not rewritten. To change what a setting is called in the panel, change its `label`; the `id` can stay.\n\n## What the validator refuses\n\nEach refusal names the path, such as `settings[3].default`, and the line in the file:\n\n- a `type` that is not a control type, listing the ones that are,\n- a bad or duplicate `id`,\n- `options` missing on a `select` or `radio`, or a default that is not among them,\n- a default of the wrong type, such as `default \"big\" is not a range`, or any default on a `header`, `paragraph` or `divider`,\n- `responsive` on a type that cannot be,\n- a `range` without `min` and `max`, or with `min` not below `max`,\n- a `name` that is empty or over 80 characters,\n- a block type declared twice, a block `limit` that is not a positive whole number, or `max_blocks` over 50,\n- a preset that sets a setting or adds a block the section does not declare or accept, a keyed preset whose `block_order` and `blocks` disagree, or a static block without an id,\n- a section that both declares its own blocks and accepts theme blocks, or accepts `@app`,\n- both `allowed_templates` and `disabled_templates`,\n- a `visible_if` that does not parse, or reads a setting that is not declared,\n- a `product_list` whose `limit` is not a whole number from 1 to 24, a `video_url` that accepts a host Storelib does not play, or a `focal_point` whose `image` is not an `image_picker` beside it,\n- a `schema_version` or `language_version` newer than the engine,\n- more than 120 settings.\n",
  "sections": "# Sections\n\nA section is one file, `sections/<type>.storelib`, that describes a section type completely: how it looks, how it is styled, what a creator can change about it, and what it does. It has four blocks, in any order, at most one of each.\n\n| Block | Required | What it holds |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `<template>` | yes | The markup, in the template language below. |\n| `<style>` | no | CSS for this section's instances only. `<style scoped>` means the same thing. See [Styling](/developers/styling). |\n| `<script>` | no | Behaviour. Checked when saved, then **run in a sandbox** against a copy of the section. See [JavaScript](/developers/javascript). |\n| `<schema>` | yes | JSON: the section's identity, settings, blocks and presets. See [Schema](/developers/schema). |\n\n## Rules the parser enforces\n\n- A block's opening and closing tags count only when they start a line. `</style>` inside a string in the template is text, not the end of the style block.\n- A second `<template>`, or a second of any block, is refused, naming the line of the first.\n- A block that is never closed is refused at the line it opened.\n- A file without `<template>` or `<schema>` is refused.\n- Outside the four blocks, only blank lines, `{# … #}` comments and lines starting `<!--` may appear. A `{# #}` comment above the blocks is where a section explains itself; it never reaches a page.\n- A file is at most 512 KB.\n- There is no unscoped CSS in a section, and no stylesheet for the whole site that you can write yet.\n\n## The template language\n\nThe template is HTML plus output, conditions, loops and comments. It is **not** Vue, React, JSX or Liquid, although it looks like Liquid: `v-if`, `@click`, `:class` and JSX braces are refused by the compiler. It is compiled to a tree and walked, with no `eval` anywhere in it, which keeps rendering deterministic: the same file and the same settings always give the same markup, in the builder and on the live site.\n\n```storelib\n<h2>{{ settings.heading }}</h2>\n\n{% if settings.show_subheading and settings.subheading != \"\" %}\n  <p>{{ settings.subheading }}</p>\n{% elsif settings.show_subheading %}\n  <p>No subheading yet</p>\n{% endif %}\n\n<ul>\n{% for block in blocks %}\n  <li data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\" class=\"{% if forloop.first %}is-first{% endif %}\">\n    {{ forloop.index }}. {{ block.settings.title | upcase }}\n  </li>\n{% else %}\n  <li>Add an item</li>\n{% endfor %}\n</ul>\n\n{# A comment. It is read and thrown away, and never reaches the page. #}\n```\n\n### Output\n\n`{{ expression }}` prints a value, always HTML-escaped. The only way to print markup is the `raw` filter, which prints a value as it is. The compiler does not check what `raw` is used on, so it is yours to keep it to `richtext` settings.\n\nPut an output that is an attribute's value inside quotes: `class=\"{{ settings.style }}\"`, never `class={{ settings.style }}`. Without them a value with a space in it becomes more than one attribute. Nothing it adds can run, because a drawn section is checked again as it is drawn, but it can still break your markup.\n\nRich text is cleaned against an allow list of tags and attributes: when a page is saved, when a theme is imported, when a website is published, and every time a page is read. Which values are cleaned is decided by the section: every `richtext` setting of the library's own sections, and, in every section including yours, a setting whose id is `body`, `content`, `answer`, `description` or `preview_text`. So give a rich text setting in a section you write one of those ids, and never `raw` a plain `text` or `textarea` setting.\n\n### Expressions\n\n- **Literals:** `\"text\"`, `'text'`, `12`, `-3`, `1.5`, `true`, `false`, and `null`, `nil` or `blank`, which are all the same empty value.\n- **Paths:** `settings.heading`, `block.settings.url`, `theme.colors.primary`, `items[0]`, `items[\"key\"]`. Indexes must be literal; there are no computed lookups.\n- **Comparisons:** `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `contains`.\n- **Logic:** `and`, `or`, `not`, and parentheses.\n- **Filters:** `value | name: arg1, arg2`, chained left to right.\n\nA path reads plain data only. `__proto__`, `constructor` and `prototype` cannot be reached, a function is never called, and a missing value is `null` rather than an error. Lists have `.size`, `.first` and `.last`. How values compare, what counts as true, and every field of every name are in [Dynamic values](/developers/dynamic-values).\n\n### Every name must be declared\n\nA section that accepts theme blocks (`\"blocks\": [{ \"type\": \"@theme\" }]`) draws them with `{% content_for \"blocks\" %}` and places a static one with `{% content_for \"block\", type: \"heading\", id: \"title\" %}`. See [Blocks](/developers/blocks).\n\n`{{ settings.foo }}` compiles only when the schema declares a setting with the id `foo`, and `{{ block.settings.bar }}` inside `{% for block in blocks %}` only when a block type declares `bar`. A typo is a compile error with a line number. A setting the schema declares and the template never reads is a warning: it would be a control in the panel that does nothing. A setting read only in `<style>` counts as unread for this warning, which does not stop a save.\n\n### What a template can read\n\n| Name | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `settings.*` | This instance's settings, worked out: the stored value, else the schema's default, else the control's own. Responsive values arrive as `{ desktop, tablet, mobile }`. |\n| `blocks` | This instance's blocks in order, each `{ id, type, settings }`. |\n| `section.id`, `section.type` | This instance. Use `section.id` to build element ids, so the section can appear twice on a page. `section.settings` and `section.blocks` are the two above. |\n| `scheme.*` | The colour scheme this instance uses, with the section's own overrides applied. See [Styling](/developers/styling#colour-schemes). |\n| `theme.colors.*`, `theme.typography.*`, `theme.radius.*`, `theme.spacing.*`, `theme.settings.*` | The theme's tokens, and every theme setting as saved. |\n| `page.template` | Which page this is: `index` for home, `page.about` and so on for the rest, `product` on a live product page and `page.product` in the builder. |\n| `page.in_editor` | `true` inside the builder. Put prompts for the creator behind it so a visitor never sees them. |\n| `page.query` | On the live search page, what was searched for. |\n| `products` | The creator's published products, newest first, each `{ id, type, settings }` with `settings.title`, `price` (already formatted), `compare_price`, `image`, `url` and `tag`. The same shape as a block, so one card works for both. Up to 24 on the live site, and on a product page every one but the product it shows. |\n| `shop` | The store: `shop.name`, `shop.tagline`, `shop.logo`, `shop.url`, and `shop.socials`, a list of `{ key, label, url }` from the theme's social links. On a published page `shop.description` and `shop.product_count` too. `shop.email` is the support email from Settings, and it is only there on a page where a section has a `show_email` setting turned on. |\n| `routes` | The addresses every Storelib website answers on: `root_url`, `products_url`, `search_url`, `cart_url`, `contact_url`, `wishlist_url`, `account_url`, `account_login_url` (the sign in page; `account_signin_url` and `account_register_url` are the same page, since a new email makes the account there), the form addresses `account_code_url`, `account_verify_url` and `account_profile_url`, and `account_logout_url`. |\n| `customer` | The signed-in customer, on the account page: `customer.name`, `first_name`, `email`, `orders_count` and `orders`, one per payment, each `{ number, date, status, total, items, action_label, action_url }`, and `sample`, true in the builder only. `null` everywhere else, and on a page for a visitor who is not signed in. |\n| `wishlist` | The visitor's saved products, on the account and Wishlist pages: `wishlist.items`, each `{ id, title, price, compare_price, image, url, tag }`, `wishlist.count`, and `wishlist.signed_in`. `null` on other pages. |\n| `creator.name`, `creator.headline`, `creator.bio`, `creator.avatar` | The creator's public profile. |\n| `product` | On the product page only, the product it shows: `title`, `price`, `compare_price`, `description` (words), `description_html` (cleaned markup, printed with `raw`), `image`, `images`, `url`, `includes` and `id`. Empty everywhere else, so `{% if product %}` tells a section which page it is on. |\n| `forloop.index`, `index0`, `first`, `last`, `length` | Inside a `for`. |\n\n`customer` and `wishlist` belong to one visitor, so they are only given to the pages drawn for one visitor, which are never cached. A section on the home page cannot read who is looking; it can draw a heart (see [JavaScript and forms](/developers/javascript#saving-products)) and the page fills it in.\n\nEvery field of each, with where it comes from and examples, is in [Dynamic values](/developers/dynamic-values).\n\n### Filters\n\n@[filters]\n\nTwo rules worth remembering: put `| url` on every `href` built from a setting, and use `raw` only on a `richtext`, `inline_richtext` or `html` setting, or on `product.description_html`. Each of those is cleaned against an allow list before a template sees it. A filter name the table does not have is not an error: the value prints unchanged.\n\n### What the compiler refuses\n\n@[refusals]\n\n## Errors\n\nNothing in the framework throws on bad input. Every stage returns either a result or a list of errors in one shape, which the code editor shows beside the line, the AI receives to fix its own output, and the importer attaches to a refused package.\n\n```ts\ninterface ThemeError {\n  stage: \"parse\" | \"schema\" | \"template\" | \"css\" | \"script\" | \"package\"\n  message: string\n  file?: string     // \"sections/hero.storelib\"\n  line?: number     // 1-based, in that file\n  column?: number\n  path?: string     // \"settings[3].default\", for errors about data\n}\n```\n\nErrors inside the schema's JSON are reported at the line of the `.storelib` file, not the line of the JSON, so an error always points where you would click. A **warning**, such as a setting the template never reads, does not stop a save.\n\n| Stage | Refuses | Example message |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `parse` | A malformed file. | `<style> is never closed` |\n| `schema` | JSON that breaks the schema rules. | `default \"big\" is not a range` at `settings[2].default` |\n| `template` | Bad markup or a refused construct. | `a <script> tag inside <template>` |\n| `css` | Syntax errors, `@import`, size. | `@import is not allowed` |\n| `script` | Forbidden names, unknown capabilities, size. | `\"window\" is not available to a section` |\n| `package` | A theme file that fails an import check. | `sha256 does not match the manifest` |\n\n## Checklist for a new section\n\n- `type` in the schema equals the file name.\n- Every setting has an `id` you are willing to keep for ever. It is the key the creator's content is stored under.\n- Every default is a valid value for its type.\n- The template reads every setting, and declares every setting it reads.\n- Repeated content is blocks, each root carrying `data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\"`.\n- Colour comes from `scheme`, type from the theme, spacing from range settings.\n- Laid out for a phone first, with container queries for wider. See [Styling](/developers/styling#responsive).\n- No fixed `id=\"…\"`; build ids from `{{ section.id }}`.\n- At least one preset, or the section is not offered in Add Section.\n",
  "selling": "# Selling: products, checkout and payments\n\nA Storelib website sells the creator's products, and the part that takes the money is Storelib's, not the section's. A section shows products and sends a buyer to checkout. It never takes a payment, never asks for a card, and never loads a payment script. This page is what a section, and whoever writes it, needs to know about that: the product data a section reads, the buy link, and where the buyer goes after paying.\n\n## Products reach a section as `products`\n\n`products` holds the creator's published products, newest first, up to 24 on the live site, in the same `{ id, type, settings }` shape as blocks. The platform reads them on the server when the page is drawn, so a price changed in the shop is on the website within a minute. On the search page it holds the products that match. Which list each page has is in [Dynamic values](/developers/dynamic-values#products).\n\n| Field | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `block.id` | The product's id. Checkout takes it. |\n| `block.settings.title` | Its name. |\n| `block.settings.price` | Its price, already formatted, such as `$48.00`. Empty when it costs nothing. For pay what you want, the lowest price. |\n| `block.settings.compare_price` | The price before a sale, formatted, or empty. Show it struck through. |\n| `block.settings.currency` | `USD`. Every price on Storelib is charged in US dollars. |\n| `block.settings.free` | `true` when it costs nothing. Write your own word for it, such as \"Free\", as a setting. |\n| `block.settings.pay_what_you_want` | `true` when the buyer chooses the amount, from `price` up. |\n| `block.settings.image` | Its main picture, or empty. |\n| `block.settings.images` | Every picture, the main one first. Loop over it for a gallery. |\n| `block.settings.url` | Its page on this website, such as `/products/preset-pack`. |\n| `block.settings.slug` | The last part of that address, `preset-pack`. |\n| `block.settings.kind` | What kind of product it is, as it was made: `digital`, `course` and so on. |\n| `block.settings.available` | `true` when it can be bought. Stock is not read yet, so today it is always `true`. |\n| `block.settings.tag` | `Sold out` when it cannot be bought, or empty. Today it is always empty, because stock is not read yet. |\n| `block.settings.checkout_url` | Storelib checkout for this product, `/checkout?productId=<id>&src=website`. Empty when it cannot be bought. |\n| `block.settings.variants` | Its options, such as sizes or licences. Empty when it has none. See below. |\n\nTemplates do not do money: never compute, round or convert a price. Show the text you are given.\n\n## A product's options: `variants`\n\nA product with options is bought as one of them. Each entry in `block.settings.variants` has:\n\n| Field | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `variant.id` | The option's id. |\n| `variant.name` | Its name, such as `Commercial licence`. |\n| `variant.price` | Its own price, formatted, or empty when it is free. |\n| `variant.compare_price` | Its price before a sale, or empty. |\n| `variant.free` | `true` when this option costs nothing. |\n| `variant.available` | `true` when it can be bought. Always `true` today, as above. |\n| `variant.checkout_url` | Storelib checkout for this option, `/checkout?productId=<id>&variantId=<option id>&src=website`. |\n\nA product with options that is sent to checkout without one arrives with its first option chosen, and the buyer can change it there.\n\n## The product page: `page.product`\n\nEvery product has a page on the website at `/products/<slug>`. A section placed on the product page reads the product the page is about as `page.product`, with the same fields as a product in `products`, and one more:\n\n| Field | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `page.product.settings.description` | The product's description as plain text, line breaks kept. It is text, never HTML: print it with `{{ }}` and give it `white-space: pre-line`. |\n\nOn a product page `products` is there too, the newest 24, for a row of more products under this one.\n\nIn the builder, while the product page is open, `page.product` is the newest product, so the section draws something real. There it has no description and no options yet. On any other page it is empty, so a section written for the product page draws nothing without it, and a note behind `page.in_editor` for the creator, as the example below does. `\"allowed_templates\": [\"product\"]` in its schema says where it belongs, though Add Section does not act on that yet: see [Schema](/developers/schema).\n\n## Two ways to sell from a section\n\n**Link to the product's page.** Every product has a page on the website with its pictures, its description and the Buy and Add to cart buttons, which already work. This is the right link for a card in a grid.\n\n```storelib\n{% for block in products %}\n  <a class=\"card\" href=\"{{ block.settings.url | url }}\">\n    {% if block.settings.image != \"\" %}<img src=\"{{ block.settings.image }}\" alt=\"{{ block.settings.title }}\" width=\"600\" height=\"600\" loading=\"lazy\">{% endif %}\n    <span class=\"card__title\">{{ block.settings.title }}</span>\n    <span class=\"card__price\">\n      {% if block.settings.compare_price != \"\" %}<s>{{ block.settings.compare_price }}</s>{% endif %}\n      {{ block.settings.price }}\n    </span>\n  </a>\n{% endfor %}\n```\n\n**Link straight to checkout.** For a section whose job is the purchase, a hero with one product or a pricing table, send the buyer to Storelib checkout with the product's own buy link:\n\n```storelib\n{% if block.settings.available %}\n  <a class=\"buy\" href=\"{{ block.settings.checkout_url | url }}\">{{ settings.button_label }}</a>\n{% else %}\n  <span class=\"sold\">{{ block.settings.tag }}</span>\n{% endif %}\n```\n\n`src=website` in that link is how the order is recorded as a Website order in the creator's Orders. Use `checkout_url` rather than writing the link yourself, and it is always right.\n\n## One product, chosen in the sidebar\n\nTo let the creator pick which product a section shows, give it a `product_picker` setting. It holds the product's page address, so keep the product in `products` whose `url` matches it:\n\n```storelib\n{% for block in products %}\n  {% if block.settings.url == settings.product or block.id == settings.product %}\n    <h2>{{ block.settings.title }}</h2>\n    <p>{{ block.settings.price }}</p>\n    <a class=\"buy\" href=\"{{ block.settings.checkout_url | url }}\">{{ settings.button_label }}</a>\n  {% endif %}\n{% endfor %}\n```\n\n`products` is the newest 24. A product older than that is not in it; link to its page instead.\n\n## Buy buttons and checkout\n\n- A buy button links to `checkout_url`, or to one option's `variant.checkout_url`. Never build the address from pieces, and never add a price to it: checkout reads the price from the product.\n- A product that is not `available` gets no buy button: show its `tag` instead, so the section is right on the day stock is read.\n- **The buyer comes back to the website.** When a buy link is followed on a website, the site adds which website it is and the page the buyer was on. After paying, checkout sends the buyer back to that page with `?checkout=paid` on the address, and the site says the payment went through. Back on the checkout page goes to the same page.\n- **A page of your own to come back to.** Add `return` to the link with a path on this site, such as `{{ block.settings.checkout_url }}&return=/thank-you`, and the buyer lands there after paying. `cancel` does the same for Back.\n- **Only this creator's own addresses.** Checkout checks `return` and `cancel` on the server against the creator's own sites: their Storelib addresses and their verified domains. Anything else is ignored, never followed.\n- **The receipt.** The receipt and its download link are emailed to the buyer either way. When checkout was not started on the creator's website, the buyer lands on the Storelib receipt, as before.\n\n## What checkout does\n\n- It is Storelib's page, at storelib.com, where the buyer is signed in. On a website's own address, `/checkout` goes there.\n- The buyer pays by card or Google Pay, through Stripe. Every price is charged in US dollars.\n- **The price is read from the product, never from the link or the page.** Nothing a section writes can change what is charged.\n- A buyer who already owns a product is not charged for it again.\n- After paying, a digital product is in the buyer's Storelib library straight away, and the download link is emailed to them.\n- The money goes to the creator's connected Stripe account, set up in Settings › Payments.\n\n## The cart\n\nA website has a cart. The header's bag opens it, the product page's **Add to cart** fills it, and `/cart` shows it and sends it to checkout as one payment, after which the buyer comes back to the website's home page. It is kept in the visitor's browser, per shop. A section cannot add to it: link to the product's page, where the button is.\n\n## What a section may not do\n\n- Build a payment form, or ask for a card number, a bank account or a password. Ever.\n- Load Stripe, PayPal or any other payment or tracking script. A `<script>` tag in a template is refused when the file is saved, and a section's own script runs in a sandbox where `fetch` and the network are refused too.\n- Put a price in a default, or invent a product, a discount or a review. Show the products the site has.\n- Promise delivery times, refunds or guarantees the creator has not written. Those belong on the policy pages, which the creator edits.\n\n## Where this lives in the builder\n\n- **Website Settings › Commerce** shows how many products the website can show, what checkout takes and where payouts go.\n- **Website Settings › Policies** has the refund, privacy and terms pages every website links to from its footer.\n- A connected agent reads the site's products, with their ids, buy links and options, from `storelib_site`. See [Building with AI agents](/developers/ai).\n\n## A product page done properly\n\nA product page section: the product's pictures as a gallery that swipes on a phone, its price with the sale price struck through, its options as choices with a buy button for the one chosen (no script: radio inputs and `:checked`), the sold out state, and the description. Every word the section adds is a setting.\n\n@[example:product_page]\n",
  "sharing-themes": "# Sharing themes\n\nA theme travels as a file. You export a website from your library as one `.storelib-theme.zip`, send it however you send files, and the other creator imports it into their library as a new draft. A preview link shows your unpublished work to someone without handing them anything, and Duplicate copies a website inside your own account.\n\n> **Note** There is no public theme store yet. Sharing is a file one creator hands another, and nothing on Storelib lists, sells or reviews themes.\n\n## Export\n\nIn your dashboard, open **Websites**, then the website's **More** menu, then **Export theme**. The browser downloads the website as `<name>.storelib-theme.zip`, a zip any unzip tool opens. The format is in [Themes](/developers/themes#the-package-format).\n\n| Part | What goes in the file |\n| --- | --- |\n| Theme settings | The draft: your latest changes, whether or not they are published. |\n| Pages | Each page **as last published**. Changes to a page since then are not in the file, and on a website that has never been published that means the pages as the website was made. Publish before exporting to include them. |\n| Section files | All 33 of the theme's section files, with your published version in place of any you changed, and every section file of your own that has been published. A file you have saved and not published goes out as its published version, or not at all. |\n| Media | Every picture, video, sound and file a setting or a page points at, downloaded and named by the SHA-256 of its bytes. Each link to one becomes `asset://` and that hash. |\n| Cover | The newest pictures of the website, desktop and phone, for the library card of whoever imports it. |\n\nYour brand name, logo, favicon, social links, the announcement bar and the SEO text are theme settings, so they travel with the theme. Change them after importing a theme someone else made.\n\nMedia is carried within these limits. A link to a file of any other type is not downloaded, and travels as the link:\n\n@[media-types]\n\nA file that cannot be downloaded, or is over its limit, is left out of the package and **the setting that pointed at it arrives empty**, so the section shows what it shows when nothing has been chosen. It is never left pointing at your storage. The library does not say what was left out yet. A YouTube or Vimeo link is a link rather than a file, so it travels as it is.\n\n## Import\n\nIn **Websites**, press **Import theme** and choose the file. It takes a `.storelib-theme.zip`, and a `.storelib-theme` or `.zip` file as well. The file goes straight to storage with a progress bar, then the import reads it from there.\n\nBefore anything is written, the whole package is checked, and **one failure refuses all of it** with the list of reasons:\n\n1. The library has room for another website. It holds five: one live and four drafts.\n2. The file is a zip, and 50 MB or less.\n3. It holds 2000 files or fewer. By the sizes the zip declares, before anything is unpacked, each text file is 512 KB or less, each asset 15 MB or less, and the whole 200 MB or less.\n4. Every path is `theme.json`, `manifest.json`, or inside `config`, `layout`, `templates`, `sections`, `blocks`, `snippets`, `assets` or `locales`. The files sit at the root of the zip, not inside a folder.\n5. `theme.json` has `\"format\": \"storelib-theme\"`, a name, and asks for no newer engine than this one.\n6. Every file and asset is in `manifest.json`, and every SHA-256 and asset size matches.\n7. Every `sections/<type>.storelib` compiles, and every `blocks/<type>.storelib` compiles as a theme block. The file name is the type.\n8. A section file that is a header or a footer is not refused: it is **left out**, with every use of it on a page, and the import says so. The website keeps its own header and footer; change theirs in the `header` and `footer` entries of the templates instead. See [The header and footer](/developers/header-and-footer).\n9. Every `templates/*.json` has `sections` and `order`, holds 60 sections or fewer with 50 blocks or fewer each, and every section on it is a type the package has or a type Storelib has.\n\nThen the import writes a **new draft website** with the name in `theme.json`:\n\n- Each asset is stored in your account and every `asset://` reference is replaced by its new address. A file's type is taken from its extension, never from what the package says it is. An asset of a type Storelib does not take is dropped, and the setting that pointed at it is emptied.\n- The theme settings and every page are cleaned against the same HTML allow list the builder's own saves go through: script, event handlers and `javascript:` links are removed before anything is stored.\n- Section files that differ from the theme's own are stored as your files. One identical to Storelib's copy is not, so the website keeps following Storelib's version.\n- The package's cover goes on the library card, and a new picture of the website is taken after.\n\nThe new website is a draft. Open it, look at every page, and publish it when it is right.\n\nStarting an import is limited to 5 a minute and 30 an hour for each account. Exports made before the package format existed, a zip with its own `manifest.json` and an `images` folder, are still read and converted on the way in.\n\n> **Warning** A theme someone else made is their code on your site. The importer compiles every section, cleans the HTML in settings and pages, and a template cannot hold a script or an event handler. One thing it cannot judge is `| raw`, which prints a value as HTML. Before you publish a theme you did not build, open its section files in the code editor, search for `| raw`, and check each one is on a `richtext` setting.\n\n## Editing a package by hand\n\nA package is a folder with a manifest, so it can be edited outside Storelib: by you, or by an AI agent working on the files. Three things decide whether the import takes it back:\n\n1. **Update the manifest.** The import recomputes the SHA-256 of every file and refuses a package where one does not match, or where a file is not listed. Run this in the unzipped folder after editing, with Node 18 or later:\n\n```js\n// update-manifest.mjs: run inside the unzipped theme folder, after editing it\nimport { createHash } from \"node:crypto\"\nimport { readFileSync, readdirSync, statSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\"\nimport { join } from \"node:path\"\n\nconst walk = (dir) =>\n  readdirSync(dir)\n    .filter((name) => !name.startsWith(\".\"))\n    .flatMap((name) => {\n      const path = dir === \".\" ? name : join(dir, name)\n      return statSync(path).isDirectory() ? walk(path) : [path]\n    })\n\nconst manifest = JSON.parse(readFileSync(\"manifest.json\", \"utf8\"))\nmanifest.files = {}\nfor (const path of walk(\".\")) {\n  if (path === \"manifest.json\" || path.startsWith(\"assets/\")) continue\n  const bytes = readFileSync(path)\n  manifest.files[path] = { sha256: createHash(\"sha256\").update(bytes).digest(\"hex\"), bytes: bytes.length }\n}\nwriteFileSync(\"manifest.json\", JSON.stringify(manifest, null, 2) + \"\\n\")\nconsole.log(`${Object.keys(manifest.files).length} files listed`)\n```\n\n2. **Zip the contents, not the folder.** `theme.json` has to be at the root of the zip. A file whose name starts with a dot, such as `.DS_Store`, is not a path inside a theme, so leave those out:\n\n```bash\ncd my-shop\nnode update-manifest.mjs\nzip -r ../my-shop.storelib-theme.zip . -x \".*\"\n```\n\n3. **Leave the assets as they are.** Each asset is named by the SHA-256 of its bytes and listed under `assets` in the manifest; the script above does not rewrite those. Add new pictures in the builder after importing.\n\nEvery section file still has to compile. The quickest check is to paste one into the code editor and save it, or run it through `storelib_compile` from a connected agent; see [Building with AI agents](/developers/ai).\n\n## Preview links\n\nA preview link shows your unpublished changes to someone who is not signed in to your account. On the live website, press **Publish** in the builder's top bar, then **Preview**. The first press makes the link, `<your address>/preview/<token>`, and opens it; the panel then shows it with a copy button and **Turn off**.\n\n- It shows the **draft pages and draft theme settings**. Section files show their published version; a section file you saved and have not published is not in a preview yet.\n- The site's pages are under it: `/preview/<token>/<page>`, the shop at `/preview/<token>/products` and each product at `/preview/<token>/products/<slug>`.\n- Search engines are told not to index it.\n- There is one link per website, a random 32-byte token checked against the database. **Turn off** removes it, and every copy of it stops working at once. Pressing Preview again makes a new one at a new address.\n- It is for the website that is live. A website that is not live has Save in place of Publish, and no Preview; a link made for one anyway, by a connected agent's `storelib_run_preview`, opens your live website instead, or your page on storelib.com when nothing is live. To show work in progress, make the changes on the live website: they stay drafts until you publish.\n\nA preview link grants reading that one website's drafts. It is not access to the account, its products or its other websites.\n\n## Duplicate\n\n**Websites › More › Duplicate** copies a website as it stands, into a new draft named with `(copy)` after it. The library needs a free place for it.\n\n| Part | Copied |\n| --- | --- |\n| Theme settings | The draft and the live copy |\n| Pages | Every page, draft and live |\n| Section files | Every file of yours, draft and live |\n| Media | The same links, to the same files in your storage |\n| Cover | Yes, then a new picture of the copy |\n\n## What does not travel\n\n| | Export and import | Duplicate |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unpublished changes to pages and section files | No | Yes |\n| The preview link | No | No |\n| Backups and each file's history | No | No |\n| Connections: Claude Code, Codex, GitHub | No | No |\n| The address and a connected domain | No | No |\n| Products, orders, customers and subscribers | Never | Never |\n\nA theme is the website's look and content. The account behind it stays where it is.\n",
  "shopify": "# Coming from Shopify\n\nA Storelib theme is built the way an Online Store 2.0 theme is: pages are lists of sections, a section is one file with its schema inside it, repeated content is blocks, and the settings panel is generated from the schema. If you have written a Dawn section, most of what you know carries over. This page lists what maps to what, and what Storelib does not have and why.\n\nThe biggest differences first:\n\n- **A section is one `.storelib` file** with `<template>`, `<style>`, `<script>` and `<schema>` blocks. The template language reads like Liquid (`{{ }}`, `{% if %}`, `{% for %}`, `{% content_for %}`, filters) but is smaller: no `assign`, no `capture`, no `render` or snippets. See [Sections](/developers/sections).\n- **Theme blocks work as they do in Shopify**: a file each in `blocks/`, accepted with `{ \"type\": \"@theme\" }`, drawn with `{% content_for \"blocks\" %}`, nested, static, private with a leading `_`, and preset in the keyed form with `block_order`. See [Blocks](/developers/blocks).\n- **CSS is scoped for you**, and a script runs in a sandbox against a copy of the section. See [Styling](/developers/styling) and [JavaScript](/developers/javascript).\n- **Storelib is for creators**, not merchants with warehouses. There are no collections, no blog and no metaobjects, so the settings that pick those do not exist. What a creator sells is `products`, and what they arrange is sections.\n\n## Setting types\n\nShopify's name, then Storelib's. A schema may use either name where both are listed: `product`, `page` and `text_alignment` are read as `product_picker`, `page_picker` and `alignment`.\n\n| Shopify | Storelib | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `checkbox` | `checkbox`, `toggle` | The same value; `toggle` draws a switch. |\n| `number`, `range`, `radio`, `select` | the same names | A `range` may be `responsive`, with a value per breakpoint. |\n| `text`, `textarea`, `richtext` | the same names | |\n| `inline_richtext` | `inline_richtext` | Bold, italic and links, on one line. |\n| `html` | `html` | Cleaned against an allow list: no scripts, no event attributes, no frames. |\n| `url` | `url` | Every link setting refuses `javascript:` and other schemes. |\n| `video_url` | `video_url` | YouTube and Vimeo; `accept` narrows it. |\n| `video` | `video_picker` | An uploaded video. |\n| `image_picker` | `image_picker` | Also `image_gallery` for several. |\n| `color` | `color` | |\n| `color_background` | `color_background` | A colour or a linear or radial gradient, checked piece by piece. |\n| `color_scheme` | `color_scheme` | The schemes themselves are the theme's, edited in Theme settings. |\n| `font_picker` | `font_picker` | |\n| `text_alignment` | `alignment` | |\n| `link_list` | `link_list` | Menus are made in Theme settings, Menus. `main-menu` is the header's own links. |\n| `page` | `page_picker` | |\n| `product` | `product_picker` | Prints as the product's address and carries its title, price and picture. |\n| `product_list` | `product_list` | Up to `limit` products, shaped like `products`. |\n| `header`, `paragraph` | the same names | Also `divider`. |\n| none | `datetime` | A date, or a date and a time, read as written. Format it with the `date` filter. |\n| none | `focal_point` | Where the important part of a picture is, as `object-position` reads it. Shopify keeps this on the image; Storelib keeps it beside the setting. |\n| none | `icon_picker`, `file_picker`, `community_picker` | An icon, a downloadable file, one of the creator's communities. |\n\nShopify types Storelib does not have are refused with the reason, rather than as an unknown word:\n\n| Shopify | Why not |\n| --- | --- |\n| `collection`, `collection_list` | A creator's shop has no collections. Use `product_list` for chosen products, or `products` for the whole shop. |\n| `blog`, `article`, `article_list` | A Storelib site has no blog of its own yet. |\n| `metaobject`, `metaobject_list` | There are no metaobjects. Repeated content is blocks. |\n| `liquid` | A section's template is its code. Write the markup in `<template>`, or make a section in the code editor. |\n| `color_scheme_group` | Colour schemes belong to the theme and are edited in Theme settings, Colors. A section picks one. |\n\n## Schema keys\n\n| Shopify | Storelib |\n| --- | --- |\n| `name`, `settings`, `blocks`, `max_blocks`, `presets` | the same |\n| a block's `limit` | the same |\n| `{ \"type\": \"@theme\" }`, a theme block by name | the same |\n| a preset's `blocks` keyed by id, `block_order`, `static` | the same |\n| a preset's `category` | the same: where Add section files it |\n| `enabled_on`, `disabled_on` | `allowed_templates`, `disabled_templates` |\n| a setting's `visible_if` | the same, written the same way: `\"{{ section.settings.layout == 'grid' }}\"`. See [Schema](/developers/schema#showing-a-setting-only-when-it-matters). |\n| `tag`, `class` on a section | none: the section's own markup is its wrapper. A theme block has both. |\n| `locales`, `t:` labels | none: see below |\n| none | `type`, which must be the file name, and `schema_version` |\n| none | `migrations`, to rename a setting without losing what creators typed |\n| none | `capabilities`, what the section's script and embeds may reach |\n\n## Templates and pages\n\nEvery site has these pages from the day it is published. The ones marked generated are built from the home page's header and footer and one section until the creator opens them in the builder; from then on, what they saved is what is served.\n\n| Shopify template | Storelib page | `page.template` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `index` | Home page | `index` |\n| `product` | Product page, one layout for every product | `product` |\n| `collection` | Shop, at `/products`: every published product, generated | `page.products` |\n| `search` | Search, at `/search`, generated | `page.search` |\n| `404` | 404 page, generated, drawn inside the site's header and footer for any address the site does not have, with a real 404 status | `page.404` |\n| `page` | About, Contact, and any page the creator makes | `page.<key>` |\n| `cart` | Cart, at `/cart`: the site's header and footer around the visitor's cart. Not arranged with sections, because a cart is one visitor's list. | none |\n| `customers/account`, `customers/login` | Account at `/account` and Sign in at `/account/login`, generated | `page.account`, `page.login` |\n| none | Wishlist at `/wishlist`, generated | `page.wishlist` |\n| `page.contact` | Contact at `/contact`, generated when the creator has not made one | `page.contact` |\n| policy pages | Refund, privacy and terms pages, from the creator's own policies | `page.<key>` |\n| `robots.txt.liquid`, sitemap | Each site answers its own `robots.txt` and `sitemap.xml` | none |\n| `blog`, `article`, `list-collections`, `gift_card`, `password` | none. There is no blog, no collection list and no gift card. A site that is not ready is left unpublished, and visitors see that it is not published rather than a password form. | |\n\nA header and a footer are the same on every page, like Shopify's header and footer groups: the builder keeps one of each and the site draws it on every page. The announcement bar is a theme setting above the header, with several messages that take turns.\n\n## Sections\n\nThe library's sections against Dawn's:\n\n| Dawn | Storelib |\n| --- | --- |\n| Image banner, Slideshow | Centered Hero, Split Hero, Product Hero. A slideshow is not offered: it hides all but one message behind a timer. Older pages that used one still draw it. |\n| Featured collection | Product Grid: cards you fill in, products you choose, or the whole shop, newest first |\n| Featured product | Featured Products, Product Spotlight |\n| Multicolumn | Feature Grid, Feature Cards, Two Column Content |\n| Multirow | Rows |\n| Image with text | Image + Text, Image + Features |\n| Rich text | Rich Text |\n| Collage | Image Collage |\n| Video | Video Section |\n| Collapsible content | Accordion FAQ, Two Column FAQ, FAQ + CTA |\n| Email signup, Newsletter | Newsletter Signup. It collects for the creator with nothing to set up. |\n| Custom Liquid | A section of your own, written in the code editor or by an agent |\n| Contact form | Contact form, on the contact page and addable anywhere |\n| Main account, Main login | The account and sign in pages' own sections |\n| Main 404 | Page not found, on the 404 page |\n| Main search | Search results, on the search page |\n| none | Plans, for a course, a membership or a service sold more than one way |\n| none | Course Outline, the modules of a course and the lessons in each |\n| none | Testimonials, logos, countdown, announcement, founder story and timeline sections |\n| Featured blog, Collection list, Apps, Pickup availability, Quick order list | none: no blog, no collections, no app blocks, nothing to pick up and no wholesale orders |\n\n## Dynamic sources\n\nShopify connects a setting to a metafield. Storelib has no metafields, so the list of what a setting can be connected to is closed and small: the shop, the creator, the product on a product page, the page, the creator's social links and the year. A creator types or inserts `{{ shop.name }}` into a setting, and the page fills it in on the server. See [Schema](/developers/schema#shop-data-in-a-setting) for the list.\n\nA template reads the same facts directly: `shop`, `creator` and `product`, beside `settings` and `blocks`. Shopify's other globals have their counterparts too: `routes` for the addresses every site answers on, and `customer` and `wishlist` on the pages drawn for one visitor.\n\n## Not in Storelib\n\n| Shopify | Why |\n| --- | --- |\n| Snippets and `{% render %}` | A section is self-contained so that it can be moved, exported and checked on its own. Shared styles live in the theme's tokens and colour schemes. |\n| `assign`, `capture` | The language has no variables. `default` covers most of what they are used for, and `products` is shaped like `blocks` so one loop body serves both. |\n| App blocks | There is no app store to provide them. A section's `embed` capability covers a player or a booking widget. |\n| Alternate templates, such as `product.special` | Every product uses the one product page. |\n| Translations and `locales/` | Sites are drawn in one language, the one the creator writes in. |\n| The section rendering API | A section is drawn on the server with its page. Its script changes the markup it was drawn with and does not fetch new markup. |\n",
  "styling": "# Styling\n\nA section's `<style>` applies to that section's instances and nothing else. Two sections that both declare `.title` do not fight, and one section cannot restyle the page around it. You do not write the scope and you cannot escape it: the compiler adds it.\n\n## What scoping does\n\nGiven this in a section of type `hero`:\n\n```css\n.hero { padding: 64px 0; }\nh1 { font-size: 48px; }\n:root { --gap: 16px; }\n@keyframes rise { from { translate: 0 8px; opacity: 0 } to { translate: 0; opacity: 1 } }\n.hero h1 { animation: rise 400ms ease-out; }\n```\n\nthe compiler emits\n\n```css\n[data-section-id=\"sec_9k2m\"] .hero { padding: 64px 0; }\n[data-section-id=\"sec_9k2m\"] h1 { font-size: 48px; }\n[data-section-id=\"sec_9k2m\"] { --gap: 16px; }\n@keyframes sl-hero-rise { … }\n[data-section-id=\"sec_9k2m\"] .hero h1 { animation: sl-hero-rise 400ms ease-out; }\n```\n\n- Every selector is prefixed with the instance, inside `@media`, `@supports` and `@container` too.\n- `html`, `body` and `:root` are rewritten to mean the section's root. A rule written against them does not do what it looks like it does, so do not write them.\n- `@keyframes` names are prefixed with the section type, and every `animation` that uses one is rewritten to match.\n- `@import` is refused. A stylesheet cannot fetch anything.\n- A `<style>`, `<link>`, `<base>` or `<meta>` tag inside `<template>` is refused on save. Each acts on the whole page, not on the section; a section's CSS goes in its `<style>` block.\n- There is no stylesheet for the whole site that you can write yet. Resets and the fonts are the theme's own, and a font is chosen in Theme › Typography rather than loaded by a section.\n\n## The page a section sees\n\nIn the builder, a section is drawn in a canvas inside the editor. The canvas is the section's window:\n\n- `position: fixed` is fixed to the canvas. A full-screen menu covers the page it is on, like a phone screen, and never the editor.\n- `100vh` and `100vw` are rewritten to the canvas's height and width. On a live site they are the window's.\n- A `@media` query that asks only about width is rewritten to the same `@container` query, so the phone preview draws the phone layout. A query about anything else, such as motion or hover, stays a media query.\n- A `popover`, or a `<dialog>` opened with `command=\"show-modal\"`, opens inside the canvas rather than above the whole screen. `:popover-open` and `:modal` still match it.\n\nNothing a section draws can reach outside the canvas, whatever its `z-index`.\n\nA section clips sideways and not downwards, so a dropdown can hang below a header. The section someone is pointing at, typing in, or has a menu open in (a pop-up, or a button with `aria-expanded=\"true\"`) is lifted above the sections after it, so the dropdown opens over them. Set `aria-expanded` on a menu's button when a script opens it.\n\n## Settings in CSS\n\nA rule can read a setting with the same `{{ }}` the template uses. The value is made safe for a stylesheet: braces and angle brackets are removed, because no legitimate CSS value holds one.\n\n```css\n.hero { padding-top: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; }\n```\n\nFor anything that differs per instance, prefer a custom property set in the markup and a static rule that reads it. The stylesheet is then identical for every instance and cached once:\n\n```storelib\n<section class=\"hero\" style=\"--hero-pt: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; --hero-pb: {{ settings.padding_bottom }}px\">\n```\n\n```css\n.hero { padding: var(--hero-pt) 24px var(--hero-pb); }\n```\n\n**Padding and spacing are settings.** Declare them as `range` settings with `\"unit\": \"px\"`, never as fixed numbers in the CSS, so a creator can tighten or loosen a section without asking for new code.\n\n## Responsive\n\n**Use container queries, not media queries.** A section is as wide as the column it sits in, and in the builder's phone preview that is not the width of the window. A width-only `@media` query is rewritten to a container query for you, so the preview is right, but a container query on your own element says what you mean and can ask about the element rather than the page.\n\nPut `container-type: inline-size` on the outermost element, write the phone layout as the base rules, then widen:\n\n```css\n.grid { container-type: inline-size; }\n.grid__items { display: grid; gap: 16px; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }\n\n@container (min-width: 640px) {\n  .grid__items { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }\n}\n@container (min-width: 1024px) {\n  .grid__items { grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols, 3), minmax(0, 1fr)); }\n}\n```\n\nA section must never scroll sideways. `minmax(0, 1fr)` instead of `1fr`, `min-width: 0` on flex children, and `overflow-wrap: anywhere` on long headings are what stop it.\n\nThe one place `@media` is right is **the visitor's own motion setting**, which is about them rather than about width:\n\n```css\n.card { transition: transform 200ms ease; }\n.card:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .card { transition: none; }\n  .card:hover { transform: none; }\n}\n```\n\n## Colour schemes\n\nA section does not choose colours. It chooses a **colour scheme**, and the creator decides what the scheme looks like in Theme settings. Change a scheme and every section on it changes, at once, in the builder and on the live site.\n\nDeclare a `color_scheme` setting, then read the scheme. In CSS, read the custom properties the renderer sets on the section's root. In the template, read `scheme.<key>`.\n\n@[scheme-vars]\n\n```css\n.card { background: var(--scheme-background-secondary); border: 1px solid var(--scheme-border); }\n.card__title { color: var(--scheme-heading); }\n.card__body { color: var(--scheme-text); }\n.button { background: var(--scheme-button-primary); color: var(--scheme-button-primary-text); }\n```\n\n### Buttons, and their hover\n\nA button reads four colours from its scheme: its fill and text, and its fill and text on hover. The two hover colours are automatic until the creator picks them, worked out from the button's own colours, so a creator who recolours a button never has to recolour its hover too. Paint hover from the scheme, not with `opacity` or a colour of your own:\n\n```css\n.s__btn--primary { background: var(--scheme-button-primary); color: var(--scheme-button-primary-text); border: 1px solid var(--scheme-button-primary-border); }\n.s__btn--primary:hover { background: var(--scheme-button-primary-hover); color: var(--scheme-button-primary-hover-text); }\n.s__btn--secondary { background: var(--scheme-button-secondary); color: var(--scheme-button-secondary-text); border: 1px solid var(--scheme-button-secondary-border); }\n.s__btn--secondary:hover { background: var(--scheme-button-secondary-hover); color: var(--scheme-button-secondary-hover-text); }\n```\n\nIn a template the same four are `scheme.primary_btn_hover_bg`, `scheme.primary_btn_hover_text`, `scheme.secondary_btn_hover_bg` and `scheme.secondary_btn_hover_text`.\n\nGive every button the class `s__btn`, or `data-button` when it has to be something else. That is what puts it under the theme's **Buttons** settings: its shape, corner radius, weight, shadow and hover motion. A link that is not a button gets neither, and stays a link.\n\nFive schemes are built in. Their keys are `white`, `light`, `dark`, `black` and `highlight`, shown to the creator as Light, Soft, Dark, Brand and Accent; a creator can rename them and add more. Renaming changes the name, never the key.\n\n**Never hard-code a hex value the scheme already answers.** A hard-coded colour stops responding to the creator's theme, and on a dark scheme it is usually unreadable. Before publishing a new section, switch it between a light and a dark scheme in the builder and read it on both. The builder's AI reviews contrast in what it writes; the code editor does not do it for you.\n\n### A section's own colour overrides\n\nBehind **Advanced colors** in the builder, a creator can override a few of a section's colours without leaving the scheme. The override is applied on top of the scheme by the renderer, so your CSS keeps reading the same variables. Say which slots your section allows:\n\n```json\n\"supports_color_overrides\": [\"background\", \"heading\", \"text\"]\n```\n\n`true` offers every slot, `false` offers none. When the schema has a `color_scheme` setting and says nothing, every slot is offered.\n\n### Colours of the section's own\n\nSome colours are not the scheme's at all: the stars on a review, the dot on a timeline, the scrim over a photo, a badge. Declare them as `color` settings. Each one is a row in the section's panel, under its settings, and the colour the creator picks there is for this section alone: it beats the scheme and Advanced colors, and nothing else on the site changes.\n\nLet it **follow the scheme until the creator picks one** by naming a scheme colour as its default:\n\n```json\n{ \"type\": \"color\", \"id\": \"star_color\", \"label\": \"Stars\", \"default\": \"scheme.accent\" }\n```\n\n```css\n.review__stars { color: {{ settings.star_color }}; }\n```\n\nUntouched, `settings.star_color` is this section's accent: it changes with the scheme, with Advanced colors and with the theme. The row says **Scheme** and shows that colour, greyed. Once the creator picks a colour the setting holds its hex, and the reset arrow beside it puts it back to following the scheme. Any name `scheme.<key>` has works: the names in the table above and the older ones (`scheme.bg`, `scheme.primary_btn_bg`).\n\nA plain hex default works too, and an empty default (`\"\"`) means the section works the colour out itself, which the row calls **Auto**: write it as `{{ settings.star_color | default: scheme.accent }}`. The older colour settings the overrides already cover (`bg_color`, `text_color`, `button_color` and the rest) are not shown twice: Advanced colors edits them under their new names.\n\n## Type\n\nThe theme has three font roles and nine text styles, each with its own size at desktop, tablet and phone width, weight, line height, letter spacing and case.\n\n@[type-styles]\n\nA section chooses a heading style and a body style, and the theme turns the choice into custom properties on the section root. Read them, with a fallback for when nothing was chosen:\n\n```css\n.hero__title {\n  font-family: var(--theme-font-heading), ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;\n  font-size: var(--section-heading-size, 32px);\n  line-height: var(--section-heading-line-height, 1.15);\n  font-weight: var(--section-heading-weight, 700);\n  letter-spacing: var(--section-heading-tracking, -0.01em);\n  text-transform: var(--section-heading-transform, none);\n}\n.hero__lead {\n  font-size: var(--section-text-size, 17px);\n  line-height: var(--section-text-line-height, 1.6);\n}\n```\n\nName a font family inside a section only as a fallback after the theme's variable. The tablet and phone sizes take over inside the section's own container, so the builder's phone preview draws phone sizes.\n\nSet `\"supports_typography_overrides\": false` on a section that has its own size control, such as a hero with a heading size slider, so the builder does not offer two controls for one thing.\n\n## Layout conventions\n\nThese are what the library's sections do, and what makes a new section sit beside them without looking out of place:\n\n```css\n.s__in {\n  margin: 0 auto;\n  max-width: var(--theme-page-width, 1280px);\n  padding-inline: var(--theme-padding-x, 24px);\n}\n```\n\n- Page width and side padding come from the theme, through `--theme-page-width` and `--theme-padding-x`.\n- Corner radius comes from the theme's tokens: `{{ theme.radius.medium }}px`.\n- Vertical rhythm is the section's own `padding_top` and `padding_bottom` range settings.\n",
  "themes": "# Themes\n\nOn Storelib, a website is a theme: one copy of a theme with its own settings, its own pages and its own section files. The website library in your dashboard lists them, and the builder edits one at a time. This page is what a theme holds, where each part is stored, and the file format a theme travels in.\n\n## What a theme holds\n\n| Part | What it is | Stored as |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Theme settings | Colour schemes, fonts and text styles, buttons, layout, animations, the announcement bar, social links, SEO and the cookie banner. One JSON object per website. | A draft and a live copy. See [Data model](/developers/data-model#storethemesettings). |\n| Pages | Each page's sections in order, with their settings and blocks. | A draft and a live copy per page. See [Pages and page settings](/developers/pages). |\n| Section files | The `.storelib` source of every section type the website can draw. | The theme's own 33 files ship with Storelib. A row is stored for each file you save or create, with a draft and a live copy. |\n| Media | Pictures, videos and files the settings point at. | In your account's storage. A setting holds the file's public URL. |\n\nChange a section file and every page that uses the section changes how it looks, with nobody's content moved. Change a page and one page's content changes. Change a theme setting and every section that reads it follows. See [How the builder works](/developers/builder) for the whole map.\n\n## The website library\n\nThe library holds up to five websites: **one live and four drafts**. Add theme, Duplicate and Import each refuse a sixth, checked on the server, and the library shows which websites are taking the room.\n\n- **Add theme** makes a new draft from a theme Storelib ships. Browse themes offers one today, Storelib Default. A new website starts with its home page and its product page; the policy pages are added the first time it opens in the builder.\n- **Duplicate** copies a website as it stands, drafts included. See [Sharing themes](/developers/sharing-themes#duplicate).\n- **Import theme** makes a new draft from a `.storelib-theme.zip` file. See [Sharing themes](/developers/sharing-themes#import).\n- **Publish** puts one website live. The website that was live is taken offline in the same step; only one is ever live.\n- **Take offline** takes the live website down without deleting anything. **Remove** deletes a draft: its pages, settings and code. The live website has to be taken offline before it can be removed.\n\nEach website's menu in the library also has **Edit code**, **Rename** and **Export theme**, and a draft's has **Website settings**; the live website's are beside Edit website.\n\n## Theme settings\n\nEvery theme setting lives in one object, saved as a draft while you work and copied to the live object when you publish. The builder's Theme panel shows them in groups, and [Using the website builder](/developers/website-builder#theme-settings) draws every one with its default. By group, with the id each is stored under:\n\n@[theme-setting-index]\n\nA few keys are written by panels of their own rather than listed in a group:\n\n| Key | Holds | Written by |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `color_schemes` | Each colour scheme's colours, by scheme key. | Theme › Colors |\n| `default_color_scheme` | The scheme a section gets when it names none, or names one that no longer exists. | Theme › Colors |\n| `color_scheme_remaps` | Where the sections on a deleted scheme went, so a page that was not open at the time follows too. | Deleting a scheme |\n| `typography_styles` | Each text style, only what differs from its default. | Theme › Typography |\n| `page_seo` | Each page's own search title, description and whether search engines may list it, by page key. | Website settings › Pages & Navigation |\n| `site_language` | The language the site is written in, for the page's `lang`. | Website settings › General |\n\nA template reads theme settings through `theme`: the tokens under `theme.colors`, `theme.typography`, `theme.radius` and `theme.spacing`, and every setting as stored under `theme.settings`. The full list, with defaults, is in [Dynamic values](/developers/dynamic-values#theme).\n\n## Colour schemes\n\nA section does not choose colours. It chooses a **colour scheme**, and the scheme decides every colour it paints: background, text, headings, links, borders, two buttons with their hover colours, and inputs. Change a scheme and every section on it changes, in the builder and on the live site.\n\n- **Five are built in**, with the keys `white`, `light`, `dark`, `black` and `highlight`, shown as Light, Soft, Dark, Brand and Accent. The keys are older than the names and never change, because every saved section stores the key. Renaming a scheme changes its name only.\n- **Each has 21 colours** in five groups (surface, text, primary button, secondary button, inputs), a name, and an optional gradient laid over the background: a `linear-gradient`, `radial-gradient` or `conic-gradient`, with no `url()` in it.\n- **Hover colours are automatic** until someone sets them. A filled button's hover is its background a step darker, or a step lighter when it is already near black. An outlined button's hover is its background, or the section's when the button is transparent, moved slightly towards its text. A hover colour is stored only when it was chosen.\n- **A creator can add schemes**, with a key of letters, digits, `_` and `-`, and delete the ones they added. The sections on a deleted scheme move to another one.\n- **A section stores its scheme's key** as the setting `color_scheme`, and any colours it overrides as `color_overrides`. A key the theme no longer has falls back to where the deleted scheme's sections went, then to `default_color_scheme`, then to `white`.\n\nHow a section reads its scheme, in a template and in CSS, is in [Styling](/developers/styling#colour-schemes).\n\n## Typography\n\nA theme has three fonts and nine text styles.\n\n@[font-options]\n\nEach text style has a font role (heading, body or accent), a weight, a size for desktop, tablet and phone, a line height, a letter spacing and a case. The tablet size applies when the section itself is 1023px wide or less, and the phone size at 767px or less, measured on the section rather than the window, so the builder's phone preview draws phone sizes.\n\n@[builder-type-styles]\n\nA section picks its heading style (Heading 1 to 4, or Custom) and its body style (Body, Body large, Body small, or Custom) in the builder. Without a choice, its heading is Heading 2 and its body is Body. How a section's CSS reads the choice is in [Styling](/developers/styling#type).\n\n## The package format\n\nA theme travels as one zip file, `<name>.storelib-theme.zip`. This is what an export writes:\n\n```text\nmy-shop.storelib-theme.zip\n├── theme.json                  what the theme is: name, version, the engine it needs\n├── manifest.json               the SHA-256 and size of every file in the package\n├── config/\n│   └── settings.json           the theme settings, media as asset:// references\n├── templates/\n│   ├── home.json               one file per page, named by its page key\n│   ├── product.json\n│   ├── about.json\n│   └── refund-policy.json\n├── sections/\n│   ├── hero_centered.storelib  all of the theme's section files, your edits in place\n│   └── pricing_table.storelib  and every section file of your own\n├── blocks/\n│   ├── heading.storelib        the theme blocks, the same way\n│   └── group.storelib\n└── assets/\n    └── 3f2a…c9.webp            media, named by the SHA-256 of its bytes\n```\n\nA package may hold files under `config`, `layout`, `templates`, `sections`, `blocks`, `snippets`, `assets` and `locales`, and `theme.json` and `manifest.json` at its root. Nothing reads `layout`, `snippets` or `locales` yet: an import keeps a `.storelib` file from them and nothing draws it.\n\n### theme.json\n\n```json\n{\n  \"format\": \"storelib-theme\",\n  \"format_version\": 1,\n  \"name\": \"My shop\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"engine\": { \"min\": 1 },\n  \"source_theme_id\": \"a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890\",\n  \"cover\": { \"desktop\": \"5d1e…\", \"phone\": \"9b0a…\" }\n}\n```\n\n| Key | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `format` | Always `storelib-theme`. |\n| `format_version` | The package format, 1. |\n| `name` | Required. The imported website is given this name. |\n| `version` | The theme's own version. An export writes `1.0.0`. |\n| `engine.min` | The oldest engine that can render the theme. A package asking for a newer engine than this one is refused. |\n| `source_theme_id` | The catalogue theme it was made from. An import keeps it when Storelib's catalogue has that theme, and uses Storelib Default when it does not. |\n| `cover` | The SHA-256 of the website's two cover pictures in `assets/`, for the library card. |\n| `author`, `description` | Optional. |\n\nOther keys are allowed, and an import ignores them.\n\n### manifest.json\n\nEvery file in the package except the manifest itself is listed with its SHA-256 and its size in bytes, and every asset with its file name, type and size. An import recomputes each hash and refuses the package if one does not match, which is why a package edited by hand needs its manifest updated. [Sharing themes](/developers/sharing-themes#editing-a-package-by-hand) has a script that does it.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"format\": \"storelib-theme\",\n  \"format_version\": 1,\n  \"files\": {\n    \"theme.json\": { \"sha256\": \"b61f…\", \"bytes\": 212 },\n    \"sections/faq_accordion.storelib\": { \"sha256\": \"0c9d…\", \"bytes\": 9038 }\n  },\n  \"assets\": {\n    \"3f2a…c9\": { \"filename\": \"hero.webp\", \"mime_type\": \"image/webp\", \"size\": 182340 }\n  },\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-09-25T10:04:11.000Z\"\n}\n```\n\n### templates/*.json\n\nOne file per page, named by the page key: `templates/home.json`, `templates/about.json`, `templates/size-guide.json`. There is no `index.json`. Sections are keyed by their id, with `order` saying the order, and each section's blocks are keyed the same way with a `block_order` beside them:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sections\": {\n    \"faq_accordion-1727200000000\": {\n      \"type\": \"faq_accordion\",\n      \"name\": \"Accordion FAQ\",\n      \"settings\": { \"heading\": \"Questions\", \"color_scheme\": \"light\" },\n      \"hidden\": false,\n      \"blocks\": {\n        \"block-m1x2-1-ab3cd\": { \"type\": \"qa\", \"settings\": { \"question\": \"Do you ship abroad?\" } }\n      },\n      \"block_order\": [\"block-m1x2-1-ab3cd\"]\n    }\n  },\n  \"order\": [\"faq_accordion-1727200000000\"]\n}\n```\n\nIn the database the same page is a plain list, and blocks a list inside each section. An import converts one to the other. See [Pages and page settings](/developers/pages#what-a-page-stores).\n\n### config/settings.json\n\nThe theme settings object, exactly as the builder stores it, with every media URL that travelled replaced by `asset://` and the SHA-256 of the file. An import stores the file in the importing account and writes its new URL back in place of the reference.\n\n## Versions\n\n@[versions]\n\n## What the site reads, and what it does not\n\nA visitor's page is drawn from three things: the website's live pages, its live theme settings, and its section files, which are the theme's own 33 with the website's changed and added files in their place. The code editor shows more files than that, laid out the way a Shopify theme is: `theme.json`, `config/settings_schema.json`, `config/settings_data.json`, `templates/*.json`, two files under `snippets` and `assets/styles.css` and `assets/theme.js`. Those are views of the theme's data. Saving an edit to one stores the file, and **nothing on the site reads it**. Only `sections/*.storelib` and `blocks/*.storelib` change what a page draws.\n\nThere is no stylesheet for the whole site that you can write yet. A section's CSS is always scoped to that section; see [Styling](/developers/styling).\n",
  "website-builder": "# Using the website builder\n\nEvery part of the Storelib website builder: what each button, panel and setting is, what it changes, and where it lives. The settings on this page are drawn from the same lists the builder reads, with their defaults, so what you see here is what you see in the editor.\n\nThe builder opens from **Websites** in your dashboard. Pick a website and press **Edit website**. It edits one website at a time, and everything you change is a draft until you publish.\n\n## The screen\n\n| Part | Where | What it is for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Top bar | Across the top | Leaving, the website's menu, Preview, Code or Website Settings, the page you are on, desktop or phone, undo, the inspector, connections, Ask AI, and Publish. |\n| Sidebar | Left, on a computer. A sheet at the bottom, on a phone | Three tabs: **Sections** of the page, the site's **Pages**, and the **Theme** settings. A selected section's settings open here. |\n| Canvas | The middle | The page, drawn by the same renderer your visitors get. Click a section to edit it. |\n| Ask AI | Between the sidebar and the canvas, when open | Describe a change, or a new section, and it is made. |\n\nA window narrower than 768px is treated as a phone: the canvas shows the phone layout, the sidebar becomes a sheet you drag up from the bottom with the same three tabs, and the menu holds what the bar has no room for. On a computer the sidebar can be dragged wider, from 240 to 560px, and a double click on its edge puts it back.\n\n## The top bar\n\nFrom left to right, on a computer:\n\n| Control | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| Exit, the arrow | Back to your websites. If a save is still going or a code file is unsaved, it asks first: **Stay here** or **Leave anyway**. |\n| The website's name | A green dot beside it means it is the live website. |\n| **…** | View code, Theme settings, Website settings, Connect Claude Code or Codex, Undo, Redo, View live site, and Back to Dashboard. On a phone it also opens the connection. |\n| Preview, Code, Website Settings | How you are looking at the website: the canvas, the [code editor](#the-code-editor), or [Website settings](#website-settings). On a phone these are in the **…** menu. |\n| Desktop or phone | One button that switches the canvas between the full width and a 390px phone. |\n| Page switcher | Which page you are editing. See [Pages](#pages). |\n| Open the live page | The page you are on, on the live site, in a new tab. |\n| Undo and Redo | See [Undo and redo](#undo-and-redo). On a phone they sit beside the menu. |\n| Inspector | On: click a section in the canvas to select it. Off: the canvas is a plain preview you can click through. |\n| Connection | The mark of what is connected, Claude, OpenAI or GitHub, with a green dot while it is live. It opens Website settings on Connections, and a change a connected agent makes is announced under it. |\n| Ask AI | Opens and closes the AI panel. A dot on it means it is still working with the panel closed. |\n| Publish, or Save | **Publish** on the live website, with a bright dot when the draft is ahead of it; it opens the [Publish panel](#publishing). **Save** on a website that is not live, which saves the draft. |\n\n## Sections, Pages and Theme\n\nThe three tabs at the top of the sidebar, and at the top of the sheet on a phone.\n\n### Sections\n\nThe page's sections, in three groups, the way a shop's theme editor shows them:\n\n- **Header** holds the site's header and nothing else, with **Add section** under it, which adds at the top of the page.\n- **Template** holds the page's own sections, top to bottom, with **Add section** at the end, which adds at the bottom. A thin line between two rows shows a **+** under the pointer: press it to add a section at exactly that point.\n- **Footer** holds the site's footer.\n\nEach row is one line: a grip to drag it (press and hold on a phone, or the arrow keys), its name, and under the pointer **Hide**, **Delete** and a **…** menu with **Duplicate**, **Move up**, **Move down**, **Hide** or **Show**, and **Delete**. A right click on the row opens the same menu. The header and footer have an icon in place of the grip and only the eye: they are the same on every page, so they can be hidden but not moved, copied or deleted, and nothing can be dropped into their groups. A hidden section is faded in the list, always shows its eye, and is left out of the page. Delete takes effect at once; Undo brings it back. See [The header and footer](/developers/header-and-footer).\n\n### Adding a section\n\n**Add section** opens the library: a search box, the categories down the side, and one card per row, each section drawn with its real content. A section with several presets has a card for each. Sections you or Ask AI coded are under **Created**. Pick one and it is placed, selected and scrolled to; on a phone its settings come up with it.\n\nThe library's categories are Hero, Products, Features, Content, Social Proof, CTA, Media, About, FAQ and Marketing. A section is only in the library when its file declares a preset; see [Presets](/developers/blocks#presets). A preset may file its card under another category with `category`.\n\n### Pages\n\n**Pages** lists the website's pages: Home, the product page, About, Contact, Shop and Search, the pages you made, and a product page for each of your products. The page switcher in the top bar does the same with a search box, a **Yours** group for the pages you made, a **Products** group, and **Create page**, which asks for a name and makes a page at an address taken from it. A new page starts with the header and footer and nothing between. A page you made can be deleted from the switcher; that takes it off the live site at once.\n\nEvery website comes with a Refund Policy, a Privacy Policy and Terms of Service page. Edit them like any other page before you publish. Keys, addresses and each page's search settings are in [Pages and page settings](/developers/pages).\n\n## A section's settings\n\nClick a section, in the canvas or the list, and its settings open in the sidebar.\n\n- **Colour scheme** comes first: pick one of the website's schemes and every colour in the section follows it. **Advanced colors** overrides single colours (background, text, heading, accent, border, and each button's fill, text, hover fill and hover text) for this section only. A hover colour says Auto until you pick one. **Reset all to scheme** undoes that.\n- **The section's own colours**, when its file declares any (stars, a timeline's dot, the scrim over a photo), are rows among its settings. Each says Scheme, Default or Auto until you pick a colour for this section alone, and the arrow beside it puts it back. See [Styling](/developers/styling#colours-of-the-sections-own).\n- **Text** picks the heading style and the body style from the theme's text styles, or **Custom** to set font, weight, size per screen, line height, letter spacing and case for this section.\n- **The section's own settings** follow, in the order its file declares them, each with its help text. A setting marked responsive has a Desktop, Tablet, Phone switch above it.\n- **Blocks**, if it has any. Each block has a grip to drag it up or down with a mouse or a finger, or with the arrow keys. It can also be duplicated, deleted, and opened to edit its own settings. **Add** adds another, until the section's `limit` for that kind of block or its `max_blocks`. Clicking a block in the canvas opens it in the list.\n- **Hide**, **Duplicate** and **Delete** are pinned along the bottom.\n\nA section's file decides which of these it offers; see [Schema](/developers/schema#top-level). With the inspector on, a selected section also shows a small toolbar at its top right: Ask AI, Move up, Move down, Duplicate, Hide and Delete.\n\n### Every control a section can have\n\nA section declares its settings in its schema, and each type is drawn as one control. This is every type, drawn as the builder draws it. To write one, see [Schema](/developers/schema).\n\n@[builder-controls]\n\n### The header's own panel\n\nThe header has a panel of its own, in three parts.\n\n- **Store Logo**: the logo, its height, a nudge left or right, a separate logo for phones if you want one, and the logo's height on phones, which applies to whichever logo the phone shows.\n- **Navigation Links**: up to eight links, each with a label, where it goes, and whether it opens in a new tab. Drag to reorder. Each link can hold one level of sub-links. Once the site has menus of its own, **Links** at the top can show one of them instead; the links here are kept for when you switch back. The same links and menus are in Website settings › Navigation.\n- **Header Settings**: the layout on a computer (logo left, nav inline, nav centred, or logo centred) and on a phone; **Menu on phones**, which slides in from the left or the right or covers the screen; the colour scheme; how menu links react to the pointer; width and spacing; sticky behaviour (always, or only when scrolling up); a transparent header, on every page or on the home page only; a bottom border and shadow; which icons show (search, account, wishlist, cart); and an optional button with its own text and link.\n\nThe header and the announcement bar are the same on every page. The announcement bar is set in General Settings.\n\n## Theme settings\n\nThe settings that belong to the whole website, under the **Theme** tab of the sidebar, where the panel is called General Settings. **Theme settings** in the **…** menu opens it too. How they are stored is in [Themes](/developers/themes#theme-settings).\n\n### Colors\n\nEvery section picks one **colour scheme**. Change a scheme here and every section using it changes with it. A website starts with five:\n\n@[builder-schemes]\n\nPick a scheme to edit it: its name, whether new sections start on it, and its twenty-one colours in five groups (surface, text, primary button, secondary button, inputs). Each button has a colour on hover, worked out from the button until you set one. If a pair of colours is hard to read, it says so and by how much. A scheme can also lay a gradient over its background. **Add scheme** makes another; a scheme you made can be deleted, and the sections on it move to another.\n\n**Page colors**, under Colors, are the colours of the page itself, behind and between sections:\n\n@[theme-settings:colors]\n\n### Branding\n\nThe store's name, logo and its height, an optional smaller logo for phones, the favicon and a tagline. The logo is kept in step with the header's Store Logo: change it in either place.\n\n@[theme-settings:branding]\n\n### Typography\n\nThree fonts, and nine text styles every section uses unless it sets its own.\n\n@[theme-settings:typography]\n\nThe text styles, with their defaults. Each can be changed: font, weight, a size for desktop, tablet and phone, line height, letter spacing and case. A dot marks a style you changed, and **Reset style** puts it back.\n\n@[builder-type-styles]\n\n### Buttons\n\n@[theme-settings:buttons]\n\n### Layout\n\n@[theme-settings:layout]\n\n### Animations\n\n@[theme-settings:animations]\n\n### Announcement bar\n\nA bar above the header on every page. With more than one message, the messages take turns, with arrows to move between them. The bar stops while the pointer or the keyboard is on it and does not move by itself for a visitor whose device asks for less motion.\n\n@[theme-settings:announcement]\n\n### Menus\n\nNamed lists of links that a section can show: a footer column, a second navigation, a list of courses. A link can have links under it. The header's own links are always there as **Main menu**, so a section that shows the main menu shows the header's navigation without it being typed twice.\n\n@[theme-settings:menus]\n\n### Social links\n\n@[theme-settings:social]\n\n### Shipping\n\n@[theme-settings:cart]\n\n### SEO\n\n@[theme-settings:seo]\n\n### Cookie and privacy\n\n@[theme-settings:cookies]\n\n## The canvas\n\nThe canvas is your page, drawn the way a visitor sees it, by the same renderer.\n\n**With the inspector on**, a section shows a faint ring under the pointer and a solid one when selected. Clicking selects a section, or a block inside it. Clicking into a form field lets you type in it rather than selecting the section. Hovering the gap between two sections shows **Add a section here**.\n\n**With the inspector off**, the canvas is a plain preview: links inside the website open that page in the builder.\n\nEither way, the builder never leaves itself:\n\n- A link to a page of the website opens that page in the builder. A link to a page that does not exist yet offers **Create this page**.\n- Links to other websites, email links and phone links do nothing.\n- Forms do not submit, and nothing can be bought. A sign-up box in the canvas does not subscribe anyone.\n\nThe phone preview is 390px wide, the width of the most common phones. A section's layout answers to its own width, so the phone preview draws the phone layout exactly as a phone would.\n\nA section that fails to draw says so in its place, in red, naming the file and the line, with the rest of the page unaffected. On the live site the same section is left out. See [Publishing and checks](/developers/publishing#when-a-visitor-opens-a-page).\n\n## Undo and redo\n\n**⌘Z** or **Ctrl+Z** undoes, and **⌘⇧Z**, **⌘Y** or **Ctrl+Y** redoes, as do the two arrows in the top bar.\n\n- The last 30 changes can be undone: moving, hiding, duplicating and deleting sections and blocks, settings, theme settings and what Ask AI changed on the page.\n- A run of typing in one box is one step.\n- Changing page starts a fresh history, so Undo never reaches into a page you are not looking at.\n- Undo does not reach a section file saved in the code editor, which has its own [History](#the-code-editor), or a page you deleted.\n\n## Saving and publishing\n\n- **The draft saves itself**, a second and a half after your last change: the theme settings, then the page you are on, then the header and footer copied onto every page. There is no save button to remember while editing. A save that fails says so.\n- **The live website does not change until you publish.** Visitors see the published version and nothing else.\n- On a website that is not live, the top bar's button is **Save**. It is published from Website settings › Publishing or from its menu in the library.\n\n### Publishing\n\nOn the live website, **Publish** opens the Publish panel: the website's address, who can see it, when it was last published, **Preview**, and **Publish changes**. Publishing puts every page's draft, the theme settings and the saved section files live at once, and keeps a backup. Every step is in [Publishing and checks](/developers/publishing#what-publish-does).\n\n**Preview** makes a private link that shows the drafts to anyone you send it to, and the panel then shows the link with a copy button and **Turn off**. See [Preview links](/developers/sharing-themes#preview-links).\n\n### Backups\n\nWebsite settings › **Backups & Versions** lists the website's backups. One is kept every time you publish, every time Ask AI finishes a task, and before a connected agent changes a page. **Back up now** makes one; **Restore** puts one back as the draft, after backing up the draft it replaces. The newest 30 are kept. A backup holds the theme settings and every page; section files keep their own history in the code editor.\n\n## Ask AI\n\nAsk AI has two modes.\n\n- **Change this page**: describe a change and it is made with the same sections, blocks and settings the panels use. It is one step of Undo, and nothing is saved until the builder saves. Select a section first and it can be limited to that one.\n- **Code a section**: describe a section the library does not have. It is written as a section file, compiled, and only kept if it compiles and passes the same checks as any agent's file; a file that fails is sent back to be fixed, up to three times. While it is being written, the panel says what it is doing (Starting, Thinking, Writing the section, Checking it, Fixing a detail), the spot it will fill shows code being typed, and the finished section takes its place. If it still cannot get it right, it says so in plain words with **Try again**, never with the compiler's report.\n\nBoth modes answer rather than guess. A greeting, a question, or a request too unclear to act on gets a short reply saying what it understood and what to ask for, and nothing on the page changes.\n\nYou can attach up to four pictures (PNG, JPEG, WebP or GIF) of something you want built. Pressing Enter while it is working queues the next request.\n\n## The code editor\n\n**Code**, in the top bar, opens the website's files in a tree: the section files, the templates, the settings, the snippets and the styles. The list marks files you changed in blue and unsaved ones in amber.\n\n- **Save** (or ⌘S) compiles a section file first. If it does not compile, nothing is saved, and each error is listed with its line; press one to jump to it, or **Solve with AI**.\n- A save is a draft: the live website changes when you publish.\n- **New file** under `sections/` saves a starter section that already compiles, with a colour scheme, a heading, text and spacing, and a preset, so it is in Add Section at once.\n- **History** lists every save of a file, the newest 50; open any of them to bring it back.\n- **Revert** puts a theme file back to how the theme shipped it. Your own files can be renamed, duplicated and deleted; the editor warns first if a page uses the section.\n- Unsaved edits are kept in the browser, and offered back when you return.\n\nOnly `sections/*.storelib` changes what the site draws. The other files in the tree are views of the theme's data, and saving an edit to one changes nothing on the site; see [Themes](/developers/themes#what-the-site-reads-and-what-it-does-not). The language section files are written in is the [Storelib Builder Framework](/developers/sections).\n\n## Website settings\n\n**Website Settings**, in the top bar, is a page of its own, with nine subjects about the website down the left and Connections under its own heading, Code. On a phone they are a list, and each opens with its own Back. On the Websites page, **Settings** beside the live website opens it, and a draft's own is in that draft's **More** menu.\n\nEvery website has its own settings. Anything marked below as part of the draft is saved with the website's other changes, can be undone, and reaches visitors when you publish.\n\n| Page | What is there |\n| --- | --- |\n| General | The website's name, which only you see. Its address, set by your username. Whether it is live. Its favicon and its language (both part of the draft); the language sets the page's `lang` and the share card's locale. |\n| Domains | The Storelib address, which starts as your username and can be given its own name with **Change address** (your username stays as it is, and the old address keeps working), and a custom domain if one is connected, with its status: waiting for DNS, securing, or connected. The `www.` address is the main one and the bare domain redirects to it. HTTPS is set up for both. A domain is added right here. Until it is connected, the three DNS records to set are listed, each with a copy button, and **Check DNS** looks them up. A site has one domain. Adding another asks first, then the old one sends its visitors to the new one for 30 days. Removing a domain is in Settings › Domains. |\n| SEO & Social | A preview of the search result. Site title, meta description, social image, share card size, and whether search engines may list the website (all part of the draft). |\n| Pages | Every page with **Edit**, which opens it in the builder, and **Add page**. **Search** opens the page's own search result: its title, its description, and **Show in search engines**. Off gives the page `noindex` and leaves it out of the sitemap. These are stored in the theme settings as `page_seo`, keyed by page, so they are saved with the draft and go live on publish. The home page's search result is the site's own, in SEO & Social. |\n| Navigation | The header's links, edited in place: add, rename, point anywhere with the link picker, drag to reorder, and one level of sub-links. **Links come from** switches the header to one of the site's menus. The footer's two menu columns. **Menus**, the named lists of links a header, a footer column or any section can show. All of it is the header's, the footer's and the theme's own settings, so it is part of the draft, saved with Undo, and live on publish. |\n| Commerce | How many published products the website can show, and **Manage products**. Checkout is Storelib's, paid by card or Google Pay through Stripe, in US dollars. **Payments settings** is where payouts go. |\n| Policies | The refund, privacy and terms pages, each with **Edit**. The cookie banner: **Show the cookie banner** (off means no banner, and nothing optional is stored for visitors), its message and its two buttons, which start with the site's default words, and **Use my own colours**. All part of the draft, and the same settings as the builder's Cookie & privacy. |\n| Publishing | Live or draft, the published address, when it was last published, **Publish**, and **Take offline**, which asks first and deletes nothing. |\n| Backups & Versions | See [Backups](#backups). **Duplicate** makes a draft copy of the website, if the library has room. |\n| Connections | Under Code, apart from the website's own settings. **Connect Claude Code, Codex or ChatGPT**: directly with a token and one line in a terminal, or through a GitHub repository. Whatever they write is a draft until you publish, and they never change a built-in section. **Edit code**, and the developer docs. See [Building with AI agents](/developers/ai) and [Rules before you ship](/developers/rules). |\n\n**How a published website is found.** Every published website answers for itself, at its Storelib address and on a connected domain:\n\n- `/sitemap.xml` lists every published page and product, when each product last changed, and every picture on them, so pictures are indexed along with pages.\n- `/robots.txt` lets crawlers in and names that sitemap. It keeps previews, the basket and search results out.\n- Every page names its **canonical** address, which is the connected domain when there is one, so the two addresses count as one page.\n- The home page carries structured data naming the website and its owner. A product page carries the product with its price, which is what lets a search result show the price and picture.\n- With search engines switched off, pages say `noindex` and the sitemap is not offered. Crawlers are still let in, so they read the `noindex` and drop the pages.\n\nA creator keeps up to five websites: one live and four drafts. Add theme, Duplicate and Import all refuse a sixth, on the server, and the Websites page shows which websites are taking the room with **Remove** beside each draft. See [Themes](/developers/themes#the-website-library).\n",
}

/** Each example section's source, by type. */
export const DEVELOPER_EXAMPLES: Record<string, string> = {
  "faq_exclusive": "<template>\n<section class=\"fq\" data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\" style=\"--fq-pt: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; --fq-pb: {{ settings.padding_bottom }}px;\">\n  <div class=\"fq__in\">\n    {% if settings.heading != \"\" %}<h2 class=\"fq__h\">{{ settings.heading }}</h2>{% endif %}\n    {% for block in blocks %}\n      <details class=\"fq__item\" name=\"{{ section.id }}-faq\" data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\">\n        <summary class=\"fq__q\">{{ block.settings.question }}</summary>\n        <div class=\"fq__a\">{{ block.settings.answer | raw }}</div>\n      </details>\n    {% else %}\n      {% if page.in_editor %}<p class=\"fq__empty\">Add a question from the panel.</p>{% endif %}\n    {% endfor %}\n  </div>\n</section>\n</template>\n\n<style>\n.fq { container-type: inline-size; background: var(--scheme-background); padding: var(--fq-pt) var(--theme-padding-x, 24px) var(--fq-pb); }\n.fq__in { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; }\n.fq__h { margin: 0 0 24px; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-size: var(--section-heading-size, 32px); line-height: var(--section-heading-line-height, 1.15); }\n.fq__item { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--scheme-border); }\n.fq__q { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 16px; padding: 18px 0; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-weight: 600; }\n.fq__q::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }\n.fq__q::after { content: \"+\"; flex: none; transition: transform 200ms ease; }\n.fq__item[open] .fq__q::after { transform: rotate(45deg); }\n.fq__a { padding: 0 0 18px; color: var(--scheme-text); line-height: 1.6; }\n.fq__a > :first-child { margin-top: 0; }\n.fq__empty { color: var(--scheme-text-muted); }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .fq__q::after { transition: none; } }\n</style>\n\n<schema>\n{\n  \"type\": \"faq_exclusive\",\n  \"name\": \"FAQ, one open at a time\",\n  \"category\": \"Content\",\n  \"settings\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"heading\", \"label\": \"Heading\", \"default\": \"Questions\" },\n    { \"type\": \"color_scheme\", \"id\": \"color_scheme\", \"label\": \"Colour scheme\", \"default\": \"white\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_top\", \"label\": \"Top padding\", \"default\": 72, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_bottom\", \"label\": \"Bottom padding\", \"default\": 72, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" }\n  ],\n  \"blocks\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"question\",\n      \"name\": \"Question\",\n      \"limit\": 30,\n      \"settings\": [\n        { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"question\", \"label\": \"Question\", \"default\": \"How long does delivery take?\" },\n        { \"type\": \"richtext\", \"id\": \"answer\", \"label\": \"Answer\", \"default\": \"<p>Most orders arrive within three working days.</p>\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"max_blocks\": 30,\n  \"presets\": [\n    { \"name\": \"FAQ\", \"blocks\": [{ \"type\": \"question\" }, { \"type\": \"question\" }, { \"type\": \"question\" }] }\n  ]\n}\n</schema>\n",
  "logo_strip": "<template>\n<section class=\"lg\" data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\" style=\"--lg-pt: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; --lg-pb: {{ settings.padding_bottom }}px;\">\n  <div class=\"lg__in\">\n    {% if settings.heading != \"\" %}<p class=\"lg__h\">{{ settings.heading }}</p>{% endif %}\n    <ul class=\"lg__row\">\n      {% for block in blocks %}\n        <li class=\"lg__item\" data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\">\n          {% if block.settings.logo != \"\" %}\n            {% if block.settings.link != \"\" %}\n              <a href=\"{{ block.settings.link | url }}\"><img class=\"lg__img\" src=\"{{ block.settings.logo }}\" alt=\"{{ block.settings.name }}\" width=\"160\" height=\"64\" loading=\"lazy\"></a>\n            {% else %}\n              <img class=\"lg__img\" src=\"{{ block.settings.logo }}\" alt=\"{{ block.settings.name }}\" width=\"160\" height=\"64\" loading=\"lazy\">\n            {% endif %}\n          {% else %}\n            <span class=\"lg__name\">{{ block.settings.name }}</span>\n          {% endif %}\n        </li>\n      {% endfor %}\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n</section>\n</template>\n\n<style>\n.lg { container-type: inline-size; background: var(--scheme-background); padding: var(--lg-pt) 0 var(--lg-pb); }\n.lg__in { max-width: var(--theme-page-width, 1280px); margin: 0 auto; }\n.lg__h { margin: 0 0 20px; padding-inline: var(--theme-padding-x, 24px); text-align: center; color: var(--scheme-text-muted); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; }\n.lg__row {\n  display: flex; gap: 40px; margin: 0; padding: 0 var(--theme-padding-x, 24px); list-style: none;\n  overflow-x: auto; overscroll-behavior-x: contain; scroll-snap-type: x mandatory; scrollbar-width: none;\n}\n.lg__row::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }\n.lg__item { flex: none; display: flex; align-items: center; scroll-snap-align: start; }\n.lg__img { display: block; width: auto; height: 32px; object-fit: contain; opacity: 0.8; transition: opacity 200ms ease; }\n.lg__img:hover { opacity: 1; }\n.lg__name { color: var(--scheme-heading); font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }\n@container (min-width: 900px) { .lg__row { justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; overflow: visible; } }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .lg__img { transition: none; } }\n</style>\n\n<schema>\n{\n  \"type\": \"logo_strip\",\n  \"name\": \"Logo strip\",\n  \"category\": \"Social proof\",\n  \"settings\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"heading\", \"label\": \"Heading\", \"default\": \"As seen in\" },\n    { \"type\": \"color_scheme\", \"id\": \"color_scheme\", \"label\": \"Colour scheme\", \"default\": \"white\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_top\", \"label\": \"Top padding\", \"default\": 48, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 160, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_bottom\", \"label\": \"Bottom padding\", \"default\": 48, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 160, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" }\n  ],\n  \"blocks\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"logo\",\n      \"name\": \"Logo\",\n      \"limit\": 16,\n      \"settings\": [\n        { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"name\", \"label\": \"Name\", \"default\": \"Publication\", \"info\": \"Shown when there is no image, and read aloud by screen readers.\" },\n        { \"type\": \"image_picker\", \"id\": \"logo\", \"label\": \"Logo\", \"default\": \"\" },\n        { \"type\": \"url\", \"id\": \"link\", \"label\": \"Link\", \"default\": \"\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"max_blocks\": 16,\n  \"presets\": [\n    { \"name\": \"Logo strip\", \"blocks\": [{ \"type\": \"logo\" }, { \"type\": \"logo\" }, { \"type\": \"logo\" }, { \"type\": \"logo\" }] }\n  ]\n}\n</schema>\n",
  "page_links": "<template>\n<section class=\"pl\" data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\" style=\"--pl-pt: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; --pl-pb: {{ settings.padding_bottom }}px;\">\n  <div class=\"pl__in\">\n    {% if settings.heading != \"\" %}<h2 class=\"pl__h\">{{ settings.heading }}</h2>{% endif %}\n    <nav class=\"pl__links\" aria-label=\"{{ settings.heading | default: 'Links' }}\">\n      {% for block in blocks %}\n        <a class=\"pl__link\" href=\"{{ block.settings.url | default: '#' | url }}\" data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\">{{ block.settings.label }}</a>\n      {% else %}\n        {% if page.in_editor %}<span class=\"pl__empty\">Add a link from the panel.</span>{% endif %}\n      {% endfor %}\n    </nav>\n    <button class=\"pl__open\" type=\"button\" popovertarget=\"{{ section.id }}-links\">{{ settings.phone_label }}</button>\n    <div class=\"pl__sheet\" id=\"{{ section.id }}-links\" popover>\n      <div class=\"pl__sheet-top\">\n        <span class=\"pl__sheet-title\">{{ settings.heading | default: settings.phone_label }}</span>\n        <button class=\"pl__close\" type=\"button\" popovertarget=\"{{ section.id }}-links\" popovertargetaction=\"hide\" aria-label=\"Close\">&times;</button>\n      </div>\n      <nav class=\"pl__sheet-links\" aria-label=\"{{ settings.phone_label }}\">\n        {% for block in blocks %}\n          <a class=\"pl__sheet-link\" href=\"{{ block.settings.url | default: '#' | url }}\">{{ block.settings.label }}</a>\n        {% endfor %}\n      </nav>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</section>\n</template>\n\n<style>\n.pl { container-type: inline-size; background: var(--scheme-background); color: var(--scheme-text); }\n.pl__in { max-width: var(--theme-page-width, 1200px); margin: 0 auto; padding: var(--pl-pt) var(--theme-padding-x, 20px) var(--pl-pb); }\n.pl__h { margin: 0 0 12px; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-size: var(--section-heading-size, 20px); font-weight: var(--section-heading-weight, 700); }\n.pl__links { display: none; }\n.pl__link { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; padding: 0 16px; border: 1px solid var(--scheme-border); border-radius: var(--theme-button-radius, 999px); color: var(--scheme-heading); text-decoration: none; font-size: 15px; transition: background-color 150ms ease; }\n.pl__link:hover { background: var(--scheme-background-secondary); }\n.pl__open { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; width: 100%; min-height: 44px; border: 1px solid var(--scheme-border); border-radius: var(--theme-button-radius, 10px); background: var(--scheme-background); color: var(--scheme-heading); font-size: 16px; cursor: pointer; }\n.pl__empty { color: var(--scheme-text-muted); font-size: 14px; }\n\n/* On a phone the links open as a sheet from the bottom, with no script. */\n.pl__sheet { inset: auto 0 0 0; width: 100%; max-height: 80vh; margin: 0; padding: 12px 20px calc(24px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px)); border: 0; border-radius: 16px 16px 0 0; background: var(--scheme-background); color: var(--scheme-text); box-shadow: 0 -12px 40px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18); overflow-y: auto; overscroll-behavior: contain; }\n.pl__sheet:popover-open { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 4px; animation: pl-in 260ms cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1); }\n.pl__sheet::backdrop { background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35); }\n.pl__sheet-top { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; min-height: 44px; }\n.pl__sheet-title { color: var(--scheme-heading); font-weight: 700; }\n.pl__close { width: 44px; height: 44px; border: 0; background: transparent; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-size: 26px; cursor: pointer; }\n.pl__sheet-links { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }\n.pl__sheet-link { display: flex; align-items: center; min-height: 48px; color: var(--scheme-heading); text-decoration: none; font-size: 17px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--scheme-border); }\n@keyframes pl-in { from { transform: translateY(24px); opacity: 0; } }\n\n@container (min-width: 640px) {\n  .pl__links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; }\n  .pl__open, .pl__sheet { display: none; }\n}\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .pl__sheet:popover-open { animation: none; }\n}\n</style>\n\n<schema>\n{\n  \"type\": \"page_links\",\n  \"name\": \"Page links\",\n  \"category\": \"Content\",\n  \"settings\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"heading\", \"label\": \"Heading\", \"default\": \"Browse by topic\" },\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"phone_label\", \"label\": \"Button on phones\", \"default\": \"Choose a topic\" },\n    { \"type\": \"color_scheme\", \"id\": \"color_scheme\", \"label\": \"Colour scheme\", \"default\": \"white\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_top\", \"label\": \"Space above\", \"default\": 24, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 160, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_bottom\", \"label\": \"Space below\", \"default\": 24, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 160, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" }\n  ],\n  \"blocks\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"link\",\n      \"name\": \"Link\",\n      \"limit\": 12,\n      \"settings\": [\n        { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"label\", \"label\": \"Label\", \"default\": \"Guides\" },\n        { \"type\": \"url\", \"id\": \"url\", \"label\": \"Link\", \"default\": \"/products\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"max_blocks\": 12,\n  \"presets\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Page links\",\n      \"blocks\": [\n        { \"type\": \"link\", \"settings\": { \"label\": \"Guides\", \"url\": \"/products\" } },\n        { \"type\": \"link\", \"settings\": { \"label\": \"Templates\", \"url\": \"/products\" } },\n        { \"type\": \"link\", \"settings\": { \"label\": \"Courses\", \"url\": \"/products\" } }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n</schema>\n",
  "product_cards": "<template>\n<section class=\"pc\" data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\" style=\"--pc-pt: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; --pc-pb: {{ settings.padding_bottom }}px; --pc-cols: {{ settings.columns }};\">\n  <div class=\"pc__in\">\n    {% if settings.heading != \"\" %}<h2 class=\"pc__h\">{{ settings.heading }}</h2>{% endif %}\n    <div class=\"pc__grid\">\n      {% for block in products %}\n        <a class=\"pc__card\" href=\"{{ block.settings.url | url }}\">\n          <span class=\"pc__media\">\n            {% if block.settings.image != \"\" %}<img class=\"pc__img\" src=\"{{ block.settings.image }}\" alt=\"{{ block.settings.title }}\" width=\"600\" height=\"600\" loading=\"lazy\">{% endif %}\n            {% if block.settings.tag != \"\" %}<span class=\"pc__tag\">{{ block.settings.tag }}</span>{% endif %}\n          </span>\n          <span class=\"pc__title\">{{ block.settings.title }}</span>\n          <span class=\"pc__price\">\n            {{ block.settings.price }}\n            {% if block.settings.compare_price != \"\" %}<s class=\"pc__was\">{{ block.settings.compare_price }}</s>{% endif %}\n          </span>\n        </a>\n      {% else %}\n        {% if page.in_editor %}<p class=\"pc__empty\">Your published products appear here.</p>{% endif %}\n      {% endfor %}\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</section>\n</template>\n\n<style>\n.pc { container-type: inline-size; background: var(--scheme-background); padding: var(--pc-pt) var(--theme-padding-x, 24px) var(--pc-pb); }\n.pc__in { max-width: var(--theme-page-width, 1280px); margin: 0 auto; }\n.pc__h { margin: 0 0 24px; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-size: var(--section-heading-size, 28px); line-height: var(--section-heading-line-height, 1.2); }\n.pc__grid { display: grid; gap: 20px 16px; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }\n.pc__card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; min-width: 0; color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }\n.pc__media { position: relative; display: block; aspect-ratio: 1; overflow: hidden; border-radius: {{ theme.radius.medium }}px; background: var(--scheme-background-secondary); }\n.pc__img { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; transition: transform 300ms ease; }\n.pc__card:hover .pc__img { transform: scale(1.03); }\n.pc__tag { position: absolute; top: 10px; left: 10px; padding: 3px 8px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--scheme-button-primary-text); background: var(--scheme-button-primary); border-radius: 999px; }\n.pc__title { overflow-wrap: anywhere; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-weight: 600; }\n.pc__price { color: var(--scheme-text); font-size: 14px; }\n.pc__was { margin-left: 6px; color: var(--scheme-text-muted); }\n.pc__empty { color: var(--scheme-text-muted); }\n@container (min-width: 900px) { .pc__grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--pc-cols), minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 28px 20px; } }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .pc__img { transition: none; } .pc__card:hover .pc__img { transform: none; } }\n</style>\n\n<schema>\n{\n  \"type\": \"product_cards\",\n  \"name\": \"Product cards\",\n  \"category\": \"Products\",\n  \"settings\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"heading\", \"label\": \"Heading\", \"default\": \"New this week\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"columns\", \"label\": \"Columns on desktop\", \"default\": 4, \"min\": 2, \"max\": 5, \"step\": 1 },\n    { \"type\": \"color_scheme\", \"id\": \"color_scheme\", \"label\": \"Colour scheme\", \"default\": \"white\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_top\", \"label\": \"Top padding\", \"default\": 64, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_bottom\", \"label\": \"Bottom padding\", \"default\": 64, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" }\n  ],\n  \"presets\": [{ \"name\": \"Product cards\" }]\n}\n</schema>\n",
  "product_page": "<template>\n<section class=\"pp\" data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\" style=\"--pp-bg: {{ scheme.bg }}; --pp-text: {{ scheme.text }}; --pp-heading: {{ scheme.heading }}; --pp-para: {{ scheme.paragraph }}; --pp-border: {{ scheme.border }}; --pp-accent: {{ scheme.accent }}; --pp-btn: {{ scheme.primary_btn_bg }}; --pp-btn-text: {{ scheme.primary_btn_text }}; --pp-btn-hover: {{ scheme.primary_btn_hover_bg }}; --pp-btn-hover-text: {{ scheme.primary_btn_hover_text }}; --pp-radius: {{ theme.radius.medium }}px; --pp-pt: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; --pp-pb: {{ settings.padding_bottom }}px;\">\n  {% if page.product %}\n  <div class=\"pp__in\">\n    <div class=\"pp__gallery\">\n      {% if page.product.settings.images.size > 0 %}\n        <div class=\"pp__track\">\n          {% for image in page.product.settings.images %}\n            <img class=\"pp__img\" src=\"{{ image }}\" alt=\"{{ page.product.settings.title }}\" width=\"1200\" height=\"1200\" {% if forloop.first %}fetchpriority=\"high\"{% else %}loading=\"lazy\"{% endif %} decoding=\"async\">\n          {% endfor %}\n        </div>\n      {% endif %}\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"pp__info\">\n      <h1 class=\"pp__title\">{{ page.product.settings.title }}</h1>\n      {% if page.product.settings.variants.size == 0 %}\n        <p class=\"pp__price\">\n          {% if page.product.settings.free %}{{ settings.free_label }}{% else %}{% if page.product.settings.pay_what_you_want %}<span class=\"pp__from\">{{ settings.pwyw_label }}</span> {% endif %}{{ page.product.settings.price }}{% endif %}\n          {% if page.product.settings.compare_price != \"\" %}<s class=\"pp__was\">{{ page.product.settings.compare_price }}</s>{% endif %}\n        </p>\n      {% endif %}\n      {% if page.product.settings.available %}\n        {% if page.product.settings.variants.size > 0 %}\n          <fieldset class=\"pp__choose\">\n            <legend class=\"pp__legend\">{{ settings.options_label }}</legend>\n            {% for variant in page.product.settings.variants %}\n              <input class=\"pp__radio\" type=\"radio\" name=\"{{ section.id }}-option\" id=\"{{ section.id }}-option-{{ forloop.index }}\" {% if forloop.first %}checked{% endif %}>\n              <label class=\"pp__option\" for=\"{{ section.id }}-option-{{ forloop.index }}\">\n                <span class=\"pp__option-name\">{{ variant.name }}</span>\n                <span class=\"pp__option-price\">{% if variant.free %}{{ settings.free_label }}{% else %}{{ variant.price }}{% endif %}{% if variant.compare_price != \"\" %} <s class=\"pp__was\">{{ variant.compare_price }}</s>{% endif %}</span>\n              </label>\n            {% endfor %}\n            <div class=\"pp__buys\">\n              {% for variant in page.product.settings.variants %}\n                <a class=\"pp__btn\" href=\"{{ variant.checkout_url | url }}\">{{ settings.button_label }}</a>\n              {% endfor %}\n            </div>\n          </fieldset>\n        {% else %}\n          <a class=\"pp__btn pp__btn--solo\" href=\"{{ page.product.settings.checkout_url | url }}\">{{ settings.button_label }}</a>\n        {% endif %}\n      {% else %}\n        <p class=\"pp__tag\">{{ page.product.settings.tag }}</p>\n      {% endif %}\n      {% if settings.show_description and page.product.settings.description != \"\" %}\n        <div class=\"pp__desc\">{{ page.product.settings.description }}</div>\n      {% endif %}\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  {% elsif page.in_editor %}\n    <p class=\"pp__empty\">Your product appears here on its product page</p>\n  {% endif %}\n</section>\n</template>\n\n<style>\n.pp { container-type: inline-size; background: var(--pp-bg); color: var(--pp-text); padding: var(--pp-pt) var(--theme-padding-x, 20px) var(--pp-pb); }\n.pp__in { display: grid; gap: 24px; max-width: var(--theme-page-width, 1200px); margin: 0 auto; }\n.pp__gallery { min-width: 0; }\n.pp__track { display: flex; gap: 8px; overflow-x: auto; scroll-snap-type: x mandatory; overscroll-behavior-x: contain; scrollbar-width: none; }\n.pp__track::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }\n/* On a phone the next picture shows at the edge, which is what says the gallery swipes.\n   min-width: 0 keeps it there: a flex item may not shrink below its content, and a\n   loaded 1200px picture would otherwise widen to the whole row and push the page down. */\n.pp__img { display: block; flex: 0 0 88%; width: 100%; min-width: 0; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 1; object-fit: cover; scroll-snap-align: start; border-radius: var(--pp-radius); background: var(--pp-border); }\n.pp__img:only-child { flex-basis: 100%; }\n.pp__info { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px; min-width: 0; }\n.pp__title {\n  margin: 0;\n  overflow-wrap: anywhere;\n  color: var(--pp-heading);\n  font-family: var(--theme-font-heading, {{ theme.typography.heading }}), ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;\n  /* The page's one h1, sized as the section's heading style rather than the\n     display size a hero uses, so the price and the buy button stay in view. */\n  font-size: var(--section-heading-size, 32px);\n  font-weight: var(--section-heading-weight, 700);\n  line-height: var(--section-heading-line-height, 1.15);\n  letter-spacing: var(--section-heading-tracking, -0.01em);\n}\n.pp__price { margin: 0; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: 8px; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--pp-heading); }\n.pp__from { font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; color: var(--pp-para); }\n.pp__was { font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; color: var(--pp-para); }\n.pp__choose { margin: 0; padding: 0; border: 0; min-width: 0; display: grid; gap: 8px; }\n.pp__legend { padding: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--pp-heading); }\n.pp__radio { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; margin: -1px; overflow: hidden; clip-path: inset(50%); }\n.pp__option { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px; min-height: 44px; align-items: center; padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid var(--pp-border); border-radius: var(--pp-radius); cursor: pointer; }\n.pp__option-name { min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; color: var(--pp-heading); }\n.pp__option-price { flex-shrink: 0; color: var(--pp-text); }\n.pp__radio:checked + .pp__option { border-color: var(--pp-heading); box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--pp-heading); }\n.pp__radio:focus-visible + .pp__option { outline: 2px solid var(--pp-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }\n.pp__buys { margin-top: 8px; }\n.pp__btn { display: none; align-items: center; justify-content: center; min-height: 48px; padding: 0 24px; border: 1px solid var(--pp-btn); border-radius: var(--pp-radius); background: var(--pp-btn); color: var(--pp-btn-text); font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; transition: background-color 150ms ease, color 150ms ease, border-color 150ms ease; }\n.pp__btn:hover { background: var(--pp-btn-hover); border-color: var(--pp-btn-hover); color: var(--pp-btn-hover-text); }\n.pp__btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--pp-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }\n.pp__btn--solo { display: flex; }\n.pp__radio:nth-of-type(1):checked ~ .pp__buys > .pp__btn:nth-of-type(1),\n.pp__radio:nth-of-type(2):checked ~ .pp__buys > .pp__btn:nth-of-type(2),\n.pp__radio:nth-of-type(3):checked ~ .pp__buys > .pp__btn:nth-of-type(3),\n.pp__radio:nth-of-type(4):checked ~ .pp__buys > .pp__btn:nth-of-type(4),\n.pp__radio:nth-of-type(5):checked ~ .pp__buys > .pp__btn:nth-of-type(5),\n.pp__radio:nth-of-type(6):checked ~ .pp__buys > .pp__btn:nth-of-type(6),\n.pp__radio:nth-of-type(7):checked ~ .pp__buys > .pp__btn:nth-of-type(7),\n.pp__radio:nth-of-type(8):checked ~ .pp__buys > .pp__btn:nth-of-type(8) { display: flex; }\n.pp__tag { margin: 0; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--pp-para); }\n.pp__desc { white-space: pre-line; overflow-wrap: anywhere; font-size: var(--section-text-size, 16px); line-height: 1.6; color: var(--pp-para); }\n.pp__empty { margin: 0; text-align: center; color: var(--pp-para); }\n@container (min-width: 900px) {\n  .pp__in { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.15fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 48px; align-items: start; }\n  .pp__track { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); overflow: visible; }\n  .pp__img:first-child { grid-column: 1 / -1; }\n  .pp__info { position: sticky; top: 24px; }\n}\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .pp__btn { transition: none; } }\n</style>\n\n<schema>\n{\n  \"type\": \"product_page\",\n  \"name\": \"Product page\",\n  \"category\": \"Products\",\n  \"allowed_templates\": [\"product\"],\n  \"settings\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"button_label\", \"label\": \"Buy button\", \"default\": \"Buy now\" },\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"options_label\", \"label\": \"Options heading\", \"default\": \"Choose an option\" },\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"free_label\", \"label\": \"Price when free\", \"default\": \"Free\" },\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"pwyw_label\", \"label\": \"Before a pay what you want price\", \"default\": \"From\" },\n    { \"type\": \"toggle\", \"id\": \"show_description\", \"label\": \"Show the description\", \"default\": true },\n    { \"type\": \"color_scheme\", \"id\": \"color_scheme\", \"label\": \"Colour scheme\", \"default\": \"white\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_top\", \"label\": \"Top padding\", \"default\": 48, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 160, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_bottom\", \"label\": \"Bottom padding\", \"default\": 64, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 160, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" }\n  ],\n  \"presets\": [{ \"name\": \"Product page\" }]\n}\n</schema>\n",
  "signup_card": "<template>\n<section class=\"su\" data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\" style=\"--su-pt: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; --su-pb: {{ settings.padding_bottom }}px;\">\n  <div class=\"su__card\">\n    {% if settings.heading != \"\" %}<h2 class=\"su__h\">{{ settings.heading }}</h2>{% endif %}\n    {% if settings.text != \"\" %}<p class=\"su__p\">{{ settings.text }}</p>{% endif %}\n    <form class=\"su__form\" data-storelib-form=\"subscribe\">\n      <label class=\"su__label\" for=\"{{ section.id }}-email\">Email address</label>\n      <input class=\"su__input\" id=\"{{ section.id }}-email\" type=\"email\" name=\"email\" required autocomplete=\"email\" placeholder=\"{{ settings.placeholder }}\">\n      <button class=\"su__btn\" type=\"submit\">{{ settings.button_text }}</button>\n      <p class=\"su__done\" data-storelib-success hidden>{{ settings.success_message }}</p>\n    </form>\n  </div>\n</section>\n</template>\n\n<style>\n.su { container-type: inline-size; background: var(--scheme-background); padding: var(--su-pt) var(--theme-padding-x, 24px) var(--su-pb); }\n.su__card { max-width: 560px; margin: 0 auto; text-align: center; }\n.su__h { margin: 0; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-size: var(--section-heading-size, 28px); line-height: var(--section-heading-line-height, 1.2); }\n.su__p { margin: 12px 0 0; color: var(--scheme-text); font-size: var(--section-text-size, 16px); line-height: 1.6; }\n.su__form { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; margin-top: 24px; }\n.su__label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip-path: inset(50%); }\n.su__input { min-width: 0; padding: 13px 16px; font: inherit; font-size: 16px; color: var(--scheme-input-text); background: var(--scheme-input); border: 1px solid var(--scheme-input-border); border-radius: {{ theme.radius.medium }}px; }\n.su__btn { padding: 13px 22px; font: inherit; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; cursor: pointer; color: var(--scheme-button-primary-text); background: var(--scheme-button-primary); border: 1px solid var(--scheme-button-primary-border); border-radius: {{ theme.radius.medium }}px; }\n.su__btn:hover { color: var(--scheme-button-primary-hover-text); background: var(--scheme-button-primary-hover); border-color: var(--scheme-button-primary-hover); }\n.su__form[data-state=\"sending\"] .su__btn { opacity: 0.6; }\n.su__done { margin: 0; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-weight: 600; }\n@container (min-width: 520px) {\n  .su__form { flex-direction: row; }\n  .su__input { flex: 1; }\n}\n</style>\n\n<schema>\n{\n  \"type\": \"signup_card\",\n  \"name\": \"Sign-up card\",\n  \"category\": \"Marketing\",\n  \"settings\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"heading\", \"label\": \"Heading\", \"default\": \"Get the next drop first\" },\n    { \"type\": \"textarea\", \"id\": \"text\", \"label\": \"Text\", \"default\": \"One email when something new is ready. Nothing else.\" },\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"placeholder\", \"label\": \"Placeholder\", \"default\": \"you@example.com\" },\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"button_text\", \"label\": \"Button\", \"default\": \"Notify me\" },\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"success_message\", \"label\": \"After signing up\", \"default\": \"You're on the list.\" },\n    { \"type\": \"color_scheme\", \"id\": \"color_scheme\", \"label\": \"Colour scheme\", \"default\": \"light\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_top\", \"label\": \"Top padding\", \"default\": 64, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_bottom\", \"label\": \"Bottom padding\", \"default\": 64, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" }\n  ],\n  \"presets\": [{ \"name\": \"Sign-up card\" }]\n}\n</schema>\n",
  "tabs_panel": "<template>\n<section class=\"tb\" data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\" style=\"--tb-pt: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; --tb-pb: {{ settings.padding_bottom }}px;\">\n  <div class=\"tb__in\">\n    {% if settings.heading != \"\" %}<h2 class=\"tb__h\">{{ settings.heading }}</h2>{% endif %}\n    <div class=\"tb__tabs\">\n      {% for block in blocks %}\n        <input class=\"tb__radio\" type=\"radio\" name=\"{{ section.id }}-tab\" id=\"{{ section.id }}-{{ block.id }}\"{% if forloop.first %} checked{% endif %}>\n        <label class=\"tb__label\" for=\"{{ section.id }}-{{ block.id }}\" data-block-id=\"{{ block.id }}\">{{ block.settings.title }}</label>\n        <div class=\"tb__panel\">{{ block.settings.content | raw }}</div>\n      {% else %}\n        {% if page.in_editor %}<p class=\"tb__empty\">Add a tab from the panel.</p>{% endif %}\n      {% endfor %}\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</section>\n</template>\n\n<style>\n.tb { container-type: inline-size; background: var(--scheme-background); padding: var(--tb-pt) var(--theme-padding-x, 24px) var(--tb-pb); }\n.tb__in { max-width: 880px; margin: 0 auto; }\n.tb__h { margin: 0 0 20px; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-size: var(--section-heading-size, 28px); line-height: var(--section-heading-line-height, 1.2); }\n.tb__tabs { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--scheme-border); }\n.tb__radio { position: absolute; opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }\n.tb__label { order: 0; padding: 10px 16px; margin-bottom: -1px; cursor: pointer; color: var(--scheme-text); border-bottom: 2px solid transparent; font-weight: 600; }\n.tb__panel { order: 1; display: none; width: 100%; padding: 20px 0; color: var(--scheme-text); line-height: 1.6; }\n.tb__panel > :first-child { margin-top: 0; }\n.tb__radio:checked + .tb__label { color: var(--scheme-heading); border-color: var(--scheme-accent); }\n.tb__radio:checked + .tb__label + .tb__panel { display: block; }\n.tb__radio:focus-visible + .tb__label { outline: 2px solid var(--scheme-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }\n.tb__empty { color: var(--scheme-text-muted); }\n</style>\n\n<schema>\n{\n  \"type\": \"tabs_panel\",\n  \"name\": \"Tabs\",\n  \"category\": \"Content\",\n  \"settings\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"heading\", \"label\": \"Heading\", \"default\": \"Details\" },\n    { \"type\": \"color_scheme\", \"id\": \"color_scheme\", \"label\": \"Colour scheme\", \"default\": \"white\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_top\", \"label\": \"Top padding\", \"default\": 64, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_bottom\", \"label\": \"Bottom padding\", \"default\": 64, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" }\n  ],\n  \"blocks\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"tab\",\n      \"name\": \"Tab\",\n      \"limit\": 6,\n      \"settings\": [\n        { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"title\", \"label\": \"Tab title\", \"default\": \"Materials\" },\n        { \"type\": \"richtext\", \"id\": \"content\", \"label\": \"Content\", \"default\": \"<p>Organic cotton, dyed without chromium.</p>\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"max_blocks\": 6,\n  \"presets\": [\n    { \"name\": \"Tabs\", \"blocks\": [{ \"type\": \"tab\" }, { \"type\": \"tab\" }, { \"type\": \"tab\" }] }\n  ]\n}\n</schema>\n",
  "video_feature": "<template>\n<section class=\"vf\" data-scheme=\"{{ settings.color_scheme }}\" style=\"--vf-pt: {{ settings.padding_top }}px; --vf-pb: {{ settings.padding_bottom }}px;\">\n  <div class=\"vf__in\">\n    <div class=\"vf__copy\">\n      {% if settings.heading != \"\" %}<h2 class=\"vf__h\">{{ settings.heading }}</h2>{% endif %}\n      {% if settings.text != \"\" %}<p class=\"vf__p\">{{ settings.text }}</p>{% endif %}\n    </div>\n    {% if settings.video_url != \"\" %}\n      <iframe class=\"vf__player\" src=\"{{ settings.video_url | embed }}\" title=\"{{ settings.video_title }}\" loading=\"lazy\" allow=\"autoplay; encrypted-media; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen></iframe>\n    {% elsif page.in_editor %}\n      <p class=\"vf__empty\">Paste a YouTube, Vimeo, Spotify or SoundCloud link in the panel.</p>\n    {% endif %}\n  </div>\n</section>\n</template>\n\n<style>\n.vf { container-type: inline-size; background: var(--scheme-background); padding: var(--vf-pt) var(--theme-padding-x, 24px) var(--vf-pb); }\n.vf__in { display: grid; gap: 28px; max-width: var(--theme-page-width, 1280px); margin: 0 auto; }\n.vf__h { margin: 0; color: var(--scheme-heading); font-size: var(--section-heading-size, 32px); line-height: var(--section-heading-line-height, 1.15); }\n.vf__p { margin: 14px 0 0; color: var(--scheme-text); font-size: var(--section-text-size, 17px); line-height: 1.6; }\n.vf__player { display: block; width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; border: 0; border-radius: {{ theme.radius.medium }}px; background: var(--scheme-background-secondary); }\n.vf__empty { margin: 0; padding: 48px 20px; text-align: center; color: var(--scheme-text-muted); border: 1px dashed var(--scheme-border); border-radius: {{ theme.radius.medium }}px; }\n@container (min-width: 900px) { .vf__in { grid-template-columns: 2fr 3fr; align-items: center; gap: 48px; } }\n</style>\n\n<schema>\n{\n  \"type\": \"video_feature\",\n  \"name\": \"Video with text\",\n  \"category\": \"Media\",\n  \"capabilities\": [\"embed\"],\n  \"settings\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"heading\", \"label\": \"Heading\", \"default\": \"See it in use\" },\n    { \"type\": \"textarea\", \"id\": \"text\", \"label\": \"Text\", \"default\": \"Two minutes, start to finish.\" },\n    { \"type\": \"url\", \"id\": \"video_url\", \"label\": \"Video link\", \"default\": \"\", \"info\": \"A YouTube, Vimeo, Spotify or SoundCloud link, as you would copy it from the address bar.\" },\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"id\": \"video_title\", \"label\": \"Video title\", \"default\": \"Product demo\", \"info\": \"Read aloud by screen readers.\" },\n    { \"type\": \"color_scheme\", \"id\": \"color_scheme\", \"label\": \"Colour scheme\", \"default\": \"white\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_top\", \"label\": \"Top padding\", \"default\": 72, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" },\n    { \"type\": \"range\", \"id\": \"padding_bottom\", \"label\": \"Bottom padding\", \"default\": 72, \"min\": 0, \"max\": 200, \"step\": 4, \"unit\": \"px\" }\n  ],\n  \"presets\": [{ \"name\": \"Video with text\" }]\n}\n</schema>\n",
}
